hadiths[469]{hadithnumber,text}:
"13819","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) explained the verse about turning among those who prostrate as describing the Prophet's ﷺ passage through the loins of prophets, from one prophet to another, until he himself became a prophet. > **""From the loins of a prophet to a prophet, until you became a prophet.""**"
"13820","Ali (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Prophet ﷺ affirmed that his lineage came through lawful marriage at every stage from Adam until his father and mother bore him, with no link of fornication. > **""I came through marriage and did not come through fornication, from Adam until my father and mother bore me.""**"
"13821","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that nothing of the fornication of the days of ignorance entered his birth, and that he was born only through marriage resembling the marriage of Islam. > **""Nothing of the fornication of Jahiliyya produced me; nothing produced me except marriage, like the marriage of Islam.""**"
"13822","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah dividing creation into two portions and placing him in the better one, then into houses, tribes, and families, always in the best, until he became the most God-fearing of the children of Adam and the noblest before Allah, without boast, purified with his household. > **""Allah divided creation into two portions and placed me in the better portion; then He made them houses and placed me in the best house, then tribes and families, and placed me in the best house. I am the most God-fearing of the children of Adam and the noblest of them before Allah, and this is no boast.""**"
"13823","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports sitting in the courtyard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a remark likening Muhammad ﷺ among Banu Hashim to sweet basil amid filth reached him. The Prophet ﷺ came with anger visible on his face and recounted the divine selections from heavens, earth, mankind, Arabs, Mudar, Quraysh, and Banu Hashim, culminating in himself, passing from best to best. > **""Allah created the heavens seven and chose the highest; He created mankind and chose the children of Adam, then the Arabs, then Mudar, then Quraysh, then Banu Hashim, then chose me from Banu Hashim. I am from the best to the best. Whoever loves the Arabs loves them through love of me, and whoever hates the Arabs hates them through hatred of me.""**"
"13824","Abdul Muttalib ibn Rabia ibn al-Harith reports that some Ansar complained that a man among his people likened Muhammad ﷺ to a palm tree growing in dung. The Prophet ﷺ asked the people who he was, affirmed his lineage as Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abdul Muttalib, and declared that Allah placed him in the better of two divisions, then the best tribe, then the best house. > **""Allah created His creation, then divided them into two groups and placed me in the better group; then made them tribes and placed me in the best tribe; then made them houses and placed me in the best house. So I am the best of them in house and the best of them in person.""**"
"13825","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ a comparison of himself and his household to a palm tree growing in a dung heap. > **""My likeness and the likeness of my household is like a palm tree growing in a dung heap.""**"
"13826","Ibn al-Zubayr (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports a demeaning comparison uttered by Quraysh concerning the Prophet ﷺ. > **""The likeness of Muhammad is the likeness of a palm tree in a filthy hollow.""**"
"13827","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that when a son of Safiyya, paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, died, she wept and wailed. The Prophet ﷺ consoled her with the reward of a house in Paradise for one who loses a child in Islam and endures patiently. She fell silent, but Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) met her and said her kinship to the Messenger would not avail her before Allah, so she wept again. The Prophet ﷺ, who honored and loved her, called Bilal (radiya Allahu anhu) to summon prayer, ascended the minbar, praised Allah, and then addressed later boasting by Quraysh about Jahiliyya, his own lineage, and the devotion of the Ansar who armed themselves around him until the Quraysh apologized. > **""What is the matter with people who claim my kinship will not benefit? Every tie and lineage is cut off on the Day of Resurrection except my tie and my lineage, for they remain joined in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"13828","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah sending Jibril at creation to divide mankind into Arabs and non-Arabs, with Allah's choice in the Arabs, then dividing Arabs into Yemen, Mudar, and Quraysh, with Allah's choice in Quraysh, from whose best stock he was brought forth. > **""Allah sent Jibril and divided mankind into two portions, the Arabs and the non-Arabs, and Allah's choice was in the Arabs; then He divided the Arabs and Allah's choice was in Quraysh; then He brought me forth from the best of those to whom I belong.""**"
"13829","Aisha (radiya Allahu anha) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Jibril (alayhi al-salam) said he had turned over the easts and wests of the earth and had seen no man more excellent than Muhammad ﷺ and no house more excellent than the house of Banu Hashim. > **""I turned over the easts of the earth and its wests, and I saw no man more excellent than Muhammad, nor any house more excellent than the house of Banu Hashim.""**"
"13830","Khuraym ibn Aws (radiya Allahu anhu) reports being with the Prophet ﷺ when Abbas ibn Abdul Muttalib (radiya Allahu anhu) asked leave to praise him. The Prophet ﷺ invited him with a supplication that Allah never still his mouth, whereupon Abbas recited verses tracing his purity through shades and repositories, through the Flood in the ship of Nuh, passed from loins to wombs through successive generations, through Khindif, until his birth lit the earth and horizon, in whose light they advance on guidance. > **""Even before it you were pure in the shades and in the repository where leaves cover; then you descended to earth, conveyed from loins to wombs whenever one generation passed and another appeared, until eminence sheltered your house of Khindif. When you were born the earth shone and the horizon lit with your light, and in that radiance we traverse the paths of guidance.""**"
"13831","Maymun reports asking Zayd ibn Arqam (radiya Allahu anhu) about the name of the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who answered that she was Amina bint Wahb (radiya Allahu anha). > **""Amina bint Wahb.""**"
"13832","Abu Umama al-Bahili (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recount that when the descendants of Maadd ibn Adnan reached forty men they raided the camp of Musa (alayhi al-salam) and plundered it, so Musa supplicated against them. Allah revealed that Musa should not supplicate against them, for from them would come the unlettered prophet, the warner and bearer of glad tidings, and the oft-shown-mercy nation of Muhammad, content with little provision and accepted by Allah for little deed, admitted to Paradise for saying there is no god but Allah, led by Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abdul Muttalib, humble in bearing, gathered in intellect in his silence, speaking wisdom and practicing forbearance, drawn from the best generation of Quraysh and the elite of Hashim, ever moving from good to good. > **""Musa ibn Imran, do not supplicate against them, for from them is the unlettered prophet, the warner and bearer of glad tidings, and from them is the oft-shown-mercy nation, the nation of Muhammad, who are content with little provision and Allah is content with little deed from them, so Allah admits them to Paradise for saying there is no god but Allah.""**"
"13833","Abu Said al-Khudri (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared himself the Prophet without lie, the son of Abdul Muttalib, the most eloquent of the Arabs, born among Quraysh and raised among Banu Sad ibn Bakr, so whence could incorrect speech come to him. > **""I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abdul Muttalib; I am the most eloquent of the Arabs. I was born among Quraysh and raised among Banu Sad ibn Bakr, so whence could solecism come to me?""**"
"13834","Al-Jafshish al-Kindi (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that people of Kinda came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ claiming he was of them. He replied that his people do not trace lineage through the mother nor disown the father, and affirmed descent from al-Nadr ibn Kinana. > **""We do not follow our mother in lineage nor disown our father; we are the children of al-Nadr ibn Kinana.""**"
"13835","Al-Jafshish (radiya Allahu anhu) reports addressing the Prophet ﷺ with a narration to the same effect as the preceding report on lineage."
"13836","Siyaba ibn Asim al-Sulami (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of Hunayn that he was the son of the Awatik women. > **""I am the son of the Awatik.""**"
"13837","Ruqayqa bint Abi Sayfi ibn Hashim, contemporary of Abdul Muttalib, reports successive drought years that dried udders and weakened bones. While distressed in sleep she heard a hoarse-voiced caller announcing the days of the awaited commissioned prophet, bidding Quraysh bring forth a distinguished, full-bodied, fair-skinned man of forbearance and right guidance with his children, with one man from each clan, washed, perfumed, having touched the Corner, ascending Abu Qubays where he would supplicate and they would say amen, so rain would fall as they wished. She awoke alarmed, related the vision, and it spread through the valleys of Makka until every Bat-ha dweller named Shaybat al-Hamd. The men of Quraysh gathered, performed the washings and rites, and climbed with Abdul Muttalib, accompanied by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a young boy. Abdul Muttalib raised his hand invoking Allah as mender of breach and remover of distress, all-knowing without being taught and besought without stint, complaining of years that destroyed livestock and crops, asking drenching, wholesome rain. Before they moved, the sky burst and the valley filled, and elders of Quraysh congratulated Abdul Muttalib as father of al-Batha, for which Ruqayqa composed verses praising Allah for watering the town through Shaybat al-Hamd, the auspicious harbinger of Mudar, through whom clouds are asked for rain, without peer among mankind. > **""O Allah, mender of breach and remover of distress, knowing without being taught and besought without stint, here are Your bondsmen and bondswomen in the courtyards of Your sanctuary complaining their years to You; You have taken away offspring and hoof. O Allah, send down upon us drenching, wholesome rain.""**"
"13838","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Prophet ﷺ was born on a Monday, with the remainder of the narration already treated under knowledge and chronology. > **""The Prophet was born on a Monday.""**"
"13839","Uthman ibn Abi al-As (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from his mother that she was present with Amina when she bore the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When labor seized her, she saw stars descending low until fearing they would drop upon her, and when the birth occurred a light came forth that lit the house and dwelling so that whatever she looked at was light. > **""They will surely fall upon me.""**"
"13840","Halima bint al-Harith al-Sadiyya (radiya Allahu anha), foster mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports going with women of Banu Sad ibn Bakr to Makka seeking infants, on a weak white donkey amid a lean year, with her husband al-Harith ibn Abdul Uzza, an old she-camel yielding hardly a drop, and her own hungry infant keeping them wakeful for lack of milk. Every woman was offered the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but refused him because he was an orphan and they hoped for generosity from the child's father, until all her companions had taken a child but her. Unwilling to return empty, she resolved to take him, and her husband hoped Allah would place good in him. No sooner was he in her lap than her breast flowed, so he drank his fill and her son drank his fill, her husband found the old camel full at night and they all drank and slept sated while both boys slept. Her husband deemed the soul blessed. On departure her donkey outstripped the caravan, and at their dwellings, the most barren land of Allah, her flocks returned full-uddered with milk while others returned hungry, though shepherds pastured the same ravine. The child ﷺ grew in a day like a boy in a month and in a month like a boy in a year, until at six he was a sturdy lad. Fearing the plague of Makka yet treasuring his blessing, they returned him briefly to his mother Amina (radiya Allahu anha), then brought him back for two months. While playing behind the houses with his brother herding their lambs, his brother ran crying that two men had laid the Qurashi boy down and split his belly. They rushed to find him standing, pale, and he told of two men in white laying him down and splitting his belly, he knew not what they did. His foster father feared affliction and urged returning him to his family before some dreaded matter appeared. When Amina asked why, after their eagerness, they first pleaded fulfillment of trust and fear of events, until she pressed them and they told her. She reassured them that her son had a great affair, recounting her light pregnancy, the meteor-like light at delivery that lit the necks of camels at Busra, and his birth resting on his hands with head raised to heaven, and bade them return to their affair. > **""Two men in white garments came to me, laid me down, then split my belly, and by Allah I do not know what they did.""**"
"13841","Utba ibn Abd (radiya Allahu anhu) reports a man asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the beginning of his affair. He told of being with his foster kin of Banu Sad ibn Bakr with a foster brother among their lambs without provisions, sending the brother for food, when two white birds like eagles came, confirmed his identity, seized him, laid him on his back, split his belly, removed his heart, split it, extracted two black clots, washed his interior with ice water and his heart with cool water, filled it with tranquility, stitched it, and sealed it with the seal of prophethood. In another wording they weighed him against a thousand of his nation and he feared they would fall upon him, yet he outweighed them, indeed his whole nation. They left him terrified, he told his foster mother who feared harm and sought refuge for him with Allah, then carried him back to his mother, declaring her trust fulfilled. His mother was unalarmed, saying she had seen a light come forth from her that lit the palaces of Sham. > **""Stitch him and seal him with the seal of prophethood; if his nation were weighed against him, he would outweigh them.""**"
"13842","Abu Umama (radiya Allahu anhu) reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the beginning of his affair. He answered that he was the supplication of Ibrahim, the glad tidings of Isa, and the vision of his mother, who saw a light come forth from her that lit the palaces of Sham. > **""I am the supplication of Ibrahim, the glad tidings of Isa, and my mother saw a light come forth from her that lit the palaces of Sham.""**"
"13843","Ubayy ibn Kab (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu), bold in asking what others did not ask, inquired about the first thing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw of prophethood. The Prophet ﷺ sat upright and recounted being in the desert at ten years and some months when voices above him asked whether he was the one, then two figures of countenance, presence, and dress never seen in creation approached, each took his upper arm without felt touch, laid him down gently, split his breast without blood or pain, removed something like a clot identified as rancor and envy, cast it away, inserted something like silver identified as mercy and compassion, shook his right big toe bidding him proceed in safety, whereupon he returned tender toward the young and merciful toward the old. > **""Lay him down; split his breast; remove rancor and envy; insert mercy and compassion. Proceed and be safe.""**"
"13844","Anas ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Jibril (alayhi al-salam) brought out the filling of the belly in a basin of gold, washed it, then packed it with wisdom, light, wisdom, and knowledge. > **""Jibril took out the contents in a basin of gold, washed them, then filled them with wisdom, light, and knowledge.""**"
"13845","Al-Irbad ibn Sariya (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that he was with Allah the Seal of the Prophets while Adam was still cast in his clay, and announced he would tell of its first signs: the supplication of Ibrahim, the glad tidings of Isa, and the vision of his mother, as the mothers of the prophets likewise see. > **""I am with Allah the Seal of the Prophets while Adam was still cast in his clay. I will tell you of its beginning: I am the supplication of Ibrahim, the glad tidings of Isa, and the vision of my mother which she saw, as the mothers of the prophets see.""**"
"13846","In another wording, the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beheld at his delivery a radiance extending to the palaces of Sham. > **""A light by which the palaces of Sham were lit.""**"
"13847","In another wording the report adds the glad tidings given by Isa to his people, explaining its interpretation as the supplication of Ibrahim for a messenger from among them, the announcement of a messenger named Ahmad coming after him, and the vision of his mother in sleep of a light that lit the palaces of Sham. > **""I will tell you its interpretation: the supplication of Ibrahim, who prayed for a messenger from among them; the glad tidings of Isa ibn Maryam, who announced a messenger to come after him named Ahmad; and the vision of my mother, who saw in sleep that she placed a light by which the palaces of Sham were lit.""**"
"13848","Maysara al-Fajr (radiya Allahu anhu) reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he was inscribed as a prophet, and he answered that it was when Adam was between soul and body. > **""While Adam was between soul and body.""**"
"13849","Abdullah ibn Shaqiq reports from a man who asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he was made a prophet, and he answered that it was when Adam was between soul and body. > **""While Adam was between soul and body.""**"
"13850","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports being asked when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was inscribed as a prophet. The reply placed it at a time when Adam was still between soul and body. > **""While Adam was between soul and body.""**"
"13851","Abu Maryam (radiya Allahu anhu) reports a Bedouin approaching the Prophet ﷺ among many people, asking for something beneficial to learn that would not harm him. The people made room, the Prophet ﷺ bade them leave him since a man asks to learn, and the Bedouin asked what his prophethood was. He answered that Allah took his covenant as He took the covenant of the prophets, then recited the verse of the covenant taken from the prophets including himself, Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa, and Isa ibn Maryam, described himself as the glad tidings of the Messiah, and mentioned the dream of the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of a lamp emerging from between her legs that lit the palaces of Sham. The Bedouin drew near, hard of hearing, and the Prophet ﷺ added that there was more beyond that. > **""Allah took my covenant as He took the covenant of the prophets. I am the glad tidings of the Messiah Isa ibn Maryam, and the mother of the Messenger of Allah saw in sleep a lamp emerge from between her legs that lit the palaces of Sham.""**"
"13852","Anas ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among his honor before his Lord that he was born circumcised and that no one saw his private parts. > **""Among my honor before my Lord is that I was born circumcised, and no one saw my nakedness.""**"
"13853","Abu Bakra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that Jibril (alayhi al-salam) circumcised the Prophet ﷺ at the time he purified his heart."
"13854","Kundir ibn Sad reports from his father that during Hajj in Jahiliyya he saw a man circumambulating the House chanting verses beseeching his Lord to return his rider Muhammad to him and bestow a favor. Asked who he meant, he was told it was Abdul Muttalib ibn Hashim, whose camels had strayed, so he sent his grandson seeking them and the boy was delayed, though never before sent on a need but he fulfilled it. Soon the Prophet ﷺ arrived bringing the camels, and Abdul Muttalib addressed him with grief like a woman's, bidding him never leave him. > **""My Lord, return my rider Muhammad to me; return him to me and bestow a favor upon me.""**"
"13855","Ammar (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that Abu Talib would prepare food for the people of Makka, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered he would not be detained until taking something to place beneath himself, upon which Abu Talib observed a sense of honor in his nephew. > **""Indeed my nephew perceives dignity.""**"
"13856","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that every one of them had a companion from the devils appointed over him. Asked whether this included himself, he affirmed it, adding that Allah aided him against his companion so he submitted. > **""There is none among you but that a companion from the devils has been appointed over him. Yes, even me, but Allah aided me against him so he submitted.""**"
"13857","Al-Mughira ibn Shuba (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that everyone had a companion from the jinn placed with him. Asked whether this included himself, he affirmed it, adding that Allah aided him so his companion submitted and commanded him only with good. > **""There is no one but that a companion from the jinn has been placed with him. Not even me, except that Allah aided me against him so he submitted, and he commands me with nothing but good.""**"
"13858","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared favor over the prophets by two traits: his devil was a disbeliever, but Allah aided him against him until he submitted. The narrator adds that he forgot the other trait. > **""I was favored over the prophets by two traits: my devil was a disbeliever, but Allah aided me against him until he submitted.""**"
"13859","Usama ibn Sharik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that every one of them had a devil with him. Asked whether this included himself, he affirmed it, adding that Allah aided him so his companion submitted. > **""There is none among you but that a devil is with him. Yes, even me, except that Allah aided me against him so he submitted.""**"
"13860","Sharik ibn Tariq (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that every one of them had a devil. Asked whether this included himself, he affirmed it, adding that Allah aided him so his companion submitted. > **""There is none among you but that he has a devil. Not even me, except that Allah aided me against him so he submitted.""**"
"13861","Urwa ibn al-Zubayr reports from a neighbor of Khadija bint Khuwaylid (radiya Allahu anha) hearing the Prophet ﷺ say to Khadija that by Allah he would never worship al-Lat and by Allah he would never worship al-Uzza. Khadija replied bidding him leave al-Uzza, those being the idols they worshipped and then reclined before. > **""O Khadija, by Allah I will never worship al-Lat, and by Allah I will never worship al-Uzza.""**"
"13862","Anas (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that he was not of frivolity nor frivolity of him. Abu Muhammad Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Qays explains the meaning as having nothing to do with falsehood and falsehood having nothing to do with him. > **""I am not of dad, nor is dad of me.""**"
"13863","Muawiya (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ the same declaration of having nothing to do with frivolity and its having nothing to do with him. > **""I am not of dad, nor is dad of me.""**"
"13864","Ali ibn Abi Talib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that he never inclined toward the deeds of Jahiliyya except twice, each time Allah intervening between him and what he intended, after which he never inclined again until Allah honored him with His message. > **""I never inclined toward anything the people of Jahiliyya used to do except twice, and each time Allah intervened between me and what I intended; then I never inclined toward anything again until Allah honored me with His message.""**"
"13865","Ammar ibn Yasir (radiya Allahu anhu) reports people asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he ever committed anything forbidden in Jahiliyya. He answered no, though he twice stood at its appointing, once overcome by his eyes in sleep and once barred by people of his nation. In the Awsat wording the question concerned forbidden dealings with women. > **""No. I twice stood at its appointing: once my eyes overcame me, and once the people of my nation intervened between me and it.""**"
"13866","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to attend the gatherings of the idolaters with them, until he heard two angels behind him conferring about standing behind him while questioning his recent contact with idols. After that he never returned to attending their gatherings with them. > **""Come with us that we may stand behind the Messenger of Allah. How can we stand behind him when his recent covenant is touching idols?""**"
"13867","Zayd ibn Haritha (radiya Allahu anhu) reports circumambulating with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day when he touched one of the idols, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade him not touch them, after which the narrator related the full narration. > **""Do not touch them.""**"
"13868","Jada (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, upon seeing a corpulent man, gesturing with his hand toward the man's belly and remarking that had it been elsewhere it would have been better for him. He adds that a man was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accused of intending to kill him, and the Prophet ﷺ reassured him repeatedly not to fear, for even had he brandished his weapon Allah would not have empowered him over him. > **""Had this been elsewhere than this, it would have been better for you. Fear not, fear not; even had you wielded it, Allah would not have empowered you over me.""**"
"13869","Salama ibn al-Akwa (radiya Allahu anhu) reports being with the Prophet ﷺ in a red tent when a man arrived on an affectionate horse followed by a filly, asking who he was, when the Hour would be, when rain would fall, and what was in his mare's belly, each answered as unseen known only to Allah. The man then asked for his sword, took it, drew and brandished it, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him he could never accomplish what he intended, afterward explaining that the comer had planned to approach, question, then seize the sword and kill him, before sheathing the blade. > **""Unseen, and none knows the unseen but Allah. Here it is. You will never be able to do what you intended.""**"
"13870","Qays ibn Habtar reports from the daughter of al-Hakam, from her grandfather, that she reproached Banu Umayya for helplessness and ill judgment toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He answered describing their appointment against him in their mosque, hearing sounds like all the horses of Tihama breaking upon them until sense failed and the Prophet ﷺ finished prayer and returned home, then a second night's advance when the two hills met and barred them from him, all to no avail. > **""Do not blame us, O my daughter. I tell you only what I saw with these two eyes.""**"
"13871","Abbas ibn Abdul Muttalib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports being in the mosque when Abu Jahl vowed that if he saw Muhammad ﷺ prostrating he would tread upon his neck. Abbas warned the Prophet ﷺ, who went out angry to the mosque, hastened at the gate, and entered reciting the opening of the chapter of the Clot. When he reached the passage on man's transgression, a man alerted Abu Jahl that Muhammad was present, but Abu Jahl protested that the horizon of heaven had been closed before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed the chapter and prostrated. > **""Allah has made it binding upon me that if I see Muhammad prostrating I will tread upon his neck. Do you not see what I see? By Allah, the horizon of the heaven has been closed before me.""**"
"13872","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the chiefs of Quraysh gathering in al-Hijr, swearing by al-Lat, al-Uzza, Manat, Isaf, and Naila to rise as one man against Muhammad ﷺ when seen and not leave him until killing him, each knowing his share of his blood. His daughter Fatima (radiya Allahu anha) came weeping to warn him. He asked for ablution water, performed wudu, entered the mosque, and when they saw him their gazes fell, chins sank to chests, and they sat hamstrung, none raising eyes or rising. He stood over their heads, took a handful of dust, pronounced disgrace upon the faces, pelted them, and every pebble-struck man among them was later killed as a disbeliever at Badr. > **""O my daughter, bring me water for wudu. Disgraced be the faces.""**"
"13873","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports Abu Jahl vowing to tread upon the neck of Muhammad ﷺ if he saw him praying at the Kaba. The Prophet ﷺ answered that had he done so the angels would have seized him openly, that had the Jews wished for death they would have died and seen their seats in the Fire, and had those who came to invoke curses against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proceeded, they would have returned finding neither family nor property. > **""Had he done so, the angels would have seized him openly.""**"
"13874","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports Abu Jahl passing and asking whether he had not forbidden him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ rebuked him. Abu Jahl boasted of the multitude of his assembly, so Jibril (alayhi al-salam) bade him summon his company. Ibn Abbas swore that had he summoned them, the stern angels would have seized him with punishment. > **""Let him call his assembly.""**"
"13875","Talha ibn Ubaydullah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports a group of idolaters around the Kaba including Abu Jahl when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approached, stood over them, and pronounced disgrace upon the faces. They fell mute, none uttering a word, even Abu Jahl excusing himself and pleading restraint, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered that he would not restrain from them, indeed Allah would slay them. > **""Disgraced be the faces. I will not restrain from you. Allah will slay you.""**"
"13876","Jabir ibn Samura (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said Satan confronted him casting the evil of fire upon him, and but for the supplication of his brother Sulayman (alayhi al-salam) he would have seized him. > **""Satan confronted me casting the evil of fire upon me, and but for the supplication of my brother Sulayman I would have seized him.""**"
"13877","Jabir (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that upon entering the house he found a devil behind the door and throttled him until feeling the cold of his tongue upon his hands. But for the supplication of the righteous servant, the devil would have remained tied in the morning for people to see. > **""I entered the house and found a devil behind the door, so I throttled him until I felt the cold of his tongue upon my hands; but for the supplication of the righteous servant he would have remained tied for people to see.""**"
"13878","Anas ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was prostrating in Makka, Iblis came to tread upon his neck, so Jibril (alayhi al-salam) struck him a blow with his wing and his feet did not settle upon the earth until he reached Jordan."
"13879","Mujahid reports from his freedman, among those who built the Kaba in Jahiliyya, recalling their dispute over placing the Black Stone. They agreed to arbitrate through the first man to emerge from the pass, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ came and they exclaimed that the Trustworthy had come to them, after which the narrator related the full narration. > **""Appoint an arbiter among you. The Trustworthy has come to you.""**"
"13880","Ali ibn Abi Talib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports concerning the building of the Kaba that when they saw the Prophet ﷺ enter they exclaimed that the Trustworthy had come. > **""The Trustworthy has come.""**"
"13881","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Prophet ﷺ passing while they gathered arak fruit, bidding them take the black thereof since he used to gather it while herding sheep. Asked whether he had herded, he affirmed that no prophet was commissioned but had herded sheep. > **""Take the black thereof, for I used to gather it while herding sheep. Yes, there was no prophet but that he herded them.""**"
"13882","Abu Said al-Khudri (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Musa (alayhi al-salam) was commissioned while herding livestock. > **""Musa was commissioned while herding livestock.""**"
"13883","Salama ibn Salama ibn Waqsh (radiya Allahu anhu), a veteran of Badr, reports a Jewish neighbor among Banu Abdul Ashhal coming shortly before the commissioning of the Prophet ﷺ, standing at their assembly while Salama, the youngest present wrapped in a cloak in his family courtyard, listened as he spoke of resurrection, reckoning, balance, Paradise, and Fire to idolaters who denied rising after death. Asked for its sign, he pointed toward Makka and Yemen announcing a prophet to be commissioned from those lands, and told them that if the youngest boy lived out his life he would meet him. Soon Allah commissioned His Prophet ﷺ among them, they believed, while the Jew denied from defiance and envy, and when reminded of his words he admitted them yet insisted this was not he. > **""A prophet to be commissioned from these lands. If this boy lives out his life, he will meet him.""**"
"13884","In his narration from Umm Salama (radiya Allahu anha), a Jew among Banu Abdul Ashhal told their assembly that the time of the Qurashi Harami prophet had dawned, looked about, pointed to the youth, and said whoever lived would meet him through that lad. When Allah decreed the Prophet ﷺ come to Madina, the narrator recognized him, the Jew swore it was indeed he, yet when asked why he withheld from Islam he vowed never to abandon Judaism. > **""The Qurashi Harami prophet has dawned. If any meets him, this youth will meet him. By Allah, it is indeed he, but by Allah I will never abandon Judaism.""**"
"13885","Abbas ibn Abdul Muttalib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports Abdul Muttalib recounting a Yemen journey during the two caravan seasons, lodging with a Jew, when a cloister man asked his lineage, examined him apart from nakedness, and testified to kingship in one hand and prophethood in the other, yet their books placed it through Banu Zuhra. Asked whether he had a wife, he had none, so the man bade him marry into Zuhra on return. > **""I bear witness that in one of your hands is kingship and in the other is prophethood. When you return, marry into Banu Zuhra.""** He married Hala bint Wuhayb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhra, who bore Hamza, and wed his son Abdullah to Amina bint Wahb, whom Quraysh said outstripped his father, and she bore the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Hamza (radiya Allahu anhu), older than the Prophet ﷺ, was also his foster brother through Thuwayba, freedwoman of Abu Lahab."
"13886","Ibn Masud (radiya Allahu anhu) reports Allah sending His Prophet ﷺ to bring a man into Paradise: entering the synagogue he found Jews with a Jew reciting the Torah over them, who stopped upon reaching the description of the Prophet ﷺ, while a sick man lay aside. Asked why they stopped, the sick man explained they had reached a prophet's description, then crawled, took the Torah, read until the description of the Prophet ﷺ and his nation, testified that it matched him and his nation, bore witness that none deserved worship but Allah and that he was His messenger, then died. The Prophet ﷺ bade them tend their brother. > **""Why did you stop? Tend your brother.""**"
"13887","Abu Sufyan ibn Harb (radiya Allahu anhu) reports Umayya ibn Abi al-Salt accompanying him at Ghazza or Iliya, drawing him aside past Utba ibn Rabia to ask of his noble descent on both sides, avoidance of wrongs and prohibitions, and aged eminence, then confiding from his books that a prophet would be commissioned from their sanctuary whom he had assumed was himself, until learned men traced him to Banu Abd Manaf, among whom only Utba seemed fit, yet whose lineage and passing forty without revelation ruled him out. Later, after revelation came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Sufyan mockingly told Umayya the awaited prophet had emerged, and Umayya affirmed his truth and urged following him, withholding himself only from shame before the women of Thaqif after claiming the role, warning Abu Sufyan that opposing him would leave him tethered like a kid brought before him for judgment. > **""I found in my books a prophet to be commissioned from our sanctuary. He is indeed true, so follow him.""**"
"13888","Khalifa ibn Abda ibn Jarwal reports asking Muhammad ibn Adi about being named Muhammad in Jahiliyya. He traced it to his father journeying fourth of four from Banu Tamim with Sufyan ibn Mujashi, Usama ibn Malik, and Yazid ibn Rabia toward Ibn Jafna al-Ghassani in Sham, lodging by a pool near a monk's cell, washing, oiling, and dressing before their host. The monk noted their foreign speech, learned they were of Mudar and Khindif, and urged them to hasten to their share in an imminent prophet from among them, the Seal of the Prophets named Muhammad, promising guidance. Upon returning from Ibn Jafna each was blessed with a son and named him Muhammad. Al-Ala adds that Qays ibn Asim asked the Prophet ﷺ whether he knew who among Arabs first knew of him before commissioning, and upon his denial told him it was Banu Tamim with this story. > **""A prophet will shortly be commissioned from among you, so hasten and strive for your share in him and you will be guided, for he is the Seal of the Prophets. His name is Muhammad.""**"
"13889","Jubayr ibn al-Mutim (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hating the harm of Quraysh to the Prophet ﷺ and, fearing they would kill him, fleeing to a monastery where the inmates granted him three days' hospitality, then pressed him to depart whether ailing, visiting kin, or trading. Denying all, he confessed his cousin in the town of Ibrahim claimed prophethood, his people harmed him, and he left lest he witness murder. Brought to their chief, questioned whether he feared killing and would recognize his likeness in a portrait, he agreed from recent acquaintance, and the chief unveiled covered portraits one by one until one matched so closely in height, build, and broad shoulders that he recognized him. The chief swore they would never kill him, that he would slay whoever sought it, that he was indeed a prophet whom Allah would manifest, and granted him stay and requests. After a stay Jubayr returned to Makka as if obedient, found Quraysh demanding the orphan wealth entrusted with his father, refused surrender save at severing head from body but offered delivery, whereupon they bound him by covenant of Allah not to eat of Muhammad's food. Reaching Madina after the news had arrived, the Prophet ﷺ perceived his hunger and called for food, but Jubayr first disclosed the covenant, and the Prophet ﷺ bade him fulfill Allah's covenant by eating and drinking nothing of theirs. > **""By Allah they will not kill him; he will slay whoever seeks his killing. He is indeed a prophet and Allah will manifest him. Fulfill the covenant of Allah and do not eat of our food nor drink of our drink.""**"
"13890","Jubayr ibn Mutim (radiya Allahu anhu) reports trading to Sham in Jahiliyya when a man of the Scripture asked of a prophet among them and whether he would recognize his portrait. Led into a house of portraits he did not find it, until another of them took them home, where upon entry he saw the portrait of the Prophet ﷺ with a man holding his heel. Asked who stood at his heel, he was told every prophet had a prophet after him except this one, who would have no prophet after him, and this was the successor after him, whose description was that of Abu Bakr (radiya Allahu anhu). > **""There was no prophet but that a prophet came after him, except this one, for there is no prophet after him, and this is the successor after him.""**"
"13891","Abu Sakhr al-Uqayli reports from a Bedouin who brought a milch animal to Madina in the Prophet's ﷺ lifetime, finished his sale, resolved to meet the man and hear him, and found him walking between Abu Bakr and Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma). Following behind, they came upon a Jew with the Torah spread, reciting to console himself over a dying son, handsome among youths. The Prophet ﷺ adjured him by Who sent down the Torah whether he found his description and emergence in his book, and the Jew signaled no with his head. His son swore by Who sent the Torah that they did find his description and emergence, bore witness that none deserved worship but Allah and that he was His messenger, then died. The Prophet ﷺ bade them remove the Jew from their brother, then undertook his shrouding, perfuming, and prayer. > **""I adjure you by Who sent down the Torah: do you find me in your book, with my description and my emergence? Remove the Jew from your brother.""**"
"13892","Al-Miswar (radiya Allahu anhu) reports a Jew passing while he stood behind the Prophet of Allah ﷺ as the Prophet ﷺ performed wudu, bidding him expose his back. As he moved toward him, the Prophet ﷺ splashed water in his face. > **""Lift his garment from his back.""**"
"13893","Jabir ibn Samura (radiya Allahu anhu) reports a Jurmuqani coming to the companions of the Prophet ﷺ asking where their master who claimed prophethood was, vowing to test whether he was a prophet or not. The Prophet ﷺ came, the Jurmuqani bade him recite or relate, he recited verses from the Book of Allah, and the Jurmuqani swore it was what Musa had brought. > **""Recite to me. By Allah, this is what Musa brought.""**"
"13894","Said ibn Abi Rashid reports meeting the Tanukhi man at Hims, the aged neighbour who had carried Heraclius's correspondence to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Asked about the two letters, he recalled the Prophet ﷺ arriving at Tabuk and sending Dihya al-Kalbi (radiya Allahu anhu) to Heraclius. Heraclius gathered the priests and patricians of Rome behind closed doors, warned them the man had encamped as they saw, that his message summoned them to follow his religion, pay tribute on their own land, or face war, and that their books foretold his taking what lay beneath Heraclius's feet. They snorted as one man, burst from their hoods, and refused to abandon Christianity or serve a bedouin from the Hijaz. Fearing they would leave and corrupt his companions and kingdom, Heraclius claimed he had only tested their firmness. He then summoned an Arab of Tujib over the Arab Christians and asked for a retentive Arabic-speaking man to bear his reply, and the Tanukhi was chosen. Heraclius charged him to remember three things from the Prophet ﷺ: whether he mentioned the letter sent to him, whether mention of night occurred upon reading the reply, and whether anything on his back aroused suspicion. The envoy reached Tabuk to find the Prophet ﷺ seated with his Companions in ihtiba posture by water. He handed the letter, which the Prophet ﷺ placed in his lap, asked his tribe, received the answer of Tanukh, and invited him to the Hanifiyya, the creed of Ibrahim. The envoy declined to leave his people's religion before returning to them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ smiled and recited the verse that guidance belongs to Allah. He then foretold the tearing of Kisra and his kingdom for tearing his letter, the same for the Najashi, and the continued strength of the envoy's master for preserving it. The envoy noted this first sign on his sword sheath. When the letter was handed to Muawiya to read, it asked where Hell was if Paradise spanned heavens and earth. The Prophet ﷺ answered with the parable of night and day, which the envoy likewise recorded. After the reading the Prophet ﷺ acknowledged the envoy's right, excused the lack of hospitality on a provisionless journey, whereupon a man provided a yellowish suit placed in his lap, identified as Uthman (radiya Allahu anhu), and an Ansari youth undertook his lodging. As he left, the Prophet ﷺ recalled him, loosened his garment from his back, bade him fulfil what he was commanded, and the envoy saw the Seal between the shoulder blades like a cupping mark. > **""O brother of Tanukh, I wrote to Kisra and he tore it, so Allah will tear him and his kingdom; I wrote to the Najashi and he tore it, so Allah will tear him and his kingdom; I wrote to your master on a sheet and he kept it, so men will continue to find strength in him while good remains in life. Glory be to Allah, where is the night when the day comes? Here, carry out what you were commanded.""**"
"13895","Dihya al-Kalbi (radiya Allahu anhu) reports being sent by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a letter to Caesar. At court stood Caesar's nephew, red-complexioned, blue-eyed, lank-haired. The superscription read from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Heraclius, chief of Rome. The nephew snorted that it should not be read that day, since the writer named himself first and wrote chief rather than king of Rome. Caesar insisted it be read. Once the company left, Caesar admitted Dihya privately and summoned the bishop who managed their affairs, informed him, and had the letter read. The bishop declared this the awaited one whom Isa had announced, pledged his own belief and obedience when asked for counsel, while Caesar admitted he knew the truth but would lose his kingdom by acting on it. Caesar then summoned Abu Sufyan, then present, and questioned him about the man risen in his land: his youth, his unsurpassed lineage, his never having lied, the apostasy of none who joined him, the return of those who left him, and alternating victory and defeat in battle. To each answer Caesar replied that it was a sign of prophethood. He then charged Dihya to convey that he knew his master was a prophet but would not abandon his kingdom. As for the bishop, he used to address the people every Sunday, then absented himself for successive Sundays, conversing privately with Dihya, until the people threatened to enter and kill him unless he emerged, saying his state had changed since the Arab came. He entrusted Dihya with a letter testifying there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is His messenger, affirming his faith while warning his people would not follow, then went out and was killed. Dihya returned to the Prophet ﷺ while envoys of the Kisra-appointed governor of Sanaa were present with fifteen men sent to seize him. The Prophet ﷺ kept them fifteen nights, then told them to inform their master that his Lord had slain their lord that night. They asked the night be recorded and described the Prophet ﷺ as the most awe-inspiring king they had seen, walking unafraid among men without guards, with none raising voices before him. News then arrived that Kisra had been slain that very night. > **""Convey to your master that I know he is a prophet, but I will not abandon my kingdom. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; I have believed in him, affirmed him, and followed him. Go to your master and tell him: my Lord slew his lord last night.""**"
"13896","Alqama ibn Waqqas reports that Amr ibn al-As (radiya Allahu anhu) commanded a Muslim force that encamped at Alexandria. A chief of the enemy asked for a man to speak with him, and Amr declared none should go but himself. With the interpreter, two pulpits were set. Asked who they were, Amr answered that they were Arabs of thorn and acacia, neighbours of the House of Allah, the narrowest in land and hardest in life, eating carrion and blood, raiding one another in the worst existence men live. Then arose among them a man neither greatest in honour nor richest in wealth, claiming to be Allah's messenger, commanding unknown things and forbidding what they and their fathers practised. They repelled, denied, and rejected him until others believed, affirmed, followed him, and fought his fighters alongside him. He left to them, they fought him and were overcome, then he turned to the neighbouring Arabs and overcame them. Amr warned that if the Arabs behind him knew the ease of life his hearers enjoyed, all would come to share it. The chief laughed and affirmed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had spoken truly, for their own messengers had brought the like and they held to it until youths arose acting by caprice and abandoning the prophets' command. He counselled that so long as they held their Prophet's command none who fought them would but be beaten and none who opposed them but be overcome, whereas if they did as his people had done, abandoning their Prophet and following caprice, they would be left to themselves, for they were neither more numerous nor stronger. Amr remarked he had never spoken with a man of sounder mind. > **""Your messenger has spoken truly; our messengers brought the like of what your messenger brought. If you hold to your Prophet's command, none who fights you will fail to be beaten by you.""**"
"13897","Kurz ibn Alqama reports the arrival of the Christian delegation of Najran before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, twenty-four of their nobles, among whom three directed their affairs: al-Aqib, their commander, counsellor, and man of judgment named Abd al-Masih, whose view and command they never left; al-Sayyid, their scholar and organizer of their caravan and assembly; and Abu Haritha ibn Alqama of Bakr ibn Wail, their bishop, learned scholar, imam, and head of their study. Abu Haritha had risen so high in knowledge that Christian kings honoured him, accepted his word, built him churches, and lavished distinctions for his devotion. Setting out from Najran, Abu Haritha rode his mule with his brother Kurz beside him questioning him, until the mule stumbled and Kurz cried ruin upon the distant one, meaning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Haritha retorted ruin upon Kurz himself, and when asked why, swore by Allah that this was the awaited prophet. Asked what withheld him knowing this, he answered that their people had honoured and exalted them and would accept nothing but opposition, and would strip away all they saw if he acted. His brother Kurz kept the matter to himself, meaning he later embraced Islam."
"13898","Abdullah ibn Salam (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that when Allah willed the guidance of Zayd ibn Sana, Zayd declared he had recognized every mark of prophethood in the face of Muhammad ﷺ upon looking at him except two yet untested: that his forbearance outran his anger, and that severity of provocation only increased his forbearance. He kept company to test this. One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emerged from the apartments with Ali ibn Abi Talib (radiya Allahu anhu), when a bedouin-like rider complained that his people of Banu Fulan had embraced Islam on promise of ample provision, yet drought and famine had struck, and he feared they would leave Islam from greed as they had entered it from greed, asking relief be sent. The man beside the Prophet ﷺ, thought to be Ali, answered nothing remained. Zayd then proposed selling specified dates from a named orchard for a specified term, and the Prophet ﷺ corrected him to specify dates and term without naming the orchard of Banu Fulan. Zayd agreed, paid eighty mithqal of gold from his purse, and the Prophet ﷺ gave it to the man bidding him deal justly and relieve them. Two or three days before maturity, the Prophet ﷺ came with Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman (radiya Allahu anhum) and others, prayed over a funeral, and approached a wall to sit, when Zayd seized his shirt and cloak with a harsh face, demanded his right, and charged the Banu Abd al-Muttalib with delay, citing experience of their company. Umar's eyes spun like a revolving orb as he glared and threatened the enemy of Allah for such speech and conduct, swearing he would strike off his head but for restraint. The Prophet ﷺ looked on calmly and replied that both needed different counsel: Umar should bid him excellent payment and bid Zayd excellent exaction, then take him, pay his right, and add twenty sa of dates for having frightened him. Umar did so, and when Zayd asked the reason for the addition, Umar cited the Prophet's ﷺ command. Asked whether Umar knew him, Zayd named himself, Umar recognized the learned scholar, and asked what moved him to such conduct. Zayd explained his two untested signs were now verified, testified his contentment with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, and Muhammad as prophet, and dedicated half his abundant wealth as charity to the nation of Muhammad ﷺ, amended to part of them at Umar's remark that he could not encompass them. Both returned, Zayd testified there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is His servant and messenger, believed, pledged allegiance, witnessed many campaigns, and fell facing the enemy at Tabuk. > **""O Umar, he and I needed other than this: bid me excellent payment and bid him excellent exaction. Take him, O Umar, pay him his right, and add twenty sa of dates in place of having frightened him.""**"
"13899","Salman (radiya Allahu anhu) reports his origin among the notables of Persia, then his wanderings borne up and down by lands until Bedouins enslaved and sold him and a woman bought him. Hearing mention of the Prophet ﷺ while life was dear, he asked his mistress for a day off and was granted it. He gathered firewood, sold it, prepared food, and placed it before the Prophet ﷺ. Asked what it was, he said charity, so the Prophet ﷺ bade his Companions eat but did not eat himself, and Salman counted this among the signs. After Allah's appointed stay, he again took leave, gathered and sold firewood for more, prepared food, and set it before the Prophet ﷺ amid his Companions. Asked what it was, he said a gift, so the Prophet ﷺ set his hand to it and bade his Companions take in Allah's name. Salman stood behind him, the cloak was lowered, and the Seal of Prophethood appeared. He testified the speaker was Allah's messenger, and when asked the reason, told of the man who had informed him. Asked whether that man would enter Paradise for telling him he was a prophet, the Prophet ﷺ twice answered that none enters Paradise but a Muslim soul. > **""Eat. Take in the name of Allah. What is this? And what is that? None shall enter Paradise but a Muslim soul.""**"
"13900","Salman (radiya Allahu anhu) reports in another narration going forth seeking religion and falling among the monks, remnants of the People of the Book, of whom Allah said they know him as they know their sons. They told him the time of a prophet had dawned, emerging from Arab land with distinguishing marks, among them a round mole between his shoulders, the Seal of Prophethood. He reached Arab land, the Prophet ﷺ emerged, he saw all they had described including the Seal, and testified there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, after which the narrator related the full narration."
"13901","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud (radiya Allahu anhu), reports a Jew passing the Prophet ﷺ while he addressed his Companions. Quraysh told the Jew the man claimed prophethood, so he vowed to ask what none but a prophet knows. He sat and asked from what man is created. The Prophet ﷺ answered that man is created from both, the fluid of the man and the fluid of the woman: the man's fluid thick, from it bone and sinew; the woman's fluid thin, from it flesh and blood. The Jew rose declaring those before him had said the same. > **""O Jew, he is created from both, from the fluid of the man and the fluid of the woman. The fluid of the man is thick, from it are bone and sinew, and the fluid of the woman is thin, from it are flesh and blood.""**"
"13902","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Jews coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking about five things, promising recognition and obedience if he informed them. He took upon them the covenant Israil took upon his sons, Allah witness to their words, and bade them ask. They asked the mark of a prophet, and he said his eyes sleep while his heart does not. They asked how the woman produces female or male offspring, and he said the two fluids meet: if the man's fluid overcomes the woman's she bears male, if the woman's overcomes the man's she bears female. They asked what Israil forbade himself, and he said he suffered sciatica and found nothing congenial but the milk of such-and-such, some saying camels, so he forbade their meat. They confirmed his truth. They asked what thunder is, and he said a angel among Allah's angels entrusted with clouds, in his hand a striker of fire driving the clouds where Allah commands. Asked about the sound heard, he said it was his voice. They confirmed his truth, leaving one matter: no prophet lacks a bringer of tidings, so who was his companion. He answered Jibril (alayhi al-salam). They objected that Jibril descends with punishment, war, and fighting and was their enemy, whereas had he named Mika'il of mercy, growth, and rain it would have sufficed. Thereupon Allah revealed the verse concerning enmity to Jibril. > **""Bring them. His eyes sleep while his heart does not. If the man's fluid overcomes the woman's fluid she bears male, and if the woman's fluid overcomes the man's fluid she bears female. Thunder is a angel of Allah entrusted with the clouds, in his hand a striker of fire driving them where Allah commands; that sound is his voice. My ally is Jibril.""**"
"13903","In another wording, each time the Prophet ﷺ informed the Jews of something and they confirmed it, he said O Allah bear witness. He adjured them by Allah who sent the Torah to Musa whether they knew the unlettered prophet whose eyes sleep while his heart does not, and they answered affirmatively. He added that his ally was Jibril, and Allah never sent a prophet but was his ally. > **""O Allah, bear witness. I adjure you by Allah who sent the Torah to Musa: do you know that this unlettered prophet's eyes sleep while his heart does not? My ally is Jibril, and Allah never sent a prophet but He was his ally.""**"
"13904","Al-Falatan ibn Asim (radiya Allahu anhu) reports sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when his gaze fixed on a man in the mosque and he called him by name. The man answered at service, and none contended his speech but said O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he testified he was Allah's messenger, and he said no. Asked whether he read the Torah, he said yes, and the Gospel too; asked about the Quran, he swore by his soul he could read it if he willed. The Prophet ﷺ then adjured him whether he found him in the Torah and Gospel. He answered he found one like him in likeness, form, and place of emergence, and they had hoped he would be from them, but when he emerged they doubted whether he was the one, then concluded he was not. Asked why, he said that prophet would have seventy thousand of his nation entering Paradise without reckoning or punishment, while this one had only a small company. The Prophet ﷺ swore by his soul that he was indeed he, and they were his nation, exceeding seventy thousand and seventy thousand. > **""Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? Do you read the Torah? And the Gospel? And the Quran? Do you find me in the Torah and the Gospel? By Him in whose hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and they are indeed my nation; they are more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand.""**"
"13905","Hamza ibn Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam reports that his grandfather Abdullah ibn Salam told the rabbis he would renew a covenant at the mosque of Ibrahim and Ismail, then set out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Makka, meeting him after the pilgrims had departed, finding him at Mina with people around him, and standing with them. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him he asked whether he was Abdullah ibn Salam, and upon confirmation bade him draw near. He adjured him by Allah whether he found him in the Torah as Allah's messenger. Ibn Salam asked him to describe their Lord, whereupon Jibril came and stood before the Prophet ﷺ with the chapter of Sincerity: He is Allah, One, the Eternal, who neither begets nor is born, with none like Him. The Prophet ﷺ recited it, Ibn Salam testified there is no god but Allah and he was His messenger, then returned to Madina concealing his Islam. When the Prophet ﷺ emigrated, Ibn Salam was atop his palm tree cutting dates, heard a commotion, asked its cause, was told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had arrived, threw himself from the tree, hastened until he reached him, greeted him, and returned. His mother reproached him that he would not have thrown himself so even for Musa ibn Imran (alayhi al-salam). He swore his joy at the Prophet's ﷺ advent exceeded his joy at Musa's commissioning. > **""Are you Abdullah ibn Salam? Draw near. I adjure you by Allah, O Abdullah ibn Salam: do you find me in the Torah as the Messenger of Allah? He is Allah, One; Allah, the Eternal; He neither begets nor is born; and none is like Him.""**"
"13906","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the first report reaching them about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerned a woman with a familiar spirit. It came to her in a bird's form and perched on their trunk, and she invited it down to exchange tidings. It told her a man had emerged at Makka forbidding adultery and depriving them of settled rest. > **""A man has emerged at Makka forbidding us adultery and depriving us of settled rest.""**"
"13907","Mujahid reports from an aged man who reached Jahiliyya, met during the Rhodes campaign, called Ibn Isa. He was driving a cow for his household when he heard from its belly a call to the house of Dharih, an eloquent saying, a man crying that there is no god but Allah. They came to Makka and found the Prophet ﷺ had emerged there. > **""O house of Dharih, an eloquent saying, a man crying that there is no god but Allah.""**"
"13908","Jubayr ibn Mutim (radiya Allahu anhu) reports standing about their idol a month before the Prophet's ﷺ commissioning, having sacrificed a camel, when a crier called from its belly bidding them hear the wonder: polytheism and defilement gone, the heavens pelted with flames, for a prophet at Makka named Ahmad whose migration would be to Yathrib. > **""Hear the wonder: polytheism and defilement are gone, the heavens are pelted with flames, for a prophet at Makka named Ahmad, whose migration is to Yathrib.""**"
"13909","Amr ibn Murra al-Juhani (radiya Allahu anhu) reports departing for pilgrimage with his people in Jahiliyya and seeing in sleep at Makka a radiant light from the Kaba reaching the mountains of Yathrib and kindling Juhayna, with a voice in the light proclaiming the darkness dispelled, the radiance risen, and the Seal of the Prophets commissioned. A further illumination showed the palaces of Hira and the white of Mada'in, with a voice proclaiming Islam manifest, idols broken, and kinship joined. He awoke alarmed, told his people an event would befall Quraysh, and narrated the vision. Then the Prophet ﷺ addressed him by name as the messenger sent to all servants, summoning to Islam, sparing blood, joining kin, worshipping Allah, abandoning idols, pilgrimage to the House, and fasting Ramadan among twelve months, with Paradise for obedience and Fire for refusal, bidding Amr believe to be secured from Hell's terror. Amr testified there is no god but Allah and he was His messenger, believed all he brought of lawful and unlawful though many peoples would resent it, then recited verses breaking first with stone gods, girding his waist as an emigrant to the best of men in soul and father, the messenger of the King above the saddles. The Prophet ﷺ welcomed him, and at his request to be sent to his people in hope Allah would set him over them as He had favoured him through the Prophet ﷺ, dispatched him with charge to gentleness, upright speech, and avoidance of harshness, arrogance, and envy. He came to his people addressing Banu Rifaa and Juhayna as messenger of Allah's messenger, summoning to Paradise, warning of the Fire, commanding sparing of blood, joining kin, worship of Allah, abandonment of idols, pilgrimage, and fasting Ramadan among twelve months, reminding them Allah had made them the best of their stock and spared them in Jahiliyya the sister-marrying, stepmother-marrying, and sacred-month raiding others loved, urging them to answer the dispatched prophet from Banu Luayy ibn Ghalib for this world's honour and the next. They answered except one man who cursed his livelihood and refused to abandon gods, sunder unity, or oppose fathers for a Tihami Qurashi, versifying that Ibn Murra's words sought no good and would prove wind, reviling the forefather sheikhs. Amr retorted the liar among them would have his mouth embittered, tongue muted, eyes blinded, teeth fallen; and indeed the man died with fallen mouth, tasteless food, blind and dumb. Amr with his followers came to the Prophet ﷺ, who welcomed, gifted, and wrote them a document in Allah's name upon His messenger's tongue, a true speaking writ for Amr ibn Murra al-Juhani and Juhayna ibn Zaydan: valleys, plains, and heights to pasture and drink pure upon affirming the fifth, praying the five, and paying two sheep when flocks joined in plenty and one each when parted, with no charity on the scavenger-fed. Qays ibn Shammas wrote it with witness of their Prophet ﷺ and present Muslims. Amr versified Allah's manifest religion and Quranic proof, a writ from the Merciful joining them in desert and town to the best walker on earth, obeying Allah's messenger as enemy bellies were cut by trenchant blades, a tribe round whom glory was built, warriors with long hands and flashing swords, the Ansar guarding their commander with spears and cutting swords, his face brightening like light's radiance. Yasir ibn Suwayd added the Prophet ﷺ once dispatched him with cavalry while his wife was pregnant; she bore a son, brought him asking a name while the father was out, the Prophet ﷺ passed his hand over him, supplicated increase of their men, fewness of their days, freedom from need and want, and named him Musri for hastening into Islam. > **""O Amr ibn Murra, I am the prophet sent to all servants. I summon them to Islam, command sparing of blood, joining of kin, worship of Allah, abandonment of idols, pilgrimage to the House, and fasting the month of Ramadan among twelve months. Whoever answers has Paradise, and whoever disobeys has the Fire. Believe in Allah, O Amr, and Allah will secure you from the terror of Hell. Welcome, O Amr ibn Murra. Hold to gentleness and upright speech, and be not harsh, nor arrogant, nor envious. In the name of Allah, the Merciful, the Compassionate: this is a writ from Allah upon the tongue of His messenger, a true and speaking writ for Amr ibn Murra al-Juhani and Juhayna ibn Zaydan. O Allah, increase their men, lessen their days, leave them unneeding, and let none of them see want. Name him Musri, for he hastened into Islam.""**"
"13910","Abbas ibn Mirdas al-Sulami (radiya Allahu anhu) reports his Islam beginning at noon among his milch camels at Umra, when a white ostrich like cotton appeared bearing a rider in garments white as cotton, addressing him by name, asking whether he had not seen heaven withholding its guards, war drained of breath, horses unsaddled, and the bringer of righteousness and guidance appearing Monday night, master of the she-camel. Terrified by what he saw and heard, he went to their idol called al-Dimad, which they worshipped and which spoke from its belly. After sweeping about it, wiping it, and kissing it, a crier from within called him by name with verses bidding him tell all the clans of Sulaym that al-Samad had perished while the people of the mosque triumphed, that the bringer of prophethood and guidance after the son of Maryam was a guided Qurashi, perished the once-worshipped idol before prayer upon Muhammad. Terrified, he told his people the whole account, then led three hundred riders of Banu Haritha to the Prophet ﷺ. Entering the mosque, the Prophet ﷺ smiled and asked how his Islam came about. Upon hearing the account he confirmed its truth and rejoiced, and Abbas with his people embraced Islam. > **""O Abbas ibn Mirdas, have you not seen that heaven has withheld its guards, war has been drained of breath, horses have been unsaddled, and the bringer of righteousness and guidance has come on Monday night, master of the she-camel? Tell all the clans of Sulaym: al-Samad has perished and the people of the mosque have triumphed; the bringer of prophethood and guidance after the son of Maryam is a guided Qurashi; perished is al-Samad, once worshipped before prayer upon Muhammad. O Abbas ibn Mirdas, how did your Islam come about? You have spoken truly.""**"
"13911","Mazin ibn al-Ghaduba reports keeping the idol Bahir at Sama'il village in Oman. Passing one day with a small sacrificial goat, he heard a voice from the idol bidding Mazin hear and rejoice: good manifest and evil buried, a prophet commissioned from Mudar with Allah's greatest religion, so abandon carved stone to be saved from Saqar's heat. Alarmed at the wonder, he passed days later and heard it bid him approach and hear what he could not ignore: a dispatched prophet with revealed truth, so believe to turn from kindled fire fuelled with stones. Judging good intended for him, a Hijazi traveller then reported a man called Ahmad summoning comers to answer Allah's caller. Recognizing the tidings, Mazin smashed the idol to fragments, mounted, reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who explained Islam, and he embraced it with verses declaring Bahir broken to pieces after blind circumambulation in error, guidance through the Hashimi unsuspected, and a message to Amr and his Khuthama brothers that whoever called Bahir his lord lied. He confessed fondness for revelry, wine, and dissolute women, with years consuming wealth, emaciating offspring, and leaving him childless, asking supplication to remove his affliction, grant modesty, and bestow a son. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated Allah to replace revelry with Quran recitation, the unlawful with the lawful, debauchery with chastity, wine with blameless quenching, to send rain, and grant a son. Mazin affirms Allah removed his affliction and granted him Habbar ibn Mazin, whom he praised in verses of his long ride from Oman seeking intercession, opposing his people's religion, and exchanging wine and debauchery for fear, chastity, struggle, fasting, and pilgrimage. Returning to his people, they reproached and reviled him and set their poet to lampoon him; he answered that answering them meant lampooning himself, withdrew to the seashore with verses on mutual rancour, until a great company came asking him to resume their affairs as their custodian, which he did, and Allah later guided them to Islam. > **""O Allah, replace for him revelry with recitation of the Quran, the unlawful with the lawful, debauchery with chastity of private parts, and wine with quenching free of sin; send them rain and grant him a son.""**"
"13912","Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi reports Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) seated in the mosque when a man passed at the rear. Asked whether he knew the comer, Umar did not, and was told it was Sawad ibn Qarib of Yemen, honoured among them, whom his familiar spirit had told of the Prophet's ﷺ emergence. Umar summoned him, confirmed his identity and the tidings, and asked whether he still practised soothsaying. Sawad flared that none had confronted him so since embracing Islam, and Umar answered their former polytheism outweighed his soothsaying, pressing for the account. Sawad recalled lying between sleep and wakefulness when his familiar struck him with his foot bidding him rise, understand, and use reason, for a messenger from Luayy ibn Ghalib had been commissioned summoning to Allah and His worship, then versified wonder at the jinn saddling camels toward Makka seeking guidance, urging journey to the elite of Hashim. Sawad dismissed him to sleep, drowsy. The second night the visit was repeated with verses on the jinn's seeking and saddling, urging journey to the elite of Hashim, and again he paid no heed. The third night brought further verses on the jinn's tidings, the believers among them unlike their disbelievers, and journey to the elite of Hashim among its hills and stones. Love of Islam fell into his heart and he desired it. At dawn he saddled his mount for Makka, learned on the way the Prophet ﷺ had emigrated to Madina, came to the mosque, hobbled his mount, and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with people around him. Asking leave to speak, Abu Bakr (radiya Allahu anhu) drew him forward until he stood before the Prophet ﷺ, who bade him report his familiar's visits. Sawad recited verses of his nightly visitant over three nights announcing a messenger from Luayy ibn Ghalib, his own journey through wastes, his testimony that none but Allah is Lord and the Prophet ﷺ trusted over all unseen, foremost of messengers in means to Allah, asking command by what came even greying forelocks and intercession on a day no intercession but his avails Sawad ibn Qarib. The Prophet ﷺ and his Companions rejoiced intensely at his Islam, visible on their faces, and Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) sprang to him, embraced him, and said he had loved to hear this from him. > **""Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib, understand and use reason. A messenger from Luayy ibn Ghalib has been commissioned, summoning to Allah and His worship. Bring them to me and tell me of your familiar's visits.""**"
"13913","In another wording from the same narrator, Sawad ibn Qarib al-Azdi (radiya Allahu anhu) reports sleeping on a mountain of al-Sawa when a comer struck him with his foot, containing the same tidings: he came to Makka, found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ manifest, reported the account, and followed him."
"13914","Al-Hasan ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi reports Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) asking Ibn Abbas for a pleasing narration. Ibn Abbas related from Khuraym ibn Fatik al-Asadi (radiya Allahu anhu) that he went seeking a stray camel and found it at Abraq al-Azzaf, hobbled it, pillowed his head on a camel's foreleg shortly after the Prophet's ﷺ emergence, and sought refuge in the chief of the valley as Jahiliyya folk did. A caller cried bidding him seek refuge in Allah of Majesty, Revealer of lawful and unlawful, affirm His oneness heedless of the jinn's terrors when Allah is remembered over miles, plains, and mountains, for the jinn's plotting had sunk low save for piety and righteous deeds. Khuraym challenged the caller whether he bore guidance or misguidance, and the voice answered this was Allah's messenger of blessings, come with Ya Sin, the Ha Mim chapters, and detailed chapters of prohibitions and permissions, commanding fasting and prayer and restraining men from vices once deemed repugnant. Asked his identity, he named himself Malik, dispatched by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ over the jinn of Najd. Khuraym wished for one to guard his camels so he might go and believe, Malik guaranteed their safe return, Khuraym secured one camel and reached Madina on a Friday during prayer. As he halted his mount, Abu Dharr (radiya Allahu anhu) emerged bidding him enter at the Prophet's ﷺ command. Entering, the Prophet ﷺ asked after the elder who had guaranteed his camels and confirmed they had arrived safely. Khuraym invoked mercy upon him, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed it, and Khuraym testified there is no god but Allah. > **""Woe to you, seek refuge in Allah of Majesty, Revealer of the lawful and unlawful. Affirm Allah's oneness and heed not the terror of the jinn. This is the Messenger of Allah of blessings, come with Ya Sin, the Ha Mims, and detailed chapters of prohibitions and permissions, commanding fasting and prayer and restraining men from vices. Enter. What became of the elder who guaranteed to deliver your camels? He has indeed delivered them safely. Yes, may Allah have mercy on him.""**"
"13915","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports Khuraym ibn Fatik telling Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) the beginning of his Islam. While circling after his livestock, night enveloped him at Abraq al-Iraq, and he cried at the top of his voice for refuge in the mighty one of the valley against its foolish folk. A caller answered bidding him seek refuge in Allah of Majesty, glory, blessing, and bounty, take verses of the Spoils, and affirm Allah's oneness heedless of else. Terrified, then composed, Khuraym challenged the caller to clarify guidance or misguidance and its means. The voice answered this was Allah's messenger of blessings at Yathrib summoning to salvation, commanding fasting and prayer and restraining men from vices. Khuraym's mount stirred, and he prayed for guidance, sustenance, clothing, lasting felicity, and a share of the good tidings. The caller followed invoking Allah's safeguarding of him, his soul, his family, and his mount, urging faith and support with promise of his Lord's prospering and strengthening. Khuraym entered Madina on a Friday, peered into the mosque, and Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (radiya Allahu anhu) emerged bidding him enter, saying news of his Islam had reached them. Confessing ignorance of purification, he was taught, entered, and saw the Prophet ﷺ on the minbar radiant as the full moon, declaring no Muslim perfects ablution then prays a light, mindful prayer but enters Paradise. Umar demanded proof on pain of exemplary punishment, whereupon Uthman ibn Affan (radiya Allahu anhu), elder of Quraysh, testified and his testimony was accepted. > **""No Muslim performs ablution excellently, then prays a prayer which he lightens and performs mindfully, but he shall enter Paradise. Believe in him, and my Lord will prosper your right; support him, and my Lord will strengthen your support.""**"
"13916","Abdullah ibn Said reports that Allah looked upon the hearts of His servants and found the heart of Muhammad ﷺ the best of them, so He chose him for Himself and commissioned him with His message. The complete wording has already preceded in the chapter on consensus."
"13917","Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when Adam (alayhi al-salam) committed his lapse, he raised his head to the Throne and begged forgiveness by the right of Muhammad. Allah asked who Muhammad was, and Adam answered that when created he raised his head to the Throne and saw inscribed there that there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, so he knew none ranked higher with Him than one whose name stood with His. Allah then told him he was the last of the prophets from his progeny, his nation the last nation from his progeny, and but for him Adam would not have been created. > **""I ask You by the right of Muhammad to forgive me. Blessed is Your name; when You created me I raised my head to Your Throne and saw written there: there is no god but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. O Adam, he is the last of the prophets from your progeny, his nation the last nation from your progeny, and but for him I would not have created you.""**"
"13918","Ali al-Hilali reports entering upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the place where he passed away, with Fatima (radiya Allahu anha) at his head. She wept until her voice rose, and the Prophet ﷺ raised his glance to her asking what made her weep. She said she feared destitution after him. He assured her Allah had surveyed the earth and chosen her father to commission with His message, then surveyed it again and chosen her husband, commanding their marriage by revelation. He told her they were a Household granted seven traits given none before them nor after: he was the Seal of the Prophets, the noblest of prophets before Allah, the dearest of creation to Allah, and her father. The remainder of the narration stands complete in the chapter on the Household's merit. > **""My beloved Fatima, what makes you weep? Do you not know that Allah surveyed the earth and chose from it your father, commissioning him with His message, then surveyed the earth and chose from it your husband, commanding me to marry you to him? I am the Seal of the Prophets, the noblest of the prophets before Allah, the dearest of creation to Allah, and I am your father.""**"
"13919","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Fatima (radiya Allahu anha) that she knew Allah had surveyed the people of the earth and chosen her father, commissioning him a prophet, then surveyed a second time and chosen her husband, whereupon Allah revealed to him and he married her to him and took him as trustee. > **""Do you not know that Allah surveyed the people of the earth and chose from them your father, commissioning him a prophet, then surveyed a second time and chose your husband, and Allah revealed to me, so I married you to him and took him as trustee?""**"
"13920","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (radiya Allahu anhu) also reports in the Smaller collection that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared their Prophet the best of the prophets. > **""Our Prophet is the best of the prophets.""**"
"13921","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he asked his Lord a question he wished he had not asked. He asked how messengers before him had winds subjected or raised the dead, and his Lord answered recounting His favours: finding him an orphan and sheltering him, finding him astray and guiding him, finding him poor and enriching him, expanding his breast and removing his burden. He replied affirming them before his Lord. > **""My Lord, there were messengers before me, among them one for whom You subjected the winds and one who raised the dead. Did I not find you an orphan and shelter you? Did I not find you astray and guide you? Did I not find you poor and enrich you? Did I not expand your breast and remove your burden? Yes, my Lord.""**"
"13922","Abu Said (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Jibril came and conveyed his Lord's and Jibril's Lord's question how his mention was raised. He answered Allah knew best, and the reply came that whenever He is mentioned, the Prophet ﷺ is mentioned with Him. > **""Jibril came to me and said: my Lord and your Lord says: how have I raised your mention? Allah knows best. When I am mentioned, you are mentioned with Me.""**"
"13923","Abdullah ibn Salam (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared himself master of Adam's children on Resurrection Day without boast, first over whom the earth splits, first intercessor and first granted intercession, with the Banner of Praise in his hand and Adam and those below under him. > **""I am the master of the children of Adam on the Day of Resurrection, without boast; the first over whom the earth splits; the first intercessor and the first granted intercession. In my hand is the Banner of Praise, beneath me Adam and those below him.""**"
"13924","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ his declaration that he was leader of the messengers without boast, Seal of the Prophets without boast, and first intercessor and granted intercession without boast. > **""I am the leader of the messengers, without boast; I am the Seal of the Prophets, without boast; I am the first intercessor and granted intercession, without boast.""**"
"13925","Abdullah ibn Salam (radiya Allahu anhu) declared the noblest of Allah's creation before Allah on Resurrection Day was Abu al-Qasim ﷺ. Asked in compassion whether that included the angels, he repeated that the noblest of Allah's creation before Allah was Abu al-Qasim ﷺ."
"13926","From the same narrator, by Him in whose hand is his soul, the nearest of men on Resurrection Day will be Muhammad ﷺ, seated at His right upon the Kursi. Its chain contains an unnamed man."
"13927","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) held Allah favoured Muhammad over the people of heaven and earth. Asked by what, he cited Allah's warning to heaven's folk that whoever among them claimed divinity besides Him would be recompensed with Hell, contrasted with Allah's opening to Muhammad of manifest victory and forgiveness of past and future fault. Asked his superiority over the prophets, he cited the rule that every messenger was sent in his people's tongue, contrasted with Muhammad's commission to all mankind as bearer of glad tidings and warner, sent to jinn and men."
"13928","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud (radiya Allahu anhu), reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah took Ibrahim as intimate friend, and their companion was the intimate friend of Allah, with Muhammad ﷺ master of Adam's children on Resurrection Day. He then recited the verse that his Lord may raise him to a praised station. > **""Allah took Ibrahim as intimate friend, and your companion is the intimate friend of Allah. Muhammad is the master of the children of Adam on the Day of Resurrection. It may be that your Lord will raise you to a praised station.""**"
"13929","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) held the best of Adam's children were five: Nuh, Ibrahim, Isa, Musa, and Muhammad ﷺ, with the best among them Muhammad ﷺ, and invoked Allah's blessings and peace upon them all."
"13930","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Prophet ﷺ told Khadija (radiya Allahu anha) he saw a light and heard a voice, fearing madness afflicted him. She answered Allah would never do that to him, son of Abdullah, then went to Waraqa ibn Nawfal and mentioned it. Waraqa replied if he spoke truly this was a visitation like that of Musa (alayhi al-salam), and if commissioned in his lifetime he would strengthen, support, and believe in him. > **""I see a light and hear a voice, and I fear madness is in me. Allah would never do that to you, O son of Abdullah. If he speaks truly, this is a visitation like the visitation of Musa; if he is commissioned while I live, I shall strengthen him, support him, and believe in him.""**"
"13931","Abu Dharr (radiya Allahu anhu) reports they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ how he knew he was a prophet. He answered he knew only when two angels came to him in a Makkan valley, one asking whether this was he, then bidding weigh him against one man, and he outweighed him; against ten, and he outweighed them; against a hundred, and he outweighed them; against a thousand, and he outweighed them. One told the other had he been weighed against his nation he would have outweighed it. Then one bade slit his belly, removed the Devil's touch and clotted blood and cast them away, bade wash his belly like a vessel and his heart like a cloth, summoned tranquillity like a white chick and placed it in his heart, bade stitch his belly, and set the Seal between his shoulders. No sooner had they turned than he beheld the matter as eye-witness. In one addition they scattered upon him from the scale's pan. > **""Is it he? Weigh him against one man; weigh him against ten; weigh him against a hundred; weigh him against a thousand. Had you weighed him against his nation he would have outweighed it. Slit his belly; wash his belly as a vessel is washed and his heart as a cloth is washed; stitch his belly.""**"
"13932","Abu Said (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the people of camels and people of sheep boasting before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon he observed Musa was commissioned while herding sheep for his household, and he himself was commissioned while herding sheep for his household at Jiyad. > **""Musa was commissioned while herding sheep for his household, and I was commissioned while herding sheep for my household at Jiyad.""**"
"13933","Waraqa al-Ansari (radiya Allahu anhu) reports asking Muhammad how the comer, meaning Jibril (alayhi al-salam), reached him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered he came to him from heaven with wings of pearl and the insides of his feet green. > **""He comes to me from heaven, his two wings pearl and the insides of his feet green.""**"
"13934","Abdullah ibn Amr (radiya Allahu anhu) reports asking the Prophet ﷺ whether he sensed revelation. He answered yes: he heard a ringing, then fell silent at it, and no revelation ever came but he supposed his soul seized. > **""Yes, I hear a ringing, then I fall silent at it. No revelation ever comes to me but I suppose my soul is seized.""**"
"13935","Khadija (radiya Allahu anha) reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, O son of her uncle, whether he could inform her when the comer arrived. He agreed. One day Jibril came while she was with him, and the Prophet ﷺ announced his companion's arrival. She bade him sit on her right thigh and asked whether he saw him; he said yes. She moved him to her left thigh and lap with the same question and answer each time. Then she uncovered, cast off her veil, and asked again; he answered no. She declared by Allah this was a noble angel, not a devil. She reported to Waraqa ibn Nawfal ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy what Muhammad the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had told her, and Waraqa affirmed her report as true. > **""Yes, O Khadija. O Khadija, this companion of mine who comes to me has come. Yes. No. Can you inform me when the comer comes to you? Sit on my right thigh; sit on my left thigh; sit in my lap. Do you see him? This, by Allah, is a noble angel; by Allah, he is no devil. True is your report, O Khadija.""**"
"13936","Al-Harith ibn Hisham (radiya Allahu anhu) reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ how revelation reached him. He answered it came as a ringing like a bell's ringing, and sometimes in a man's form speaking words, the easier upon him, then parting having retained it. > **""It comes to me as a ringing like the ringing of a bell, and at times it comes in a man's form and speaks to me, which is easier upon me; then it parts from me having retained it.""**"
"13937","Aisha (radiya Allahu anha) reports revelation would descend upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he rode, and his mount would strike the ground with its chest."
"13938","Zayd ibn Thabit (radiya Allahu anhu) reports writing revelation for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When it descended, intense distress seized him and heavy sweat like pearls poured, then it was lifted. Zayd would enter with a palm-stalk piece or fragment and write as the Prophet ﷺ dictated, until before finishing his leg nearly broke under the Quran's weight and he thought he would never walk upon his leg again. When done, the Prophet ﷺ bade him recite it; he recited, and any omission was set right, then he carried it out to the people. > **""Recite it.""**"
"13939","Qays ibn Makhrama (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was born in the Year of the Elephant, with twenty years between the Elephant and the Fijar, so named for their violating what they had held sacred; fifteen years between the Fijar and the building of the Kaba; and five years between the building and the Prophet's ﷺ commission, with commission at forty."
"13940","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared he was but commissioned as a gifted mercy. > **""I was but commissioned as a gifted mercy.""**"
"13941","Anas (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say Jibril came to him in the form of Dihya al-Kalbi. Anas added Dihya was a handsome, fair man. > **""Jibril comes to me in the form of Dihya al-Kalbi.""**"
"13942","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Prophet ﷺ asked Jibril (alayhi al-salam) to show himself in his form. Jibril bade him supplicate his Lord, he did, and a darkness rose from the east, swelling and spreading. When the Prophet ﷺ saw it he swooned, Jibril came, covered him, and wiped the spittle from his cheeks."
"13943","Aisha (radiya Allahu anha) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he saw Jibril descending, filling what lay between heaven and earth, wearing garments of sundus with pearl and ruby suspended upon him. > **""I saw Jibril descending, filling what is between the heaven and the earth, upon him garments of sundus with pearl and ruby suspended.""**"
"13944","Abu Musa (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted five: commission to red and black, the earth made pure and a mosque, spoils permitted him as to none before, aid through awe a month's journey, and intercession, which every prophet had already asked while he had stored his and made it for whoever of his nation died associating nothing with Allah. > **""I was granted five: I was commissioned to the red and the black; the earth was made for me pure and a mosque; spoils were permitted me, never permitted those before me; I was aided through awe a month's journey; and I was granted intercession, which I stored and made for whoever of my nation dies associating nothing with Allah.""**"
"13945","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted five no prophet before him received, without boast: commission to red and black, aid through awe a month's journey, spoils permitted him alone before, the earth made mosque and purification, and intercession deferred for his nation, for whoever died associating nothing with Allah. > **""I was granted five no prophet before me received, and I say it without boast: I was commissioned to the red and the black; I was aided through awe a month's journey; spoils were permitted me, never permitted any before me; the earth was made for me mosque and purification; and I was granted intercession, which I deferred for my nation, for whoever dies associating nothing with Allah.""**"
"13946","In another wording, none of red or black entering his nation but would be among them of intercession. In other wordings, even the enemy feared him a month's or two months' journey, and he was told to ask and be given, so he stored his supplication as intercession for his nation. > **""There is none of red or black who enters my nation but shall be among them. Even the enemy fears me a month's or two months' journey. Ask and you shall be given, so I stored my supplication as intercession for my nation.""**"
"13947","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted five no prophet before him among prophets received: the earth made purification and mosque, whereas no prophet before would pray until reaching his sanctuary; aid through awe a month's journey, cast by Allah into the hearts of the polytheists ahead of him; every prophet commissioned to his own people while he was commissioned to jinn and men; and the remainder of the five in the full narration. > **""I was granted five no one before me among the prophets received: the earth was made for me purification and mosque; I was aided through awe a month's journey ahead of me; every prophet was commissioned to his own people, while I was commissioned to jinn and men.""**"
"13948","Ali ibn Abi Talib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted five no prophet before him received: aid through awe, comprehensive speech, and permitted spoils. He mentioned two further traits which escaped the narrator, and the full narration was related. > **""I was granted five no prophet before me received: I was aided through awe; I was granted comprehensive speech; spoils were permitted me.""**"
"13949","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was aided through awe against his enemy a two months' journey."
"13950","Abu Dharr (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ his grant of five no one before him received: the earth made purification and mosque, spoils permitted him as to no prophet before, aid through awe a month's journey against his enemy, commission to every red and black, and intercession attaining whoever of his nation associated nothing with Allah. In another wording, whoever died associating nothing with Allah. > **""I was granted five no one before me received: the earth was made for me purification and mosque; spoils were permitted me, never permitted a prophet before me; I was aided through awe a month's journey against my enemy; I was commissioned to every red and black; and I was granted intercession, attaining whoever of my nation associates nothing with Allah.""**"
"13951","Abu Umama (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was favoured with four: the earth made mosque and purification for his nation, his dispatch to all mankind, aid through awe a month's journey ahead of him, and spoils permitted his nation. > **""I was favoured with four: the earth was made for my nation mosque and purification; I was dispatched to all mankind; I was aided through awe a month's journey ahead of me; and spoils were permitted my nation.""**"
"13952","In another wording, wherever prayer overtook a man of his nation, there were his mosque and his purification. In another wording, commission to every white and black. > **""Wherever prayer overtakes a man of my nation, there are his mosque and his purification. I was commissioned to every white and black.""**"
"13953","Ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted five no prophet before him received: commission to all mankind, red and black, whereas every prophet before was commissioned to his village; aid through awe with his enemy terrified a month's journey; granted spoil; the earth made mosque and purification; and intercession deferred for his nation. > **""I was granted five no prophet before me received: I was commissioned to all mankind, red and black; I was aided through awe, my enemy terrified a month's journey; I was granted spoil; the earth was made for me mosque and purification; and I was granted intercession, which I deferred for my nation.""**"
"13954","Al-Saib ibn Yazid (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was favoured over the prophets with five: commission to all mankind, his intercession stored for his nation, aid through awe a month ahead and a month behind, the earth made mosque and purification, and spoils permitted him as to none before him. > **""I was favoured over the prophets with five: I was commissioned to all mankind; I stored my intercession for my nation; I was aided through awe a month ahead of me and a month behind me; the earth was made for me mosque and purification; and spoils were permitted me, never permitted any before me.""**"
"13955","Aisha (radiya Allahu anha) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when revelation first came to him, or commission, or a like word, no stone or tree he passed but said peace upon him, O Messenger of Allah. > **""When revelation came to me, no stone or tree I passed but said: peace upon you, O Messenger of Allah.""**"
"13956","Ali (radiya Allahu anhu) reports going out with the Prophet ﷺ, and no stone or tree he passed but greeted him with peace."
"13957","Ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was visited in sleep-vision by two angels, one sitting at his feet and one at his head. The one at his feet bade the one at his head strike a parable of him and his nation. He answered their likeness was travellers reaching the head of a waterless desert with provisions enough neither to cross nor return, when a man came in fine garments saying he would lead them to sweet water and lush pasture if they followed. One party said he spoke truthfully and followed to safety, another lay down in place. They proposed striking the parable and interpreting it in turn, concluding the leader was their Prophet ﷺ and the parties those who obeyed or disobeyed him. > **""Strike a parable of him and his nation: travellers at the head of a waterless desert, then a guide offering sweet water and lush pasture. Whoever follows him is saved, and whoever stays perishes.""**"
"13958","Rabia al-Jarshi (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was visited and told to let his eye sleep, his ear hear, and his heart retain. He said his eye slept, his ear heard, and his heart retained. He was told a master built a house, prepared a feast, and sent a caller: whoever answered entered the house, ate of the feast, and earned the master's pleasure, and whoever refused neither entered nor attained. The interpretation followed in the full narration. > **""Let your eye sleep, your ear hear, and your heart retain. A master built a house, prepared a feast, and sent a caller; whoever answers the caller enters the house, eats of the feast, and the master is pleased with him.""**"
"13959","Abdullah ibn Masud (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ outran him until they reached such-and-such place, where the Prophet ﷺ drew a line and bade him remain amid it and never leave, for leaving meant ruin. He stayed while the Prophet ﷺ went far off into the distance, then figures crowded about him and barred his way, filling him with intense terror, and he sat. At daybreak's splitting they departed. The Prophet ﷺ then returned heavy and pained from what had mounted him, said he felt heavy, and laid his head in Abdullah's lap. Then men in long white garments came while the Prophet ﷺ slumbered, terrifying Abdullah worse than before. They proposed striking him a parable, differing who strikes and who interprets, then likened him to a master who built a fortress and sent men food: whoever did not come to his food, or follow him, would be severely punished. The master was the Lord of the worlds, the building Islam, the food Paradise, and he the caller: whoever followed him was in Paradise, whoever did not was punished. The Prophet ﷺ then asked what Ibn Umm Abd had seen; Abdullah told it, and the Prophet ﷺ said nothing they said was hidden from him: they were a company of angels. > **""Remain between these lines; do not leave them, for if you leave them you will perish. I feel heavy. What did you see, O son of Umm Abd? Nothing they said was hidden from me.""**"
"13960","Abu Musa (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said none of this nation, neither Jew nor Christian, heard of him without believing but would be among the Fire's folk. The narrator added the Prophet ﷺ said nothing but what stood in Allah's Book, reciting that the Fire was the appointment of whoever among the parties disbelieved in him. > **""None of this nation hears of me, neither Jew nor Christian, without believing in me, but he shall be among the people of the Fire. Whoever among the parties disbelieves in him, the Fire is his appointment.""**"
"13961","In another wording, whoever did not believe in him would not enter Paradise. > **""Whoever does not believe in me shall not enter Paradise.""**"
"13962","Abu Hurayra (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by Him in whose hand was Muhammad's soul that none of this nation, neither Jew nor Christian, heard of him and died disbelieving his message but would be among the Fire's companions. > **""By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, none of this nation hears of me, neither Jew nor Christian, then dies disbelieving in what I was sent with, but he shall be among the companions of the Fire.""**"
"13963","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports Umar ibn al-Khattab (radiya Allahu anhu) bringing the Prophet ﷺ a book taken from some People of the Book and reading it to him. He grew angry and asked whether they wavered in it, swearing he had brought it white and pure: they must not ask them about anything lest they tell truth and be disbelieved or tell falsehood and be believed, and had Musa lived he could but follow him. > **""Are you wavering in it, O son of Khattab? By Him in whose hand is my soul, I have brought it to you white and pure. Do not ask them about anything, lest they tell you truth and you disbelieve it, or tell you falsehood and you believe it. By Him in whose hand is my soul, had Musa lived, he could but follow me.""**"
"13964","Tamim al-Dari (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare this affair would reach wherever night and day reached, and Allah would leave no house of clay or hair but admit this religion through a mighty one's honour or a lowly one's humiliation: honour Allah granted Islam, humiliation He inflicted on disbelief. Tamim added he saw it in his household: those embracing Islam gained good, honour, and might, while disbelievers among them met humiliation, littleness, and tribute. > **""This affair shall reach wherever night and day reach, and Allah shall leave no house of clay or hair but Allah shall admit this religion into it, through a mighty one's honour or a lowly one's humiliation: honour Allah grants Islam, and humiliation Allah inflicts on disbelief.""**"
"13965","Abu Thalaba al-Khushani (radiya Allahu anhu) reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, upon returning from travel, would begin at the mosque with two cycles, then visit Fatima (radiya Allahu anha), then greet his wives. Returning once, after praying in the mosque he came to Fatima, who met him at the door kissing his mouth and eyes and weeping. Asked what made her weep, she said she saw him dishevelled, weary, his garments worn. He bade her not weep, for Allah had commissioned her father with a matter leaving no house of clay, stone, hair, or wool upon earth's face but Allah would bring it honour or humiliation, until it reached where night reached. > **""What makes you weep? Do not weep, for Allah has commissioned your father with a matter that shall leave no house of clay, stone, hair, or wool upon the face of the earth but Allah shall bring it honour or humiliation, until it reaches where night reaches.""**"
"13966","Muawiya (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ his declaration that he was but a conveyer while Allah guided, with the remainder of the narration related in full. > **""I am but a conveyer, and Allah guides.""**"
"13967","Abu Umama al-Bahili (radiya Allahu anhu) reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in his sermon completing the Farewell Pilgrimage that there would be no prophet after him nor any nation after his, with the remainder of the narration related in full. > **""O men, there is no prophet after me, nor any nation after you.""**"
"13968","Ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ his declaration that he was granted the keys of all things except the five of the verse: with Allah alone is knowledge of the Hour, He sends down rain, knows what lies in wombs, no soul knows what it earns tomorrow, and no soul knows in what land it dies, for Allah is Knowing, Aware. > **""I was granted the keys of all things except the five: with Allah is knowledge of the Hour; He sends down the rain; He knows what is in the wombs; no soul knows what it shall earn tomorrow; and no soul knows in what land it shall die.""**"
"13969","ʿAbd Allah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, رضي الله عنه, said: Your Prophet ﷺ was given the keys of all things except five: Verily Allah, with Him alone is knowledge of the Hour, He sends down rain, knows what is in the wombs, no soul knows what it will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die; verily Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware, Luqman 34. > **""Your Prophet was given the keys of all things except five: Verily Allah, with Him alone is knowledge of the Hour, He sends down rain, knows what is in the wombs, no soul knows what it will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die; verily Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware.""**"
"13970","Abu Musa al-Ashʿari, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I have been given the openings and sealings of speech. We said: O Messenger of Allah, teach us from what Allah has taught you, so he taught us the tashahhud. > **""I have been given the openings and sealings of speech.""**"
"13971","Abu Dharr, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left us, and no bird flapped its wings in the sky except that he mentioned to us some knowledge concerning it. In an addition the Prophet ﷺ said: Nothing remains that brings near to Paradise and distances from the Fire except that it has been explained to you. > **""Nothing remains that brings near to Paradise and distances from the Fire except that it has been explained to you.""**"
"13972","Al-Mughira ibn Shuʿba, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among us in a station and informed us of what will occur in his Ummah until the Day of Resurrection; whoever retained it retained it, and whoever forgot it forgot it."
"13973","Abu al-Dardaʾ, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left us, and there was no bird flying in the sky with its wings except that he mentioned to us some knowledge concerning it."
"13974","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, رضي الله عنه, said: I retained from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a thousand parables."
"13975","ʿImran ibn Husayn, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would narrate to us for most of the night about Banu Israil, and he would not rise except for the major prayer."
"13976","In a narration, meaning the prescribed obligatory prayer, that he would rise only for it during that long narration of the night."
"13977","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported from the Prophet ﷺ: Sacrifice has been prescribed upon me and has not been prescribed upon you. > **""Sacrifice has been prescribed upon me and has not been prescribed upon you.""**"
"13978","In a narration: I have been commanded with two rakʿas of Duha and you have not been commanded with them, and I have been commanded with sacrifice and it has not been made obligatory upon you. > **""I have been commanded with two rakʿas of Duha and you have not been commanded with them, and I have been commanded with sacrifice and it has not been made obligatory upon you.""**"
"13979","In a narration from Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, also: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Three are obligations upon me and voluntary for you: witr, sacrifice, and Duha prayer. > **""Three are obligations upon me and voluntary for you: witr, sacrifice, and Duha prayer.""**"
"13980","In a narration: I have been commanded with two rakʿas of Duha and witr, and they have not been prescribed as obligation upon you. > **""I have been commanded with two rakʿas of Duha and witr, and they have not been prescribed as obligation upon you.""**"
"13981","ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Three are obligatory upon me and sunnah for you: witr, siwak, and standing in night prayer. > **""Three are obligatory upon me and sunnah for you: witr, siwak, and standing in night prayer.""**"
"13982","Umm Salama, رضي الله عنها, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿAsr, then entered my house and prayed two rakʿas. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you have prayed a prayer you did not used to pray. He said: Wealth came to me and distracted me from two rakʿas I used to pray after Zuhr, so I prayed them now. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we make them up when we miss them. He said: No. > **""Wealth came to me and distracted me from two rakʿas I used to pray after Zuhr, so I prayed them now.""** > **""No.""**"
"13983","Abu Umama, رضي الله عنه, concerning His saying, as extra devotion for you, Israʾ 79, said: The extra devotion was peculiar to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In another wording concerning, And in the night stand in tahajjud as extra devotion for you: it was supererogatory for the Prophet ﷺ and a merit for you. > **""And in the night stand in tahajjud as extra devotion for you.""**"
"13984","Muʿadha reported: A woman asked ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, while I was present, about the continuous fasting of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so ʿAʾisha said to her: Do you act like his act. His past and future sins have been forgiven, and his deed was supererogatory for him."
"13985","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, said: When food from other than his household was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would ask about it; if it was said it was a gift, he ate, and if it was said it was charity, he said: Eat, and he did not eat. > **""Eat.""**"
"13986","Jabir ibn Samura, رضي الله عنه, reported that when food was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he ate from it, he would send its remainder to Abu Ayyub, رضي الله عنه, and Abu Ayyub would place his fingers where he saw the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ was brought a bowl in which he found the smell of garlic, so he did not taste it and sent it to Abu Ayyub. He looked and saw no trace of the fingers of the Prophet ﷺ, so he did not taste it, then came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not see the trace of your fingers in it. He said: I found in it the smell of garlic. He said: Do you send me what you do not eat. He said: An angel comes to me. > **""I found in it the smell of garlic.""** > **""An angel comes to me.""**"
"13987","ʿImran ibn Husayn al-Dabbi reported that he came to Basra while ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, was governor, and there was a man standing in the shade of the palace saying: Allah and His Messenger have spoken truth, Allah and His Messenger have spoken truth, adding nothing to that. I approached him and said: You have often said, Allah and His Messenger have spoken truth. He said: By Allah, if you wish I shall inform you. I said: Yes. He said: Then sit. He said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ in Madinah from such and such place, while two elders of the tribe had a son who had gone and joined him, so they said: You are going to Madinah, and a son of ours has joined this man, so go to him and seek him from him, and if he refuses except for ransom, then ransom him. I came to Madinah and entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, two elders of the tribe have commanded me to seek a son of theirs with you. He said: Do you know him. He said: I know his lineage. He called the boy and he came, then said: Here he is, so take him to his father. I said: The ransom, O Prophet of Allah. He said: It is not proper for us, the household of Muhammad, to eat the price of anyone from the children of Ismail. Then he struck my shoulder and said: I fear for Quraysh nothing except itself. I said: Why, O Prophet of Allah. He said: If your life is prolonged you will see them here, until you see people among them like sheep between two watering troughs, once here and once there. So now I see people seeking permission from Ibn ʿAbbas, whom I saw this year seeking permission from Muʿawiyah, and I remembered the saying of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Do you know him.""** > **""Here he is, so take him to his father.""** > **""It is not proper for us, the household of Muhammad, to eat the price of anyone from the children of Ismail.""** > **""I fear for Quraysh nothing except itself.""** > **""If your life is prolonged you will see them here, until you see people among them like sheep between two watering troughs, once here and once there.""**"
"13988","ʿAbd Allah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sleep lying on his back until he snored, then rise and pray without performing wudu."
"13989","A man said: I saw the Prophet of Allah ﷺ sleep until he snored, then pray without performing wudu."
"13990","ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, رضي الله عنهما, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not shake hands with women at the pledge."
"13991","Asmaʾ bint Yazid, رضي الله عنها, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I do not shake hands with women. > **""I do not shake hands with women.""**"
"13992","Abu Saʿid, رضي الله عنه, and Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Allah, I take with You a covenant which You will not break, for I am only a man; whichever of the believers I harm or revile, or he said, curse, or flog, then make it for him purification, prayer, and nearness by which You bring him near to You on the Day of Resurrection. > **""O Allah, I take with You a covenant which You will not break, for I am only a man; whichever of the believers I harm or revile or curse or flog, then make it for him purification, prayer, and nearness by which You bring him near to You on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13993","Anas ibn Malik, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted a man to Hafsa bint ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, and said to her: Guard him. Hafsa was heedless and the man left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and said: O Hafsa, what did the man do. She said: I was heedless of him, O Messenger of Allah, and he left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: May Allah sever your hand. She gestured with her hand thus. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and said: What is the matter with you, O Hafsa. She said: O Messenger of Allah, you said such and such about me before. He said: Lower your hand, for I asked my Lord, blessed and exalted, that whomever of my Ummah I supplicate against, He make it forgiveness for him. > **""Guard him.""** > **""O Hafsa, what did the man do.""** > **""May Allah sever your hand.""** > **""What is the matter with you, O Hafsa.""** > **""Lower your hand, for I asked my Lord, blessed and exalted, that whomever of my Ummah I supplicate against, He make it forgiveness for him.""**"
"13994","ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, said: Delegations of the Arabs crowded upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they distressed him, and the Muhajirun stood to relieve him, until he stood at the threshold of ʿAʾisha and they pressed him, so he pulled his cloak from their hands and leapt over the threshold and entered, saying: O Allah, curse them. ʿAʾisha said: O Messenger of Allah, the people are ruined. He said: No, by Allah, O daughter of Abu Bakr, I have stipulated upon my Lord a condition that has no breach. I said: I am only a man, I am constrained by what constrains men, so whichever of the believers a hasty word of mine reaches, make it expiation for him. > **""O Allah, curse them.""** > **""No, by Allah, O daughter of Abu Bakr, I have stipulated upon my Lord a condition that has no breach. I said: I am only a man, I am constrained by what constrains men, so whichever of the believers a hasty word of mine reaches, make it expiation for him.""**"
"13995","Samura ibn Jundub, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say to us: I become angry with you and I excuse you, then I supplicate to Allah between me and Him: O Allah, whomever I have cursed or reviled or been angry with, make it for them blessing, mercy, forgiveness, and prayer, for they are my family and I am a sincere adviser to them. > **""I become angry with you and I excuse you, then I supplicate to Allah between me and Him: O Allah, whomever I have cursed or reviled or been angry with, make it for them blessing, mercy, forgiveness, and prayer, for they are my family and I am a sincere adviser to them.""**"
"13996","Muʿawiyah, رضي الله عنه, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: O Allah, whomever I cursed in Jahiliyyah and he then entered Islam, make that nearness for him to You. > **""O Allah, whomever I cursed in Jahiliyyah and he then entered Islam, make that nearness for him to You.""**"
"13997","Abu al-Tufayl ʿAmir ibn Wathila, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Allah, I am only a man, I become angry as men become angry and I am pleased as men are pleased, so whomever of my Ummah I have cursed, make it for him purification and mercy. > **""O Allah, I am only a man, I become angry as men become angry and I am pleased as men are pleased, so whomever of my Ummah I have cursed, make it for him purification and mercy.""**"
"13998","ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUthman ibn Khuthaym said: I entered upon Abu al-Tufayl ʿAmir ibn Wathila, رضي الله عنه, and found him in good spirits, so I said: O Abu al-Tufayl, inform me about the people whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed. He was about to inform me, when his wife Sawda said: Stop, O Abu al-Tufayl, has it not reached you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Allah, I am only a man, so whichever servant among the believers I supplicate against, make it for him purification and mercy. > **""O Allah, I am only a man, so whichever servant among the believers I supplicate against, make it for him purification and mercy.""**"
"13999","Jabir, رضي الله عنه, said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the market, a woman seized the bridle of his mount while he was upon a donkey and said: O Messenger of Allah, my husband does not approach me, so separate me from him. Her husband passed, so the Prophet ﷺ called him and said: What is the matter with you and her. She came complaining of you truthfully, complaining that you do not approach her. He said: O Messenger of Allah, by the One who honoured you, my last intimacy with her was this night. The woman wept and said: He lies, separate me from him, for he is among the most hateful of Allah's creation to me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled, then took his head and her head and joined them and said: O Allah, bring each one of them close to his companion. Jabir said: We remained as long as Allah willed, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed through the market and there was a woman carrying leather; when she saw him she threw the leather and came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with truth, no human is more beloved to me than him except you. > **""What is the matter with you and her. She came complaining of you truthfully, complaining that you do not approach her.""** > **""O Allah, bring each one of them close to his companion.""**"
"14000","Hudhayfah, رضي الله عنه, reported that when the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for a man, it reached him, his child, and his grandchild."
"14001","In a narration from Hudhayfah, رضي الله عنه, also: The prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reaches a man, his child, and his grandchild."
"14002","Anas, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in a circle and intended to rise, so a boy stood and handed him his sandals. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Did you seek the pleasure of your Lord. May Allah be pleased with you. That boy then attained distinction in Madinah until he was martyred. > **""Did you seek the pleasure of your Lord. May Allah be pleased with you.""**"
"14003","Anas, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ one day said to a boy of the Ansar: Hand me my sandals. The boy said: O Prophet of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, leave me until I place them upon your feet myself. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Allah, this servant of Yours seeks Your pleasure, so be pleased with him. > **""Hand me my sandals.""** > **""O Allah, this servant of Yours seeks Your pleasure, so be pleased with him.""**"
"14004","Dahr al-Aslami, رضي الله عنه, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say on his march to Khaybar to ʿAmir ibn al-Akwaʿ, رضي الله عنه, the hadith, until he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: May Allah have mercy on him. ʿUmar, رضي الله عنه, said: It is assured, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, if only you had let us benefit by him. He was then killed as a martyr on the day of Khaybar. > **""May Allah have mercy on him.""**"
"14005","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: I have been favoured over the prophets with six, which none before me was given: my past and future sins were forgiven, spoils were made lawful for me though not lawful for anyone before me, my Ummah was made the best of nations, the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, I was given al-Kawthar, I was aided by dread, and by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, your companion is the bearer of the banner of praise on the Day of Resurrection, beneath it Adam and those below him. > **""I have been favoured over the prophets with six, which none before me was given: my past and future sins were forgiven, spoils were made lawful for me though not lawful for anyone before me, my Ummah was made the best of nations, the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, I was given al-Kawthar, I was aided by dread, and by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, your companion is the bearer of the banner of praise on the Day of Resurrection, beneath it Adam and those below him.""**"
"14006","Abu Saʿid, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I have been given five which no prophet before me was given: I was sent to the red and the black, while a prophet would be sent only to his people; I was aided by dread over a month's journey; spoils were granted to me though none before me consumed them; the earth was made for me purification and mosque; and every prophet was given a supplication which he hastened, while I deferred my supplication as intercession for my Ummah, and it will reach, if Allah wills, whoever dies not associating anything with Allah. > **""I have been given five which no prophet before me was given: I was sent to the red and the black, while a prophet would be sent only to his people; I was aided by dread over a month's journey; spoils were granted to me though none before me consumed them; the earth was made for me purification and mosque; and every prophet was given a supplication which he hastened, while I deferred my supplication as intercession for my Ummah, and it will reach, if Allah wills, whoever dies not associating anything with Allah.""**"
"14007","ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, رضي الله عنهما, reported from the Prophet ﷺ: I have been given the strength of forty in grip and intimacy. > **""I have been given the strength of forty in grip and intimacy.""**"
"14008","Anas, رضي الله عنه, said: I have been favoured over people with four: generosity, courage, abundant intimacy, and strength of grip. > **""I have been favoured over people with four: generosity, courage, abundant intimacy, and strength of grip.""**"
"14009","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I have been favoured over the prophets with two traits: my satan was a disbeliever, so Allah aided me against him until he submitted. The narrator said: I forgot the other trait. > **""I have been favoured over the prophets with two traits: my satan was a disbeliever, so Allah aided me against him until he submitted.""**"
"14010","ʿAbd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, رضي الله عنهما, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was being cupped, and when he finished he said: O ʿAbd Allah, take this blood and pour it where no one sees it. When I went out of sight of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I took the blood and drank it. When I returned to the Prophet ﷺ, he said: What did you do, O ʿAbd Allah. He said: I placed it where I thought it hidden from people. He said: Perhaps you drank it. He said: Yes. He said: Who commanded you to drink blood. Woe to you from people and woe to people from you. > **""O ʿAbd Allah, take this blood and pour it where no one sees it.""** > **""What did you do, O ʿAbd Allah.""** > **""Perhaps you drank it.""** > **""Who commanded you to drink blood. Woe to you from people and woe to people from you.""**"
"14011","Safina, رضي الله عنه, said: The Prophet ﷺ was cupped, then said: Take this blood and bury it away from beasts, birds, and people. I went aside and drank it, then mentioned that to him, and he laughed. > **""Take this blood and bury it away from beasts, birds, and people.""**"
"14012","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, رضي الله عنه, reported that his father Malik ibn Sinan, رضي الله عنه, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was wounded in his face on the day of Uhud, sucked the blood of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and swallowed it. It was said to him: Do you drink blood. He said: Yes, I drink the blood of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: His blood has mixed with mine; the Fire shall not touch him. > **""His blood has mixed with mine; the Fire shall not touch him.""**"
"14013","Salma, wife of Abu Rafiʿ, رضي الله عنها, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting above his house and said: O Salma, bring me washing water. I brought him a vessel with lote leaves, clarified it for him, then he knelt upon a cushion stuffed with fibre while I poured over his head and he washed it, and I saw every drop falling from his head into the vessel as though shining pearls. Then I brought him water and he washed. When he finished washing he said: O Salma, pour what is in the vessel where none shall step over it. I took the vessel and drank some of it, then poured the rest upon the earth. He said to me: What did you do with what was in the vessel. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I envied the earth for it, so I drank some and poured the rest upon the earth. He said: Go, Allah has forbidden your body to the Fire. > **""O Salma, bring me washing water.""** > **""O Salma, pour what is in the vessel where none shall step over it.""** > **""What did you do with what was in the vessel.""** > **""Go, Allah has forbidden your body to the Fire.""**"
"14014","Hakima bint Umayma, رضي الله عنها, reported from her mother: The Prophet ﷺ had a wooden cup in which he urinated and placed beneath his bed. He rose and sought it but did not find it, so he asked: Where is the cup. They said: Barra, the servant of Umm Salama who came with her from Abyssinia, drank it. The Prophet ﷺ said: She has secured a strong barrier against the Fire. > **""Where is the cup.""** > **""She has secured a strong barrier against the Fire.""**"
"14015","Umm Ayman, رضي الله عنها, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose in the night to an earthen vessel in the side of the house and urinated in it. I rose in the night thirsty and drank what was in it without knowing. In the morning the Prophet ﷺ said: O Umm Ayman, rise and pour out what is in that vessel. She said: By Allah, I have drunk what was in it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until his molars appeared, then said: You shall never suffer belly pain. > **""O Umm Ayman, rise and pour out what is in that vessel.""** > **""You shall never suffer belly pain.""**"
"14016","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Mirdas al-Sulami, رضي الله عنه, said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ when he called for purification water, dipped his hand and performed wudu, so we pursued it and drank it. The Prophet ﷺ said: What carried you to what you did. We said: Love of Allah and His Messenger. He said: If you love that Allah and His Messenger love you, then fulfil when entrusted, speak truthfully when you speak, and do good to the neighbour beside you. > **""What carried you to what you did.""** > **""If you love that Allah and His Messenger love you, then fulfil when entrusted, speak truthfully when you speak, and do good to the neighbour beside you.""**"
"14017","ʿAwn ibn ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUtba reported from his father: The Prophet ﷺ did not die until he read and wrote, with a note that this hadith is munkar and opposed to the Book of Allah, and its meaning is thought to be that ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUtba had read and written before the Prophet ﷺ passed, meaning he had attained understanding in his lifetime."
"14018","ʿAbd Allah ibn Masʿud, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: My description is Ahmad the trusting, neither harsh nor severe; he requites good with good and does not return evil for evil; his birthplace Makkah and his migration Madinah; his Ummah the praising ones, girding to their mid-calves, washing their limbs; I am their light in their breasts; they line for prayer as they line for battle; their offering by which they seek nearness to Me is their blood; monks by night, lions by day. > **""My description is Ahmad the trusting, neither harsh nor severe; he requites good with good and does not return evil for evil; his birthplace Makkah and his migration Madinah; his Ummah the praising ones, girding to their mid-calves, washing their limbs; I am their light in their breasts; they line for prayer as they line for battle; their offering by which they seek nearness to Me is their blood; monks by night, lions by day.""**"
"14019","Yazid al-Farisi said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ in a dream in the time of Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, while Yazid wrote copies of the mushaf. I said to Ibn ʿAbbas: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a dream. Ibn ʿAbbas said: The Prophet ﷺ used to say: Satan cannot take my form, so whoever sees me in a dream has truly seen me. Then he said: Can you describe to us this man whom you saw. He said: Yes. I saw a man of medium stature, his body and flesh wheatish toward whiteness, handsome of smile, as though kohl upon his eyes, beautiful in the roundness of his face, his beard filling from here to here until it nearly filled his chest. Ibn ʿAbbas said: Had you seen him awake, you could not describe him beyond this. > **""Satan cannot take my form, so whoever sees me in a dream has truly seen me.""**"
"14020","Yusuf ibn Mazin reported that a man asked ʿAli, رضي الله عنه: O Commander of the Believers, describe for us the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: He was not tall to excess, above the medium when he came with people he overlooked them, intensely white and luminous, large of head, fair and bright, long of eyelashes, thick of hands and feet, when he walked he lifted firmly as though descending a slope, sweat upon his face like pearls; I never saw before him nor after him his like, may my father and mother be ransom for him ﷺ."
"14021","In a further addition: fine straight hair, handsome of hair."
"14022","In a narration of it: large of eyes."
"14023","Anas, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was wheatish in complexion."
"14024","ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, illustrated with this verse while Abu Bakr, رضي الله عنه, listened: And white one by whose face the clouds seek rain, guardian of orphans, protector of widows. Abu Bakr said: That is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""That is the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"14025","A man of al-ʿAdawiyyah said: My grandfather told me: I set out to Madinah and alighted in this valley, and there were two men with a single goat between them, and the buyer said to the seller: Deal with me fairly. I said to myself: Is this the Hashimi who misled people. I looked, and there was a man handsome of body, great of forehead, fine of nose, fine of eyebrows, and from the pit of his chest to his navel like a black thread of black hair. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"14026","Al-Hasan ibn ʿAli, رضي الله عنهما, said: I asked my maternal uncle Hind ibn Abi Hala al-Tamimi, رضي الله عنه, who was descriptive, about the description of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, desiring that he describe something I could hold to. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was dignified and honoured, his face shone like the moon on the night of fullness, taller than the medium and shorter than the lanky, great of head, wavy of hair, parting it when parted, his hair not passing the lobes of his ears when full, radiant of colour, wide of forehead, arched of eyebrows, full without junction, with a vein between them stirred by anger, aquiline of nose with light upon it, full of beard, smooth of cheeks, wide of mouth, bright of teeth with gaps, fine line of hair upon the chest, his neck like a silver ewer in purity, proportioned of build, fleshy yet firm, belly and chest level, broad of chest, wide between shoulders, large of joints, luminous of bare skin, connected by hair from throat to navel like a line, bare of chest and belly besides that, hairy of forearms, shoulders, and upper chest, long of forearms, wide of palm, full of hands and feet, even of limbs, hollow of soles, smooth of feet so water runs off them, when he moved he moved vigorously, stepping humbly, walking gently, far-reaching in stride as though descending a slope, turning wholly when he turned, lowering his gaze, his look to the earth longer than to the sky, mostly observing, leading his Companions, greeting first whom he met. I said: Describe his speech to me. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ever sorrowful, ever reflective, never at rest, long of silence, beginning and ending speech with full mouth, فصل of speech, neither excess nor shortcoming, soft yet not weak, honouring blessing though small, never blaming food, neither praising nor dispraising, angered for truth beyond resistance, gesturing with his whole hand, turning his whole palm in wonder, joining thumb to forefinger in speech, striking left palm with right thumb in anger, turning away and excusing when addressed with dislike, never speaking except in hope of reward, gathering his Companions and dividing among them, visiting, mild of nature, neither harsh nor rough, neither clamorous nor obscene, nor fault-finding nor miserly, turning from what he disliked, despairing of it, his Companions silent before him, speaking when he was silent, never disputing before him, laughing at what they laughed at, wondering at what they wondered at, answering the stranger though harsh, forbearing the lapse of the Bedouin, saying: Bring me one in need, never accepting praise except for good, never interrupting, exceeding in silence over speech, his silence upon four: forbearance, caution, estimation, and reflection."
"14027","Umm Salama, رضي الله عنها, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry, his face turned red."
"14028","Ibn Masʿud, رضي الله عنه, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry, his cheeks turned red."
"14029","Hakim ibn Hizam, رضي الله عنه, said: I went to Yaman and bought the cloak of Dhu Yazan, then gifted it to the Prophet ﷺ during the truce between him and Quraysh, but he said: I do not accept the gift of a polytheist, so he returned it. I sold it, then he bought it and wore it, then went out to his Companions wearing it; I never saw anything upon anything more beautiful than him in it ﷺ. Soon I said: How can judges look for فصل after what has appeared clear of splendour and adornment; when they compare glory to him, he exceeds them like one drawing abundant well water over scanty. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard them, smiled, then entered. > **""I do not accept the gift of a polytheist.""**"
"14030","Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Salit reported from his father from his grandfather: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out on the Hijrah with Abu Bakr, رضي الله عنه, ʿAmir ibn Fuhayra, freedman of Abu Bakr, and Ibn Urayqit guiding them, he passed by Umm Maʿbad al-Khuzaʿiyyah, رضي الله عنها, who did not know him. He said to her: O Umm Maʿbad, have you any milk. She said: By Allah, the sheep are far away. He said: What is this sheep I see in the corner of the tent. She said: A sheep left behind by weakness. He said: Do you permit milking it. She said: By Allah, no ram has ever touched it, yet do with it as you will. He wiped its back and udder, called for a vessel sufficing a group, milked therein and filled it, gave his Companions to drink repeatedly, then milked again and left it with her, and departed. When her husband came in the evening he said: O Umm Maʿbad, what is this milk when there is no milch animal at home and the sheep are away. She said: By Allah, a man of radiant fairness and handsome face passed us, long of eyelashes, intense black and white of eyes, resonant of voice, a branch between two branches, neither disfigured by tallness nor despised by shortness, no paunch, no gauntness, his neck as a silver ewer, when you looked at him majesty overcame, when silent dignity covered him, his speech like ordered beads, most beautiful of his Companions in aspect, finest of face, surrounded yet not importuned, with Companions crowding about him, hastening to his command when he commanded, stopping at his prohibition when he forbade. He said: This is the companion of Quraysh; had I seen him I would have followed him. > **""O Umm Maʿbad, have you any milk.""** > **""What is this sheep I see in the corner of the tent.""** > **""Do you permit milking it.""**"
"14031","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke, light was seen issuing from between his front teeth."
"14032","Abu Qirsafa, رضي الله عنه, said: When we pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I, my mother, and my aunt, and returned from him, my mother and aunt said to me: O son, we never saw the like of this man, more handsome of face, cleaner of garment, softer of speech, and we saw as though light issued from his mouth."
"14033","Jubayr, meaning Ibn Mutʿim, رضي الله عنه, reported from the Prophet ﷺ: He turned to us with his face like a piece of the moon."
"14034","Abu ʿUbayda ibn Muhammad ibn ʿAmmar ibn Yasir said: I said to al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfraʾ, رضي الله عنها: Describe for me the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: Had you seen him, you would have seen the sun rising."
"14035","Abu al-Tufayl, رضي الله عنه, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of the conquest of Makkah, and I never forget the whiteness of his face with the intense blackness of his hair; among men some were taller than him and some shorter; he walked while they walked around him. I said to my mother: Who is this. She said: This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"14036","Umm Haniʾ, رضي الله عنها, said: I never looked at the belly of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except that I recalled folded sheets, one upon another."
"14037","Jabir ibn Samura, رضي الله عنه, said: The finger of the Prophet ﷺ was joined."
"14038","Maymuna bint Kardam, رضي الله عنها, said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ, and his toe next to the big toe had excess in length over the big toe, meaning of the foot."
"14039","Saʿid ibn al-Musayyab heard Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, describing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: He was a man of medium stature, closer to tallness, intense of whiteness, black of beard, fine of hair, long of eyelashes, wide between the shoulders, stepping with both feet together without hollow of sole, advancing wholly and retreating wholly; I never saw his like before him nor after him."
"14040","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, رضي الله عنه, was asked about the seal of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between his shoulders, so he gestured with his forefinger thus: flesh protruding between his shoulders ﷺ."
"14041","Abu Zayd, meaning ʿAmr ibn Akhtab, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: O Abu Zayd, come near me and wipe my back. He uncovered his back, so I wiped his back, placing the seal between my fingers. It was said: What is the seal. He said: Clustered hair. In a narration: I saw the seal upon the back of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his back as though a seal. > **""O Abu Zayd, come near me and wipe my back.""**"
"14042","ʿAbbad ibn ʿAmr, رضي الله عنه, who served the Prophet ﷺ, reported that a Jew addressed him and his cloak fell from his shoulders, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked that the seal be seen, so I straightened it upon him. He said: Who did this. I said: I. He said: Come toward me. I sat before him, he placed his hand upon my head and passed it over my face and chest, and said: When anything comes to us, come to me. I came to him and he ordered for me a jadhaʿa, a young camel. The seal was upon the edge of his left shoulder like the knee of a goat. > **""Who did this.""** > **""Come toward me.""** > **""When anything comes to us, come to me.""**"
"14043","Jabir ibn Samura, رضي الله عنه, said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ from behind to look at the place of the seal. When he looked at me, he cast aside the cloak and I looked at it."
"14044","Anas ibn Malik, رضي الله عنه, said: The Prophet ﷺ had four braids in his head."
"14045","From him, meaning Anas, رضي الله عنه: The Prophet ﷺ had a large curly jumma of hair."
"14046","Fadala ibn ʿUbayd, رضي الله عنه, reported that he entered upon ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, and she brought out for him some hairs of the Prophet ﷺ, and they were reddish and dyed."
"14047","Jahdam ibn al-Dahhak said: I passed by al-Raji and saw an old man there. They said: This is al-ʿAddaʾ ibn Khalid ibn Hawdha. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I said: Describe him to me. He said: He had a handsome sabala, and the Arabs used to call the beard sabala."
"14048","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when he walked, walked with gathered strength, and there was no laziness in him. In an addition: He did not turn aside, and it was known from his walking that he was neither lazy nor weak."
"14049","Abu ʿInaba, رضي الله عنه, said: When the Prophet ﷺ walked, he walked with a gait as if tearing rock."
"14050","Shaddad, رضي الله عنه, said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and took his hand, and it was softer than silk and colder than snow."
"14051","Burayda, رضي الله عنه, said: The Prophet ﷺ would most often only laugh until his front molars were seen."
"14052","Anas, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to visit Umm Sulaym, رضي الله عنها, and sleep upon her bedding, and his sleep was heavy. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"14053","Anas, رضي الله عنه, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed through a road of the roads of Madina, the scent of musk was found from him, and it was said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed through this road. In a wording: We recognized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by the sweetness of his scent when he came toward us."
"14054","Muʿadh, meaning Ibn Jabal, رضي الله عنه, said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey and he seated me behind him. I never touched anything softer than the skin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, nor did I find any scent sweeter than the scent of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"14055","Umm ʿAsim, wife of Farqad ibn ʿUtba, said: We were four wives with ʿUtba. Every one of us strove in perfume to be sweeter than her companion, while ʿUtba touched no perfume except oil with which he wiped his beard, yet his scent was sweeter than ours. When he went out to the people, they said: We never smelled a scent sweeter than the scent of ʿUtba. I said to him one day: We strive hard in perfume, yet you are sweeter-scented than us; from what is that. He said: A skin eruption seized me in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I complained of it to him. He ordered me to remove my outer garments, so I removed them and sat before him, placing my garment over my private part. He breathed into his hand, then placed his hand upon my back and my belly, and this fragrance has clung to me since that day. In a wording: Then he spread his hands and spat into them, rubbed one over the other, wiped one upon my belly and the other upon my back."
"14056","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have married off my daughter and I wish you would help me with something. He said: I have nothing with me, but tomorrow come with a wide-mouthed bottle and a stick from a tree, and the sign between me and you is that I close the side of the door. So he brought a wide-mouthed bottle and a stick, and the Prophet ﷺ began to wipe sweat from his forearms until it was full. He said: Take it, and tell your daughter that when she wishes to perfume herself, she should dip this stick into the bottle and perfume herself with it. He said: When she perfumed herself, the people of Madina smelled the scent of perfume, and they were named the house of the perfumed ones. > **""I have nothing with me, but tomorrow come with a wide-mouthed bottle and a stick from a tree, and the sign between me and you is that I close the side of the door.""** > **""Take it, and tell your daughter that when she wishes to perfume herself, she should dip this stick into the bottle and perfume herself with it.""**"
"14057","Yazid ibn al-Aswad al-Suwaʾi, رضي الله عنه, said: I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and prayed Fajr with him at Mina. When he finished his prayer, two men were behind the people who had not prayed with the people. He said: Bring me the two men. They were brought with their limbs trembling. He said: Did you not pray with us. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we were in our lodgings and thought we would not reach the prayer. He said: Do not do so; when you have prayed in your lodgings and then reach the prayer, pray, and it will be voluntary for you. One of them said: Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: O Allah, forgive him. The people crowded upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I was that day among the strongest of men, so I pressed through until I took the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and placed it upon my chest, and I never saw anything cooler nor sweeter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Bring me the two men.""** > **""Did you not pray with us.""** > **""Do not do so; when you have prayed in your lodgings and then reach the prayer, pray, and it will be voluntary for you.""** > **""O Allah, forgive him.""**"
"14058","Jaʿfar ibn Mahmud ibn Maslama reported that his grandmother ʿUmayra bint Masʿud, رضي الله عنها, told him that she entered upon the Prophet ﷺ with her sisters to pledge allegiance, and they were five. They found him eating dried meat. He chewed a piece of dried meat for them, then handed me the piece, and each one chewed a portion, and they met Allah with no foulness ever found in their mouths."
"14059","Yahya ibn al-Jazzar said: A group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Umm Salama, رضي الله عنها, and said: O Mother of the Believers, tell us of the private conduct of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: His private and public conduct were alike. Then she regretted it and said: I have disclosed the secret of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: When he entered, I informed him, and he said: You did well. > **""You did well.""**"
"14060","Hudhayfa, رضي الله عنه, said: While I was walking on a road of Madina, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came, and I heard him say: I am Muhammad and Ahmad and the Prophet of mercy and the Prophet of repentance and the Gatherer and the Follower and the Prophet of fierce battles. > **""I am Muhammad and Ahmad and the Prophet of mercy and the Prophet of repentance and the Gatherer and the Follower and the Prophet of fierce battles.""**"
"14061","Jabir ibn ʿAbd Allah, رضي الله عنهما, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I am Ahmad and I am Muhammad and I am the Gatherer before whom the people are gathered, and I am the Effacer through whom Allah effaces disbelief. When the Day of Resurrection comes, the banner of praise will be with me, and I will be the leader of the messengers and the owner of their intercession. > **""I am Ahmad and I am Muhammad and I am the Gatherer before whom the people are gathered, and I am the Effacer through whom Allah effaces disbelief. When the Day of Resurrection comes, the banner of praise will be with me, and I will be the leader of the messengers and the owner of their intercession.""**"
"14062","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported from the Prophet ﷺ: I am Ahmad and Muhammad and the Gatherer and the Follower and the Seal. > **""I am Ahmad and Muhammad and the Gatherer and the Follower and the Seal.""**"
"14063","Muhammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr said: ʿUmayr ibn Wahb al-Jumahi and Safwan ibn Umayya sat in al-Hijr a little after the calamity of the people of Badr from Quraysh. ʿUmayr ibn Wahb was a devil among the devils of Quraysh, among those who harmed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions, who met hardship from his harm at Makkah, and the son of Wahb ibn ʿUmayr was among the captives of Badr. He said: They mentioned the people of the well by their calamity, and one said: By Allah, there is no good in life after them. ʿUmayr ibn Wahb said: You spoke truly; by Allah, were it not for a debt upon me which I cannot repay, and my dependants whom I fear will be lost after me, I would ride to Muhammad and kill him, for I have a pretext among them, my son a captive in their hands. Safwan seized upon it and said: Your debt is upon me, I will repay it for you, and your dependants are with my dependants, I will treat them equally as long as they remain, never failing them. ʿUmayr said: Conceal my affair and yours. He said: I will. Then ʿUmayr ordered his sword to be sharpened and poisoned, then set out for Madina. Meanwhile ʿUmar, رضي الله عنه, was at Madina among a group of Muslims recalling the day of Badr and what Allah had honored them with and shown them of their enemy, when he saw ʿUmayr ibn Wahb halting at the door of the mosque with his sword slung. He said: This dog, by Allah, ʿUmayr ibn Wahb, has come only for evil; it is he who stirred trouble between us and incited the people against us on the day of Badr. Then ʿUmar entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is ʿUmayr ibn Wahb come with sword slung. He said: Admit him. ʿUmar came and seized the strap of his sword at his neck and gripped him with it, and ʿUmar said to some Ansari men with him: Enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sit with him, and beware of this dog, for he is not trustworthy. Then he entered with him upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ʿUmar holding his sword strap, and he said: Release him, O ʿUmar; come near, O ʿUmayr. He came near and said: Good morning. That was the greeting of the people of ignorance among themselves. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Allah has honored us with a greeting better than yours, O ʿUmayr: peace, the greeting of the people of Paradise. He said: By Allah, O Muhammad, my acquaintance with it is recent. He said: What has brought you. He said: I came for this captive in your hands. He said: Then why is the sword upon your neck. He said: May Allah render such swords ugly; did they avail us anything. He said: Tell me truthfully, for what did you come. He said: I came only for this. He said: Rather, you and Safwan ibn Umayya sat in al-Hijr and recalled the people of the well from Quraysh, and you said: Were it not for a debt upon me and my dependants, I would go out and kill Muhammad. So Safwan undertook your debt and dependants on condition that you kill me, but Allah stands between you and that. ʿUmayr said: I testify that you are the Messenger of Allah. We used to deny you, O Messenger of Allah, in what you brought of tidings of heaven and what descended upon you of revelation, and this is a matter attended by none but I and Safwan. By Allah, I know none informed you of it but Allah. Praise be to Allah who guided me to Islam and drove me upon this course. Then he bore the true testimony. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Instruct your brother in his religion, teach him the Qurʾan, and release his captive for him. Then he said: O Messenger of Allah, I strove to extinguish the light of Allah and severely harmed those upon the religion of Allah, and I wish you would permit me to go to Makkah and call them to Allah and Islam, perhaps Allah will guide them, and I will not harm them as I harmed your Companions in their religion. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted him, and he joined Makkah. When ʿUmayr ibn Wahb set out, Safwan said to Quraysh: Rejoice in an event that will make you forget the event of Badr. Safwan asked the riders about him until a rider came and informed him of his Islam, so he swore never to speak to him nor ever benefit him. When ʿUmayr reached Makkah, he remained calling to Islam and severely harming whoever opposed him, and many people embraced Islam at his hands. > **""Admit him.""** > **""Release him, O ʿUmar; come near, O ʿUmayr.""** > **""Allah has honored us with a greeting better than yours, O ʿUmayr: peace, the greeting of the people of Paradise.""** > **""What has brought you.""** > **""Then why is the sword upon your neck.""** > **""Tell me truthfully, for what did you come.""** > **""Rather, you and Safwan ibn Umayya sat in al-Hijr and recalled the people of the well from Quraysh, and you said: Were it not for a debt upon me and my dependants, I would go out and kill Muhammad. So Safwan undertook your debt and dependants on condition that you kill me, but Allah stands between you and that.""** > **""Instruct your brother in his religion, teach him the Qurʾan, and release his captive for him.""**"
"14064","Similar to it was reported from ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr as a mursal report, in which: The Muslims rejoiced when Allah guided him, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, رضي الله عنه, said: A pig was more beloved to me than him when he appeared, and today he is more beloved to me than some of my own children."
"14065","Abu ʿImran al-Jawni, not knowing it except from Anas, رضي الله عنه, said: Wahb ibn ʿUmayr had attended Uhud as a disbeliever and was wounded and lay among the slain. A man of the Ansar passed him and recognized him, placed his sword in his belly until it came out of his back, then left him. When night came and cold seized him, he joined Makkah and recovered. He and Safwan ibn Umayya gathered in al-Hijr, and he said to Safwan ibn Umayya: Were it not for my dependants and a debt upon me, I would wish to be the one to kill Muhammad myself. Safwan said: Then how would you do it. He said: I am a swift rider none can overtake; I would come to him and deceive him, then strike him with the sword, then reach the mountain where none could overtake me. Safwan said to him: Then your dependants and your debt are upon me. He went out, sharpened his sword and poisoned it, then set out for Madina intending only the killing of Muhammad ﷺ. When he reached Madina, ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, رضي الله عنه, saw him, and it alarmed him and weighed upon him, and he said to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: I have seen Wahb come, and his coming makes me suspicious; he is a treacherous man, so surround your Prophet ﷺ. The Muslims surrounded the Prophet ﷺ. Wahb came and stood before the Prophet ﷺ and said: Good morning, O Muhammad. He said: Allah has replaced it for us with better. He said: My acquaintance with you is that you spoke it while impressed. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: What has brought you. He said: I came to ransom your captives. He said: What about the sword. He said: We carried such on the day of Badr, but they brought us neither success nor triumph. He said: Then what is the thing you said to Safwan while you were in al-Hijr: Were it not for my dependants and my debt, I would be the one to kill Muhammad myself. The Prophet ﷺ informed him of the report, so Wahb said: What, how did you say. He repeated it to him. Wahb said: You used to inform us of reports of the people of the earth and we denied you, and now I see you inform the reports of the people of heaven. I testify that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: O Messenger of Allah, give me your turban. The Prophet ﷺ gave him his turban, then he returned toward Makkah. ʿUmar said: He came when he was more hateful to me than a pig, then he returned while he is more beloved to me than my own child. > **""Allah has replaced it for us with better.""** > **""What has brought you.""** > **""What about the sword.""** > **""Then what is the thing you said to Safwan while you were in al-Hijr: Were it not for my dependants and my debt, I would be the one to kill Muhammad myself.""**"
"14066","Aban ibn Salman, from his father Salman, رضي الله عنه, said: The Islam of Qubath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi came about when men of the Arabs and others came to him and said: Muhammad ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib has come out calling to other than our religion. Qubath rose until he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he entered upon him, he said to him: Sit, O Qubath. He fell silent in displeasure. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: You are the one who said: If all the women of Quraysh went out, they would turn back Muhammad and his Companions. Qubath said: By Him who sent you with the truth, my tongue did not move with it, my lips did not murmur it, none heard it from me; it was only something that occurred inwardly to myself. I testify that there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and I testify that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and that what you brought is the truth. > **""Sit, O Qubath.""** > **""You are the one who said: If all the women of Quraysh went out, they would turn back Muhammad and his Companions.""**"
"14067","ʿUmar, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has raised the world for me, so I look at it and at what will occur in it until the Day of Resurrection, as if I look at this palm of mine; a clarity which Allah granted His Prophet ﷺ as He granted it to the prophets before him. > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has raised the world for me, so I look at it and at what will occur in it until the Day of Resurrection, as if I look at this palm of mine; a clarity which Allah granted His Prophet as He granted it to the prophets before him.""**"
"14068","Abu Bakra, رضي الله عنه, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sent, Kisra sent to his governor over Yemen and those under him of the Arabs, called Badham, that it reached me that a man has appeared in your direction claiming he is a prophet, so tell him to desist or I shall send to him one who will kill him or kill his people. He said: The messenger of Badham came to the Prophet ﷺ and said this to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Had it been something I did of my own, I would have desisted, but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent me. The messenger stayed with him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: My Lord has killed Kisra, and there shall be no Kisra after today, and He has killed Qaysar, and there shall be no Qaysar after today. He said: He wrote down the saying in the hour and day and month in which he was told, then returned to Badham, and Kisra had died and Qaysar had been killed. > **""Had it been something I did of my own, I would have desisted, but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent me.""** > **""My Lord has killed Kisra, and there shall be no Kisra after today, and He has killed Qaysar, and there shall be no Qaysar after today.""**"
"14069","Khuraym ibn Aws, رضي الله عنه, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: This white Hira has been raised for me, and this is al-Shammaʾ bint Buqayla al-Azdiyya upon a white mule wrapped in a black veil. I said: O Messenger of Allah, if we enter Hira and I find her in this condition, is she mine. He said: She is yours. Then the Arabs apostatized, but none of Tayyiʾ apostatized. We fought Qays upon Islam, among them ʿUtba ibn Hisn, and we fought Tulayha ibn Khuwaylid al-Faqʿasi. Khalid ibn al-Walid praised us, and among what he said of us were verses: May Allah reward Tayyiʾ in their abodes at the encounter of heroes with the best reward; they are the people of banners of liberality and bounty when the east wind overturns every tent; they struck Qays upon religion after they had answered a caller of darkness and blindness. Then Khalid ibn al-Walid marched toward Musaylima, and we marched with him. When we finished with Musaylima and his companions, we advanced toward Basra and met Hurmuz at Kazima in a great host, and none was more hostile to the Arabs than Hurmuz. Ibn al-Walid came forth to him and challenged to single combat; Hurmuz came forth, and Khalid, رضي الله عنه, killed him and wrote of it to Abu Bakr, رضي الله عنه, who granted him his spoils. The cap of Hurmuz reached one hundred thousand dirhams, for when a man rose to eminence among the Persians, they made his cap one hundred thousand dirhams. Then we traveled until we entered Hira, and the first to meet us there was Shaymaʾ bint Buqayla upon her white mule with a black veil, as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. I clung to her and said: This one the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted me. Khalid summoned me for proof upon her, so I brought it to him, and he surrendered her to me. Her brother ʿAbd al-Masih came down to us and said to me: Sell her to me. I said: By Allah, I shall not reduce her below one thousand by anything. He paid me one thousand dirhams. It was said to me: Had you said one hundred thousand, he would have paid you. I said: I did not think any wealth greater than one thousand existed. It reached me in other than this hadith that the two witnesses were Muhammad ibn Maslama and ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar. > **""This white Hira has been raised for me, and this is al-Shammaʾ bint Buqayla al-Azdiyya upon a white mule wrapped in a black veil.""** > **""She is yours.""**"
"14070","ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها, said: There was a day of the year in which the wives of the Prophet ﷺ gathered with him for a day until night. She said: On that day he said: The quickest of you to join me is the longest-handed among you. She said: We began to measure our arms among ourselves which of us was longest-handed. She said: Sawda was the longest-handed among them. When Sawda passed away, we knew she had been the longest-handed among them in good and charity. She said: Zaynab used to spin yarn and give it to the expeditions of the Prophet ﷺ, who sewed with it and sought help with it in their campaigns. She said: On that day he said: What will be the state of one of you when the dogs of Hawʾab bark upon her. > **""The quickest of you to join me is the longest-handed among you.""** > **""What will be the state of one of you when the dogs of Hawʾab bark upon her.""**"
"14071","Umm Salama, رضي الله عنها, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took me in marriage, he said: O Umm Salama, I gifted musk and a robe to Najashi, and I do not see him but dead, nor my gift but that it shall be returned to me. She said: It was as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. He gave each of his wives an ounce and gave me the rest of the musk and the robe. > **""O Umm Salama, I gifted musk and a robe to Najashi, and I do not see him but dead, nor my gift but that it shall be returned to me.""**"
"14072","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Kisra shall perish, and there shall be no Kisra after him, for he says: I am king of kings. And Qaysar shall perish, and there shall be no Qaysar after him, for he says: I am king of kings. > **""Kisra shall perish, and there shall be no Kisra after him, for he says: I am king of kings. And Qaysar shall perish, and there shall be no Qaysar after him, for he says: I am king of kings.""**"
"14073","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When Kisra perishes, there shall be no Kisra after him, and when Qaysar perishes, there shall be no Qaysar after him. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, their treasures shall surely be spent in the path of Allah. > **""When Kisra perishes, there shall be no Kisra after him, and when Qaysar perishes, there shall be no Qaysar after him. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, their treasures shall surely be spent in the path of Allah.""**"
"14074","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us while a cloud shaded us which we hoped for rain, and he said: The angel who drives it, or who drives this cloud, entered upon me and greeted me, and informed me that he drives it to such-and-such valley. > **""The angel who drives this cloud entered upon me and greeted me, and informed me that he drives it to such-and-such valley.""**"
"14075","Rafiʿ, رضي الله عنه, said: Rahhal ibn Ghanmuya showed such humility, devotion to recitation of the Qurʾan, and goodness in the sight of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as was wondrous. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day while Rahhal sat with us among a group, and he said: One of this group is in the Fire. Rafiʿ said: I looked among the people, and there were Abu Hurayrah al-Dawsi, Abu Arwa al-Dawsi, al-Tufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsi, and Rahhal ibn Ghanmuya. I kept looking in wonder, saying: Who is this wretched one. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Banu Hanifa apostatized. I asked: What became of Rahhal ibn Ghanmuya. They said: He was tempted; he is the one who testified for Musaylima against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he made him partner in the affair after him. I said: What the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said is true. Rahhal was heard saying: Two rams butted, and the dearer to us is our ram. > **""One of this group is in the Fire.""**"
"14076","Aws ibn Khalid said: Whenever I came to Abu Mahdhura, رضي الله عنه, he asked me about a certain man, and whenever I came to that man, he asked me about Abu Mahdhura. I said to Abu Mahdhura: When I come to you, you ask me about so-and-so, and when I come to so-and-so, he asks me about you. He said: I, Abu Hurayrah, and so-and-so were in a house, and the Prophet ﷺ said: The last of you to die is in the Fire. Then Abu Hurayrah died, then Abu Mahdhura died, then the man died. > **""The last of you to die is in the Fire.""**"
"14077","Abu Yunus said: I was a trader at Madina. When I came to Madina, Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, asked me about Samura ibn Jundub, and when I came to Basra, Samura asked me about Abu Hurayrah. Abu Hurayrah said: We were seven in a house, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon us and said: The last of you to die is in the Fire. None remained but I and Samura. I say: Perhaps he meant the fire of this world, for Samura died thus by fire, and Allah knows best. > **""The last of you to die is in the Fire.""**"
"14078","Jabir ibn Samura, رضي الله عنه, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: A lone woman traveler shall surely go out from Madina until she enters Hira, fearing none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic. > **""A lone woman traveler shall surely go out from Madina until she enters Hira, fearing none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"14079","Abu Juhayfa, رضي الله عنه, in what some narrators know, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The world shall be opened for you until your houses are draped as the Kaba is draped. We said: While upon our religion. He said: Yes. We said: Shall we be better that day than today. He said: Rather, you today are better than on that day. > **""The world shall be opened for you until your houses are draped as the Kaba is draped.""** > **""Yes.""** > **""Rather, you today are better than on that day.""**"
"14080","Hudhayfa, رضي الله عنه, said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: There shall be in my Ummah a man who speaks after death. I say: The hadith of al-Nuʿman ibn Bashir concerning one who spoke after death, in the caliphate chapter on the four caliphs, has preceded. > **""There shall be in my Ummah a man who speaks after death.""**"
"14081","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, رضي الله عنه, said: A wolf attacked a sheep and seized it. The shepherd pursued it and recovered it from him. The wolf sat upon its tail and said: Do you not fear Allah, taking from me provision which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, drove to me. He said: How wondrous, a wolf sitting upon its tail speaking to me with human speech. The wolf said: Shall I inform you of more wondrous than that: Muhammad ﷺ at Yathrib informs people of tidings of what has passed. He said: The shepherd came driving his sheep until he entered Madina, gathered them in a corner of its corners, then came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered and it was announced: Prayer in congregation. Then he came out and said to the Bedouin: Inform them. He informed them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: He spoke truthfully. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, the Hour shall not stand until beasts speak to men, the tip of a man's whip and the strap of his sandal speak to him, and his thigh informs him of what his household did after him. > **""Inform them.""** > **""He spoke truthfully. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, the Hour shall not stand until beasts speak to men, the tip of a man's whip and the strap of his sandal speak to him, and his thigh informs him of what his household did after him.""**"
"14082","In a narration, from Abu Saʿid also, رضي الله عنه: While a man of Aslam was among his small flock driving them at the desert of Dhu al-Hulayfa, a wolf attacked him and seized a sheep from his flock. The man shouted at him, throwing stones until he rescued his sheep from him. Then he mentioned similar to it."
"14083","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, said: A wolf came to a shepherd of sheep and took a sheep from them. The shepherd pursued him until he recovered it from him. He said: The wolf climbed a hill, sat, tucked its tail, and said: You set upon provision which Allah provided me and took it from me. The shepherd said: By Allah, never did I see like today a wolf speaking. The wolf said: More wondrous than this is a man among the date-palms between the two lava tracts informing you of what has passed and what shall be after you. The man was a Jew. He came to the Prophet ﷺ, informed him, and the Prophet ﷺ confirmed him and said: They are signs among signs before the Hour; a man shall soon go out and not return until his sandals and his whip inform him of what his household did after him. > **""They are signs among signs before the Hour; a man shall soon go out and not return until his sandals and his whip inform him of what his household did after him.""**"
"14084","Abu Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reported from the Prophet ﷺ similar to it, meaning similar to the preceding hadith, with addition: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one day the dawn prayer, then said: This is the wolf, and what is the wolf; he has come to you asking you to give him or to make him partner in your wealth. A man threw a stone at him, and he passed on, or turned away, howling. > **""This is the wolf, and what is the wolf; he has come to you asking you to give him or to make him partner in your wealth.""**"
"14085","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey when a Bedouin came. When he drew near, the Prophet ﷺ said to him: Where do you intend. He said: To my family. He said: Do you desire good. He said: What is it. He said: That you testify that there is no god but Allah alone without partner and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. He said: Who witnesses to what you say. He said: This tree. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called it while it stood at the bank of the valley, and it came tearing the earth until it stood before him. He called it to witness three times, and it testified that it is as he said. Then it returned to its place of growth. The Bedouin returned to his people and said: If they follow me, I shall bring them to you; otherwise I shall return to you and be with you. > **""Where do you intend.""** > **""Do you desire good.""** > **""That you testify that there is no god but Allah alone without partner and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger.""** > **""This tree.""**"
"14086","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, رضي الله عنه, with the hadith of the lizard: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in a gathering of his Companions when a Bedouin of Banu Sulaym came, having hunted a lizard and placed it in his sleeve. He went with it toward his lodging and saw a group. He said: For whom is this group. They said: For this one who claims he is the Prophet. He tore through the people, then came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Muhammad, no woman ever bore one of speech more lying and more deficient than you. Were it not that the Arabs would call me hasty, I would hasten upon you and kill you, delighting all people by your killing. ʿUmar said: O Messenger of Allah, let me kill him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Do you not know that the forbearing is near to being a prophet. Then the Bedouin turned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: By al-Lat and al-ʿUzza, I shall not believe in you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to him: O Bedouin, what carried you to say what you said, speaking falsehood and not honoring my gathering. He said, belittling the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: And you speak to me too; by al-Lat and al-ʿUzza, I shall not believe in you until this lizard believes in you. He took the lizard from his sleeve and cast it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: If this lizard believes in you, I shall believe in you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O lizard. The lizard spoke to him in clear Arabic understood by all the people: At your service and felicity, O Messenger of the Lord of the worlds. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whom do you worship. He said: Him whose Throne is in heaven, whose dominion is in earth, whose path is in the sea, whose mercy is in Paradise, and whose punishment is in the Fire. He said: Who am I, O lizard. He said: You are the Messenger of the Lord of the worlds and the Seal of the Prophets; successful is whoever confirms you, and disappointed is whoever denies you. The Bedouin said: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that you are truly the Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I came to you when none upon the face of the earth was more hateful to me than you, and by Allah, at this hour you are dearer to me than myself and my child. I have believed in you with my hair and skin, my inward and outward, my secret and open. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: Praise be to Allah who guided you to the religion which is supreme and not surpassed; Allah, Exalted, accepts it only with prayer, and prayer is accepted only with Qurʾan. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him the Opening and Say: He is Allah, One. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I never heard in extended nor in rajaz verse better than this. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: This is the speech of the Lord of the worlds and not poetry. When you recite Say: He is Allah, One once, it is as if you recited a third of the Qurʾan; when you recite it twice, it is as if you recited two thirds; and when you recite it thrice, it is as if you recited the whole Qurʾan. The Bedouin said: What an excellent God, for our God accepts little and grants much. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Give the Bedouin. They gave him until they satisfied him. ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf, رضي الله عنه, said: O Messenger of Allah, I wish to give him a she-camel seeking nearness to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, below the Bukhti and above the common, ten months pregnant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: You have described what you give, and I shall describe for you what Allah, Exalted, shall give you in recompense. He said: Yes. He said: Yours shall be a she-camel of hollow pearl, its legs of green emerald, its neck of yellow topaz, upon it a litter, and upon the litter silk and brocade; it shall pass with you over the Bridge like darting lightning. The Bedouin left the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and was met by one thousand Bedouins upon one thousand mounts with one thousand spears and one thousand swords. He said to them: Where do you intend. They said: To fight this one who lies and claims he is a prophet. The Bedouin said: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said to him: Have you apostatized. He said to them: I have not apostatized. He told them this hadith, and they all said: There is no god but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he met them in a cloak. They dismounted from their mounts, kissing what they saw of him, saying: There is no god but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. They said: Command us by your command, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You shall enter under the banner of Khalid ibn al-Walid. He said: None of the Arabs believed one thousand all together except Banu Sulaym. > **""Do you not know that the forbearing is near to being a prophet.""** > **""O Bedouin, what carried you to say what you said, speaking falsehood and not honoring my gathering.""** > **""O lizard.""** > **""Whom do you worship.""** > **""Who am I, O lizard.""** > **""Praise be to Allah who guided you to the religion which is supreme and not surpassed; Allah, Exalted, accepts it only with prayer, and prayer is accepted only with Qurʾan.""** > **""This is the speech of the Lord of the worlds and not poetry. When you recite Say: He is Allah, One once, it is as if you recited a third of the Qurʾan; when you recite it twice, it is as if you recited two thirds; and when you recite it thrice, it is as if you recited the whole Qurʾan.""** > **""You have described what you give, and I shall describe for you what Allah, Exalted, shall give you in recompense.""** > **""Yours shall be a she-camel of hollow pearl, its legs of green emerald, its neck of yellow topaz, upon it a litter, and upon the litter silk and brocade; it shall pass with you over the Bridge like darting lightning.""** > **""Give the Bedouin.""** > **""You shall enter under the banner of Khalid ibn al-Walid.""**"
"14087","Anas ibn Malik, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a people who had hunted a gazelle and tied it to the pole of a tent. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I have given birth to two young fawns; permit me to suckle them, then I shall return. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Let her go until she comes to her fawns and suckles them and comes back to you. They said: And who guarantees that for us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I do. They released her; she went, suckled, then returned to them, and they tied her. He said: Will you sell her. They said: O Messenger of Allah, she is yours. They released her and she went away. > **""Let her go until she comes to her fawns and suckles them and comes back to you.""** > **""I do.""** > **""Will you sell her.""**"
"14088","Umm Salama, رضي الله عنها, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the desert when a caller called to him: O Messenger of Allah. He turned but saw no one. Then he turned and there was a gazelle tied. She said: Come near me, O Messenger of Allah. He drew near her and said: What is your need. She said: I have two fawns in this mountain; free me until I go and suckle them, then I shall return to you. He said: And will you do so. She said: May Allah punish me with the punishment of the tax-collector if I do not. He released her; she went and suckled her fawns, then returned, and he tied her. The Bedouin awoke and said: Do you have a need, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Yes, that you free her. He freed her, and she went out running, saying: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. > **""What is your need.""** > **""And will you do so.""** > **""Yes, that you free her.""**"
"14089","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported that a Jewish woman gifted a poisoned sheep to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He sent for her and said: What carried you to do what you did. She said: I wished, or intended, that if you are a prophet, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will surely inform you of it, and if you are not a prophet, I shall relieve people of you. He said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ felt anything of that, he was cupped. He said: He once traveled, and when he entered ihram he felt something of that and was cupped. > **""What carried you to do what you did.""**"
"14090","Anas, رضي الله عنه, reported similar to it, with addition: A Jewish woman gifted a prepared sheep to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he extended his hand to it to eat, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A limb of it informs me that it is poisoned. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ held back, and those with him held back. He sent for the Jewess and said: What carried you to spoil it after you had prepared it well. She said: I wished to know that if you are a prophet, you will surely know it, and if you are not a prophet, I shall relieve people of you. > **""A limb of it informs me that it is poisoned.""** > **""What carried you to spoil it after you had prepared it well.""**"
"14091","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, رضي الله عنه, reported that a Jewess gifted a prepared sheep to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the people extended their hands, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Withhold, for a limb of it informs me that it is poisoned. He sent to its owner: Did you poison this food of yours. She said: Yes. He said: What carried you to that. She said: I intended that if you are false, I would relieve people of you, and if you are truthful, I knew that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will surely inform you of it. He extended his hand and said: Eat in the name of Allah. He said: We ate and mentioned the name of Allah, and it harmed none of us. > **""Withhold, for a limb of it informs me that it is poisoned.""** > **""Did you poison this food of yours.""** > **""What carried you to that.""** > **""Eat in the name of Allah.""**"
"14092","Kaʿb ibn Malik, رضي الله عنه, reported that a Jewish woman gifted a roasted sheep at Khaybar to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said to her: What is this. She said: This is a gift, being cautious not to say charity. He and his Companions ate, then he said to them: Withhold. Then he said to the woman: Did you poison this sheep. She said: Who informed you. He said: This bone of its leg, while it was in his hand. She said: Yes. He said: Why. She said: I said: If you are false, people shall be relieved of you, and if you are a prophet, it shall not harm you. The Prophet ﷺ was cupped and ordered his Companions, and they were cupped, but one of them died. Al-Zuhri said: The woman embraced Islam, though they claimed he killed her. > **""What is this.""** > **""Withhold.""** > **""Did you poison this sheep.""** > **""This bone of its leg.""** > **""Why.""**"
"14093","Yahya ibn ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Labiba, from his father, from his grandfather, said: A poisoned roasted sheep was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He and Bishr ibn al-Baraʾ ibn Maʿrur ate from it, and they fell severely ill. Then Bishr died. When he died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for the Jewess who had gifted it and said: Woe to you, what did you feed us. She said: I fed you poison. He said: What carried you to that. She said: I heard you mention prophethood; if you are a prophet, I knew it shall not harm you, and if otherwise, I wished to relieve people of you. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered concerning her, and she was crucified. > **""Woe to you, what did you feed us.""** > **""What carried you to that.""**"
"14094","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not eat from a gift until he ordered its owner to eat from it, because of the sheep gifted to him at Khaybar."
"14095","Jabir, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the sun, and it receded an hour of the day."
"14096","Asmaʾ bint ʿUmays, رضي الله عنها, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr at al-Sahbaʾ, then sent ʿAli, رضي الله عنه, for a need. He returned after the Prophet ﷺ had prayed ʿAsr. The Prophet ﷺ placed his head in the lap of ʿAli and slept, and ʿAli did not move him until the sun set. The Prophet ﷺ said: O Allah, Your servant ʿAli held himself back for Your Prophet, so return the sun for him. Asmaʾ said: The sun rose upon him until it stood upon the mountains and the earth. ʿAli rose, performed ablution, and prayed ʿAsr, then it set there at al-Sahbaʾ. > **""O Allah, Your servant ʿAli held himself back for Your Prophet, so return the sun for him.""**"
"14097","In a narration from her also, she said: When revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would almost be overcome. It descended one day while he was in the lap of ʿAli. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: Have you prayed ʿAsr. He said: No, O Messenger of Allah. He supplicated Allah, and He returned the sun for him until he prayed ʿAsr. She said: I saw the sun rise after it had set, when it was returned, until he prayed ʿAsr. > **""Have you prayed ʿAsr.""**"
"14098","Qatada ibn al-Nuʿman, رضي الله عنه, said: A bow was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ handed it to me on the day of Uhud. I shot with it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until its tip broke, and I did not leave my station facing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, meeting arrows with my face. Whenever an arrow inclined toward the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I inclined my face and head to shield the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, without shooting back. The last of them was an arrow by which my eyeball protruded onto my cheek, and the host dispersed. I took my eyeball in my palm and hastened with it in my palm to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw it, his eyes filled with tears, and he said: O Allah, Qatada has shielded Your Prophet with his face, so make it his finest eye and sharpest in sight. And it became his finest eye and sharpest in sight. Al-Tabarani and Abu Yaʿla reported it, with wording: From Qatada ibn al-Nuʿman that his eye was struck on the day of Badr, his eyeball flowed onto his cheek, they wished to cut it off, they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: No. He called for him and pressed his eyeball with his palm, and none knew which of his eyes had been struck. > **""O Allah, Qatada has shielded Your Prophet with his face, so make it his finest eye and sharpest in sight.""** > **""No.""**"
"14099","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn ʿUbayda, from his grandfather, said: The eye of Abu Dharr, رضي الله عنه, was struck on the day of Uhud, and the Prophet ﷺ spat into it, and it became the soundest of his eyes."
"14100","A man of Salaman ibn Saʿid, from his mother, reported that her maternal uncle Habib ibn Furayk told her that her father took him out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while his eyes were white, seeing nothing with them. He asked him what had befallen him. He said: I was leading my camels when my foot fell upon the eggs of a snake, and my sight was afflicted. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ breathed into his eyes, and he saw. He was seen threading a needle though eighty years old, while his eyes remained white."
"14101","Makhlad ibn ʿUqba ibn Shurahbil, from his grandfather ʿAbd al-Rahman, from his father, said: I came to the Messenger ﷺ with a cyst in my palm. I said: O Prophet of Allah, this cyst has swollen me, barring me from the hilt of the sword to grip it and from the rein of the mount. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Come near me. I drew near and opened it. He breathed into my palm, then placed his hand upon the cyst and kept pressing it until he lifted his hand, and I saw no trace of it. > **""Come near me.""**"
"14102","ʿAbd Allah ibn Unays, رضي الله عنه, said: al-Mustanir ibn Rizam the Jew struck my face with a scratcher of shawhat wood, wounding me with a displacing or penetrating wound. I brought it to the Prophet ﷺ; he uncovered it and breathed into it, and I never saw anything of it again."
"14103","Suwayd ibn Zayd said: I saw Abu Dharr, رضي الله عنه, sitting alone in the mosque. I seized the opportunity and sat with him, and mentioned ʿUthman to him. He said: I shall never say of ʿUthman but good, for something I saw with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I used to follow the private moments of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and learn from him. One day I went, and he had come out, so I followed him. He sat in a place and I sat with him. He said: O Abu Dharr, what has brought you. He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger. He said: Abu Bakr, رضي الله عنه, came, greeted, and sat at the right of the Prophet ﷺ. He said to him: What has brought you, O Abu Bakr. He said: Allah and His Messenger. He said: ʿUmar, رضي الله عنه, came and sat at the right of Abu Bakr. He said: O ʿUmar, what has brought you. He said: Allah and His Messenger. Then ʿUthman, رضي الله عنه, came and sat at the right of ʿUmar. He said: O ʿUthman, what has brought you. He said: Allah and His Messenger. He said: The Prophet ﷺ took seven pebbles, or nine pebbles, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he placed them and they fell silent. Then he placed them in the hand of Abu Bakr and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he placed them and they fell silent. Then he took them and placed them in the hand of ʿUthman and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he placed them and they fell silent. > **""O Abu Dharr, what has brought you.""** > **""What has brought you, O Abu Bakr.""** > **""O ʿUmar, what has brought you.""** > **""O ʿUthman, what has brought you.""**"
"14104","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ awoke one day and said: Is there any water. They said: No. He said: Is there a worn waterskin. They brought a waterskin and placed it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He placed his hand upon it, then spread his fingers, and water gushed forth like the staff of Musa, from the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: O Bilal, call the people to ablution. They came performing ablution from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while the concern of Ibn Masʿud, رضي الله عنه, was drinking. When they performed ablution, he led them in Fajr. Then he sat for the people and said: O people, whose faith is most wondrous. They said: The angels. He said: And how should not the angels believe while they witness the affair. They said: Then the prophets, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And how should not the prophets believe while revelation descends upon them from heaven. They said: Then your Companions, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And how should not my Companions believe while they see what they see. Rather the most wondrous of people in faith are a people coming after me, believing in me though they never saw me, and affirming me though they never saw me; those are my brethren. > **""Is there any water.""** > **""Is there a worn waterskin.""** > **""O Bilal, call the people to ablution.""** > **""O people, whose faith is most wondrous.""** > **""And how should not the angels believe while they witness the affair.""** > **""And how should not the prophets believe while revelation descends upon them from heaven.""** > **""And how should not my Companions believe while they see what they see. Rather the most wondrous of people in faith are a people coming after me, believing in me though they never saw me, and affirming me though they never saw me; those are my brethren.""**"
"14105","Al-Baraʾ ibn ʿAzib, رضي الله عنه, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey and came to a well of little water. Six descended into it, I being the sixth, to draw. A bucket was let down to us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at the edge of the well. We placed in it half or near two thirds, and it was raised to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-Baraʾ said: I brought my vessel seeking something to place in my throat, but found nothing. The bucket was raised to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he dipped his hand into it and said what Allah willed him to say. The bucket was returned to us with what was in it. He said: I saw the last of us brought out with force for fear of drowning. He said: Then it flowed, meaning it ran as a river."
"14106","Anas ibn Malik, رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equipped an army toward the polytheists, among them Abu Bakr and ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما. He placed them both and all the people in command and said to them: March hard, for between you and the polytheists is water; if the polytheists precede to that water, it shall distress the people, and you shall thirst severely, you and your beasts and mounts. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed behind with eight, he being their ninth. He said to his Companions: Would you rest briefly at night, then join the people. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. They rested, and nothing awakened them but the heat of the sun. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions awoke, and he said to them: Rise and relieve yourselves. They did, then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: Is there water with any of you. A man among them said: O Messenger of Allah, a small ablution vessel with some water in it. He said: Bring it. He brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He wiped it with his palms and supplicated for blessing, then said to his Companions: Come and perform ablution. They came, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ poured upon them until they performed ablution. A man among them called the adhan and iqama. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in prayer and said to the owner of the vessel: Cherish your vessel, for it shall have a great matter. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions mounted before the people. He said to his Companions: What do you think the people did. They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Among them are Abu Bakr and ʿUmar, and they shall guide the people. The people arrived after the polytheists had preceded to that water, and the people were distressed and thirsted severely, with their mounts and beasts. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Where is the owner of the vessel. He said: Here he is, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Bring your vessel. He brought it with some water in it. He said to them all: Come and drink. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ poured for them until they all drank, watered their beasts and mounts, and filled every skin and water-bag. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions advanced toward the polytheists. Allah sent a wind striking the faces of the polytheists, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sent His help, and enabled them against their backs. They slew a great slaughter among them, took many captives, drove much booty, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people returned safe and sound. > **""March hard, for between you and the polytheists is water; if the polytheists precede to that water, it shall distress the people, and you shall thirst severely, you and your beasts and mounts.""** > **""Would you rest briefly at night, then join the people.""** > **""Rise and relieve yourselves.""** > **""Is there water with any of you.""** > **""Bring it.""** > **""Come and perform ablution.""** > **""Cherish your vessel, for it shall have a great matter.""** > **""What do you think the people did.""** > **""Among them are Abu Bakr and ʿUmar, and they shall guide the people.""** > **""Where is the owner of the vessel.""** > **""Bring your vessel.""** > **""Come and drink.""**"
"14107","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father, رضي الله عنه, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on an expedition and severe thirst befell us. We complained of it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: Is there leftover water in a small skin. A man brought leftover water in a skin. The Prophet ﷺ dug a pit in the earth, placed a drop upon it, placed his palm upon the earth, then said to the owner of the skin: Pour water upon my palm and mention the name of Allah. He did. Abu Layla said: I saw water springing from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until the people quenched and watered their mounts. > **""Is there leftover water in a small skin.""** > **""Pour water upon my palm and mention the name of Allah.""**"
"14108","Abu Rajaʾ said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out until he entered a garden of an Ansari, where he was drawing water. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: What will you give me if I irrigate this garden of yours. He said: I strive to irrigate it but cannot manage it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: Will you give me one hundred dates which I choose from your dates. He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the bucket and soon irrigated it, until the man said: You have flooded my garden. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ chose one hundred dates. He said: He and his Companions ate until they were full, then he returned to him one hundred dates as he had taken from him. > **""What will you give me if I irrigate this garden of yours.""** > **""Will you give me one hundred dates which I choose from your dates.""**"
"14109","ʿAli, رضي الله عنه, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered Banu ʿAbd al-Muttalib, among them a group each of whom would eat a young goat and drink a faraq. He said: He prepared a mudd of food for them. They ate until full, while the food remained as it was, as if untouched. Then he called for a small cup; they drank until full, while the drink remained as if untouched and undrunk. He said: O children of ʿAbd al-Muttalib, I have been sent to you specially and to people generally. You have seen of this sign what you have seen, so which of you will pledge me that he be my brother and companion. He said: None rose to him. He said: I rose to him, though the youngest of the people. He said: Sit. Three times, each time I rose to him and he said to me: Sit, until the third time he struck his hand upon my hand. > **""O children of ʿAbd al-Muttalib, I have been sent to you specially and to people generally. You have seen of this sign what you have seen, so which of you will pledge me that he be my brother and companion.""** > **""Sit.""**"
"14110","ʿAli, رضي الله عنه, said: When Warn your nearest kin was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O ʿAli, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food, and gather Banu Hashim for me. They were that day forty men, or forty less a man. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for the food and placed it among them. They ate until full, though among them was one who would eat a jadhʿa with its condiment. Then he took the cup and they drank from it until quenched, meaning of milk. One of them said: We never saw like magic, deemed to be Abu Lahab who said it. He said: O ʿAli, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and prepare a bowl of milk. He said: I did. They ate as they ate the first day, drank as they drank the first time, and surplus remained as the first time. He said: We never saw like today in magic. He said: O ʿAli, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and prepare a bowl of milk. I did. He said: O ʿAli, gather Banu Hashim for me. I gathered them; they ate and drank. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastened to them and said: Which of you will settle my debt for me. He said: He fell silent, and the people fell silent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeated the speech, and I said: I will, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You, O ʿAli; you, O ʿAli. > **""O ʿAli, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food, and gather Banu Hashim for me.""** > **""O ʿAli, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and prepare a bowl of milk.""** > **""O ʿAli, gather Banu Hashim for me.""** > **""Which of you will settle my debt for me.""** > **""You, O ʿAli; you, O ʿAli.""**"
"14111","Abu Ayyub, رضي الله عنه, said: I prepared food for the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr, رضي الله عنه, enough for them both, and brought it to them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: Go and invite for me thirty of the nobles of the Ansar. That weighed upon me, and I said: I have nothing to add. As if I feigned heedlessness. He said: Go and bring me thirty of the nobles of the Ansar. I invited them and they came. He said: Eat. They ate until full, then testified that he is the Messenger of Allah, then pledged him before leaving. Then he said: Go and invite for me sixty of the nobles of the Ansar. Abu Ayyub said: By Allah, I was more willing for the sixty than for the thirty. He said: I invited them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Pause. They ate until full, then testified that he is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then pledged him before leaving. Then he said: Go and invite for me ninety of the Ansar. I was more willing for the ninety and sixty than for the thirty. He said: I invited them; they ate until full, then testified that he is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then pledged him before leaving. One hundred and eighty men ate from that food of mine, all of the Ansar. > **""Go and invite for me thirty of the nobles of the Ansar.""** > **""Eat.""** > **""Go and invite for me sixty of the nobles of the Ansar.""** > **""Pause.""** > **""Go and invite for me ninety of the Ansar.""**"
"14112","Abu Hubaysh al-Ghifari, رضي الله عنه, reported that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of Tihama until, at a camp, the Companions came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, hunger has exhausted us; permit us concerning the mounts to eat them. He said: Yes. ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, رضي الله عنه, was informed of it and came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Prophet of Allah, what have you done; you ordered the people to slaughter the mounts, then upon what shall they ride. He said: Then what is your view, O son of Khattab. He said: I view that you order them to bring the surplus of their provisions, gather it in a vessel, then supplicate Allah for them. He ordered them; they placed the surplus of their provisions in a cloth, then he supplicated for them. Then he said: Bring your vessels. Every man among them filled his vessel. Then he ordered departure. When they passed on and waited, they halted; he halted and they halted with him, and they drank of the water of heaven. Three men came; two sat with the Prophet ﷺ and the other went away turning aside. The Prophet ﷺ said: Shall I inform you of the three men: as for one, he was shy before Allah, so Allah was shy of him; as for the other, he came repentant, so Allah forgave him; and as for the other, he turned away, so Allah turned away from him. > **""Yes.""** > **""Then what is your view, O son of Khattab.""** > **""Bring your vessels.""** > **""Shall I inform you of the three men: as for one, he was shy before Allah, so Allah was shy of him; as for the other, he came repentant, so Allah forgave him; and as for the other, he turned away, so Allah turned away from him.""**"
"14113","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, رضي الله عنه, said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ on an expedition and said: O Messenger of Allah, the enemy has arrived while full, and the people are hungry. The Ansar said: Shall we not slaughter our water-camels and feed the people. The Prophet ﷺ said: Whoever has surplus food, let him bring it. A man brought a mudd and a sa, more and less, until all in the army was a little over twenty sa. The Prophet ﷺ sat beside it and supplicated for blessing. The Prophet ﷺ said: Take and do not plunder. A man took in his bag and sack, and they took in their vessels, until a man tied the sleeve of his shirt and filled it. They finished while the food remained as it was. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah; no servant brings them truthfully but Allah shall shield him from the heat of the Fire. > **""Whoever has surplus food, let him bring it.""** > **""Take and do not plunder.""** > **""I testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah; no servant brings them truthfully but Allah shall shield him from the heat of the Fire.""**"
"14114","Al-Nuʿman ibn Muqarrin, رضي الله عنه, said: We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, four hundred of Muzayna. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered concerning us, and some people said: O Messenger of Allah, we have no food to provision them. The Prophet ﷺ said to ʿUmar: Provision them. He said: I have only leftover dates, and I do not see them availing them. He said: Go and provision them. He took us to an upper chamber, and in it were dates like a young grey camel. He said: Take. The people took their need. He said: I was among the last of the people. He said: I looked and did not miss the place of a single date, though four hundred men had carried from it. > **""Provision them.""** > **""Go and provision them.""**"
"14115","Dukayn ibn Saʿid al-Khathʿami, رضي الله عنه, said: We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, forty and four hundred, asking him for food. The Prophet ﷺ said to ʿUmar: Rise and give them. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have only what suffices me and the children for the summer, and Wakiʿ said: Qayz in Arab speech is four months. He said: Rise and give them. ʿUmar said: O Messenger of Allah, hearing and obeying. He said: ʿUmar rose and we rose with him; he took us up to his chamber, took the key from his room, and opened the door. Dukayn said: In the chamber were dates resembling a reclining young camel. He said: Help yourselves. He said: Every man among us took his need as he wished. He said: I looked, though among the last of them, and it was as if we had not taken a single date from it. > **""Rise and give them.""** > **""Hearing and obeying.""**"
"14116","Wathila ibn al-Asqaʿ, رضي الله عنه, said: I was among the people of al-Suffa. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called me one day for a loaf; he broke it in the tray, made hot water in it, then made fat in it, then crumbled it, then mixed it, then prepared it. Then he said: Go and bring me ten, you being their tenth. I brought them. He said: Eat, and eat from its lower part, and do not eat from its upper part, for blessing descends in its upper part. They ate from it until full. > **""Go and bring me ten, you being their tenth.""** > **""Eat, and eat from its lower part, and do not eat from its upper part, for blessing descends in its upper part.""**"
"14117","Wathila ibn al-Asqaʿ, رضي الله عنه, also said: I was of the companions of al-Suffa. My companions complained of hunger and said: O Wathila, go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and seek food for us. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, my companions have complained of hunger. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAʾisha: Is there anything with you. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I have only crumbs of bread. He said: Bring it to me. She brought a bag. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a tray, emptied the bread into the tray, then set about preparing tharid with his hand, and it grew until the tray was full. He said: O Wathila, go and bring ten of my companions, you being their tenth. I went and brought ten of my companions, I being their tenth. He said: Sit and take in the name of Allah; take from around it and do not take from its top, for blessing descends from its top. They ate until full, then rose, while in the tray remained as had been in it. Then he set about preparing it with his hand, and it grew until full. He said: O Wathila, go and bring ten of your companions. I brought ten, and he said: Sit. They sat and ate until full, then rose. He said: Go and bring ten of your companions. I went and brought ten, and they did likewise. He said: Has any remained. I said: Yes, ten. He said: Go and bring them. I went and brought them. He said: Sit. They sat and ate until full, then rose, while in the tray remained as had been. Then he said: O Wathila, take this to ʿAʾisha, رضي الله عنها. > **""Is there anything with you.""** > **""Bring it to me.""** > **""O Wathila, go and bring ten of my companions, you being their tenth.""** > **""Sit and take in the name of Allah; take from around it and do not take from its top, for blessing descends from its top.""** > **""O Wathila, go and bring ten of your companions.""** > **""Sit.""** > **""Go and bring ten of your companions.""** > **""Has any remained.""** > **""Go and bring them.""** > **""Sit.""** > **""O Wathila, take this to ʿAʾisha.""**"
"14118","In a narration: I was in al-Suffa, and they were twenty men. Then he mentioned similar to it, except that he said: They said: Here is a crumb and some milk."
"14119","Abū Talha رضي الله عنه reports entering the mosque and recognizing hunger on the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He went to Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها, mother of Anas ibn Malik, and asked whether she had anything. She indicated a small amount with her palm. He told her to prepare it well, then sent Anas to invite the Prophet ﷺ privately in his ear. When Anas came, the Prophet ﷺ asked his companions to proceed in Allah's name. Anas hurried back and announced that the Prophet ﷺ was coming with people. Abū Talha met the Prophet ﷺ at the door and explained he had only prepared a little because he had seen hunger. The Prophet ﷺ told him to enter with glad tidings, gathered the food in a dish with his hand, mended it, asked for condiment, squeezed ghee from their skin, blessed it, ordered ten by ten to enter, and all ate to satiety. The remainder was left for Abū Talha and his household. > **""Admit ten by ten.""**"
"14120","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day and finding him seated with his companions, conversing, with his belly bound with a stone. A companion told him it was from hunger. Anas went to Abū Talha رضي الله عنه, husband of Umm Sulaym bint Milhan, and informed him. Abū Talha asked his wife whether anything was available. She said she had pieces of bread and dates, enough to satisfy the Prophet ﷺ alone but insufficient if others came. Abū Talha instructed Anas to stand near the Prophet ﷺ, wait until the people dispersed, and invite him alone at his doorway. Anas did so, but the Prophet ﷺ called all his companions to come. He seized Anas by the hand and advanced with them until near the house, then released him. Anas entered grieved by their number and informed his father. Abū Talha came out and explained he had intended only the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ told him to enter, for Allah would satisfy them from what he had. He entered, ordered all that was present to be gathered and brought near, seated those with him at the bench, placed the bread and dates on their mat, supplicated for blessing, placed his palm over the food, and ordered groups of eight to enter and eat in Allah's name until eighty men ate to satiety. Then he called Anas, his mother, and Abū Talha to eat, and after they were satisfied he lifted his hand and asked Umm Sulaym how the food compared to when she presented it. She replied by his parents that had she not seen them eating she would have said nothing had diminished. > **""Enter, for Allah will satisfy them from what you have.""**"
"14121","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that Abū Talha رضي الله عنه, his stepfather, came to Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها and asked whether she had anything, for he had passed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was teaching the people of the bench Surat al-Nisa with a stone tied on his belly from hunger. She said she had some barley and ground it. The party then set out, eighty men that day, with the Prophet ﷺ holding Anas by the hand. When near the house Anas pulled his hand away. Abū Talha pursued him in the house, throwing stones and saying he had disgraced him before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then Abū Talha went out, informed the Prophet ﷺ of the situation, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered the people to sit, then entered. They brought the loaf, and he asked whether there was condiment. Umm Sulaym said they had a skin that she and Abū Talha had already squeezed. The Prophet ﷺ ordered it brought, for the squeezing of three is more effective than of two, squeezed it with them by his hand, supplicated for blessing therein, and ordered ten to be called. They ate until they belched from satiety. > **""Bring it, for the squeezing of three is more effective than the squeezing of two.""**"
"14122","Jābir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that his mother prepared food and told him to go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and invite him. He came to the Prophet ﷺ, spoke to him privately, and said his mother had prepared something. The Prophet ﷺ told his companions to rise, and fifty men rose with him. He sat at the door and ordered ten by ten to be admitted. They ate until satisfied, and nearly as much as before remained. > **""Rise.""**"
"14123","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to gather his companions. He went after them in the mosque, waking them one by one, until they came to the door of the Prophet ﷺ and entered. A dish was placed before them, prepared from about two mudd of barley. The Prophet ﷺ told them to eat in Allah's name, and when the dish was set down he swore by the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad that nothing remained in the household of Muhammad besides what they saw. Between seventy and eighty men ate until satisfied, with a remainder left in it. Asked what it looked like when they finished, Abū Hurayra said it looked as when it was set down, except for traces of fingers therein. > **""Eat in the name of Allah.""**"
"14124","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports missing supper with the Prophet ﷺ one night, with none of his companions inviting him. He prayed night prayer, then wished to sleep but could not, then wished to pray but could not. He noticed a man at the chamber of the Prophet ﷺ and found it was the Prophet ﷺ praying. After praying the Prophet ﷺ leaned against the pillar toward which he prayed and asked who was there. When Abū Hurayra answered, he asked whether he had missed supper with them that night. Upon confirmation he told him to go to the house and ask for whatever food was there. They gave him a dish containing porridge with dates. He brought it and placed it before the Prophet ﷺ, who told him to call the people of the mosque. Abū Hurayra feared within himself both the scantiness of the food and disobedience, yet he went to each sleeper waking him and each praying man calling him until they gathered. The Prophet ﷺ placed his fingers in it, pressed its sides, and told them to eat in Allah's name. All ate to satiety, and he too ate to satiety. The Prophet ﷺ then told him to take it back to the household of Muhammad, for no living creature had any food among them besides it, a gift from a man of the Ansar. When he lifted the dish it looked as when he had set it down, except for traces of the fingers of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Eat in the name of Allah.""**"
"14125","Safiyya, wife of the Prophet ﷺ رضي الله عنها, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came one day and asked her, O daughter of Huyayy, whether she had anything, for he was hungry. She swore by Allah that she had only two mudd of flour. He told her to prepare it. She placed it in the pot and cooked it, then announced it was done. He asked whether she knew of anything in the skin of the daughter of Abū Bakr. When she said she did not know, he went himself to her house and asked her whether anything was in her skin. She said there was nothing but a little. He brought it himself, squeezed its edges into the pot until she saw what came out, placed his hand therein, and told her in Allah's name to call her sisters, for he knew they felt as he felt. She called them and they ate to satiety. Then Abū Bakr, Umar, and another man came in turn and ate to satiety, with food remaining over them. > **""In the name of Allah, call your sisters, for I know they feel as I feel.""**"
"14126","Umm Anas ibn Malik, Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها, reports that they owned a sheep, from whose ghee she filled a skin, then sent it with Rubayba, instructing her to deliver it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his condiment. Rubayba brought it and said Umm Sulaym had sent a skin of ghee. The Prophet ﷺ ordered its skin emptied for her. It was emptied and handed back, and she departed. When Umm Sulaym later saw the skin full and dripping, she asked Rubayba whether she had not ordered her to go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Rubayba replied she had done so, and if not believed, Umm Sulaym could go ask the Prophet ﷺ herself. Umm Sulaym went with Rubayba and said she had sent a skin with ghee through her. The Prophet ﷺ replied she had indeed brought it. Umm Sulaym swore by the One Who sent him with guidance and the religion of truth that it was full and dripping ghee. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether she marvelled that Allah had fed her as she had fed His prophet, and told her to eat and to feed others. She returned home, divided it among such and such of their bowls, and left therein what served them as condiment for a month or two months. > **""Do you marvel that Allah has fed you as you fed His prophet? Eat and feed.""**"
"14127","Umm Malik al-Ansariyya رضي الله عنها reports bringing a skin of ghee to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He ordered Bilal رضي الله عنه to squeeze it, then returned it to her. When she returned she found it full. She came back to the Prophet ﷺ and asked whether anything had been revealed concerning her. He asked what was the matter, O Umm Malik. She asked why he had returned her gift. He summoned Bilal and questioned him, and Bilal swore by the One Who sent him with truth that he had squeezed it until he felt shy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ congratulated her, saying Allah had hastened its reward, then taught her to say after every prayer ten times glory be to Allah, ten times praise be to Allah, and ten times Allah is greatest. > **""Congratulations to you, O Umm Malik, Allah has hastened its reward.""**"
"14128","Umm Aws al-Bahziyya رضي الله عنها reports filling her clarified butter into a skin and presenting it as a gift to the Prophet ﷺ. He accepted it, took what was inside, supplicated for blessing for her, and it was returned to her full of ghee. Thinking the Prophet ﷺ had not accepted it, she came crying. He ordered that the matter be explained to her. She ate from it through the remainder of the life of the Prophet ﷺ, the rule of Abū Bakr, the rule of Umar, and the rule of ʿUthmān, until what occurred between ʿAlī and Muawiya occurred. > **""Inform her of the matter.""**"
"14129","Hamza ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reports that the food of the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ circulated by turns among his companions, one night upon this man and one night upon that. When the turn fell upon him he prepared their food but left the skin untied and unsealed, then carried the food to them. The skin moved and what was in it spilled. He lamented whether the food of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had spilled by his hands. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to bring it near. He replied he could not, O Messenger of Allah. When he returned to his place the skin was sounding qab qab. He told it to stop, for the excess had spilled and a remainder stayed. On inspection he found it filled to its breast. He carried it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ replied that had he left it, it would have filled to its mouth and then been sealed. > **""Bring it near.""**"
"14130","Masʿūd ibn Khalid رضي الله عنه reports sending a sheep to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then leaving for an errand. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned half of it to them. When he returned to Umm Khunas, his wife, he found meat with her and asked whence it came. She said his friend ﷺ had returned it to them from the sheep he had sent. He asked why she had not fed it to her dependents. She replied this was their remainder, and she had already fed them all, though they were so many that two or three sheep slaughtered would not have sufficed them. > **""Why do you not feed it to your dependents?""**"
"14131","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that a man of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned a guest. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered for him half a wasq of barley. They ate from it for a time, then one day he took it and measured it to see how much remained. Soon after it perished. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether they had measured it, and said had they not measured it, it would have remained for such and such, or for their lifetime. > **""Did you measure it? Had you not measured it, it would have remained.""**"
"14132","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that a bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask him about something. The Prophet ﷺ entered seeking something for him and found a morsel in one of his chambers. He brought it out, broke it into pieces, placed his hand over it, and told the bedouin to eat. The bedouin ate and a remainder stayed. The bedouin kept raising his head, looking at him, and saying he was a righteous man. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to accept Islam, but he refused Islam and kept saying he was a righteous man. > **""Eat, O bedouin.""**"
"14133","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reports that a roasted sheep was prepared for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and brought to him. He said, O Abū Rāfiʿ, hand me the foreleg, and he handed it to him. He said it a second time and he handed it to him. He said it a third time, whereupon Abū Rāfiʿ asked whether a sheep has but two forelegs. The Prophet ﷺ replied that had he kept silent he would have continued handing him a foreleg whenever he called for it. The narrator adds that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ liked the foreleg. > **""O Abū Rāfiʿ, hand me the foreleg.""**"
"14134","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reports in another narration that a sheep was gifted to him, which he placed in the pot. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and asked what it was, O Abū Rāfiʿ. He replied it was a gifted sheep they were cooking in the pot. The Prophet ﷺ said, hand me the foreleg. In another wording he had commanded him to roast a sheep and he roasted it. > **""What is this, O Abū Rāfiʿ? Hand me the foreleg.""**"
"14135","Salmā, wife of Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنها, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a sheep to Abū Rāfiʿ, as she knew on the day of the Trench. Abū Rāfiʿ roasted it, placed it in a basket, and set out with it. He met the Prophet ﷺ returning from the Trench, who said, O Abū Rāfiʿ, hand me the foreleg. He handed it to him. He repeated it a second, third, and fourth time, each time being handed it, until Abū Rāfiʿ asked whether a sheep has but two forelegs. The Prophet ﷺ replied that had he kept silent he would have handed him whenever he asked. > **""O Abū Rāfiʿ, hand me the foreleg.""**"
"14136","Abū ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه reports cooking a pot containing meat for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, hand me its foreleg, and he handed it to him. He repeated it a second and third time. Abū ʿUbayd asked how many forelegs a sheep has, O Prophet of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ swore by the One in Whose hand is his soul that had he kept silent he would have been given a foreleg whenever he called for it. > **""Hand me its foreleg.""**"
"14137","Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq reports that a man of Banu Ghifar informed him in the gathering of Salim ibn Abdillah, from so-and-so, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought food of bread and meat. He said, hand me the foreleg, and a foreleg was handed to him and he ate it. Yahya added to his knowledge that he said it again, was handed a foreleg and ate it, then said it a third time, whereupon the man said only two forelegs exist, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ replied by his father that had he kept silent he would have continued to be handed a foreleg whenever he called for it. > **""Hand me the foreleg.""**"
"14138","Abu Umama رضي الله عنه reports that a foul-tongued woman, known for that, came to the Prophet ﷺ while dried meat lay before him which he was eating. The Prophet ﷺ took a sinewy piece of dried meat, cast it into his mouth, and chewed it now on this side and now on the other. The woman said, O Prophet of Allah, will you not feed me. He said yes, and offered her from what lay before him. She refused except what was in his mouth. He took it out and gave it to her. She cast it into her mouth, chewed it until she swallowed it, and after that the foulness and sharpness formerly known from her was never again perceived. > **""Yes.""**"
"14139","The daughter of Khabbab رضي الله عنها reports that Khabbab went out on an expedition while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked after them, even milking a she-goat of theirs. He would milk it into a large bowl until it filled and nearly overflowed. When Khabbab returned and milked it, its yield returned to what it had been. They said to Khabbab that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to milk it until their bowl filled, but when he milked it the yield diminished."
"14140","Qays ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Sakuni رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out with Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, concealing themselves from Quraysh. They passed a shepherd. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether any ewe impregnated by the ram was present. He replied no, but here was a ewe left behind by weakness. The Prophet ﷺ told him to bring it. He brought it, the Prophet ﷺ wiped its udder, supplicated for blessing, milked, gave Abū Bakr to drink, then milked and gave the shepherd to drink, then milked and drank himself. The shepherd swore by Allah he had never seen his like and asked who he was. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether if informed he would conceal it. Upon his assent he said he was Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The man asked whether he was the one Quraysh claimed had apostatized. The Prophet ﷺ replied they said so. The man testified he was indeed Allah's Messenger, for none but a messenger could do what he had done, then asked whether to follow him. The Prophet ﷺ replied not today, but when he heard they had prevailed he should come to them. He came to the Prophet ﷺ after his triumph at Madina. > **""Bring it to me.""**"
"14141","Umm Mabad رضي الله عنها reports sending a domestic pregnant ewe to the Prophet ﷺ. He returned it, asking her to seek out a ewe that gives no milk. > **""Seek for me a ewe that gives no milk.""**"
"14142","Saʿd, freedman of Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, reports being with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, as he thought on a journey. They alighted at a station and the Prophet ﷺ told him to go to that she-goat and milk it, though shortly before he had known that place with no goat therein. When he came he found a full-uddered goat and milked it many times. He entrusted it to a man and busied himself with the baggage, whereupon the goat departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ found him slow and called him. He explained the baggage had occupied them and the goat had gone. The Prophet ﷺ replied that the goat had been taken away by its Lord. > **""O Saʿd, go to that she-goat and milk it.""**"
"14143","Abdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports being with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Makka among a group of his companions when he said that one man among them should rise with him, and no man with even a mote's weight of deceit in his heart should rise with him. Ibn Masʿūd rose, taking a waterskin he supposed held water, and went with him until at the heights of Makka they saw a dark gathering. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew a line for him and told him to stand there until he came to him. He stood while the Prophet ﷺ went to them; he saw them surging toward him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ conversed with them long into the night, then returned and asked whether he had remained standing, O son of Masʿūd. When he replied had he not been told to stand until he came, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had ablution water. He opened the skin and found it was nabidh. He swore he had taken it supposing it water, yet it was nabidh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that dates are wholesome and water is purifying, then performed ablution from it. When he rose to pray, two persons among them came and asked him to lead them in prayer. He aligned them behind him and led them. When he finished, Ibn Masʿūd asked who they were. He replied they were jinn of Nasībīn who had come disputing matters among them and had asked him for provision, which he had granted. Asked what he had granted, he said he had granted them droppings and bones, finding barley in every dung and meat clinging to every bone. At that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade seeking purity with bone or dung. > **""Stand here until I come to you.""**"
"14144","Abdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and said he had been commanded to recite to their brothers among the jinn, so one man should rise with him, and no man with even a grain's weight of pride in his heart should rise. He rose with him, taking a skin containing nabidh, and set out. When they emerged the Prophet ﷺ drew a line for him and told him not to leave it, for if he left it he would never see him nor would he see him until the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet ﷺ went on and vanished from him until he could not see him. At dawn he came back and asked whether he found him standing. He replied he had never sat down. The Prophet ﷺ asked what harm sitting would have done. He replied he feared leaving the line. The Prophet ﷺ said had he left it they would never have seen each other until the Day of Resurrection, then asked whether he had ablution water. When he said no, he asked what the skin held. He said nabidh. The Prophet ﷺ replied that dates are wholesome and water is purifying, performed ablution, and established prayer. When he finished, two men of the jinn rose to him and asked him for food. He asked whether he had not ordered for them and their people what would suffice them. They replied yes, but they had wished some of them to witness prayer with him. Asked who they were, they said they were of Nasībīn. He declared those two and their people successful, then ordered droppings and bones for them as food and meat. > **""Do not leave it, for if you leave it you will never see me nor shall I see you until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"14145","Abdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took him along on the night of the jinn. They went until they reached the heights of Makka, where he drew a line for him and told him not to move. Then he advanced among the mountains of the jinn, and Ibn Masʿūd saw men descending upon him from the mountain tops until they stood between him and the Prophet ﷺ. He drew his sword, intending to strike until he rescued the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then remembered his order not to move until he came to him. He remained so until dawn broke. The Prophet ﷺ came while he was standing and asked whether he had remained in his state. He replied that had he stayed a month he would not have moved until he came to him, then informed him what he had intended to do. The Prophet ﷺ said had he left they would never have met until the Day of Resurrection. Then he interlaced his fingers with his and said he had been promised that men and jinn would believe in him; men had believed, and the jinn he had seen. He added that he thought his term had drawn near. Ibn Masʿūd asked whether he would not appoint Abū Bakr as successor, but he turned away, and he saw it did not meet approval. He asked about Umar, and he turned away again. He asked about ʿAlī, and the Prophet ﷺ swore by the One besides Whom there is no god that if they pledged him and obeyed him he would enter them all into Paradise. > **""Do not move until I come to you.""**"
"14146","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that a woman brought her son to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said he suffered fits that seized him at their food and spoiled it for them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped his chest, supplicated for him, and the boy vomited, with something like a black pup emerging from his mouth. He was healed."
"14147","In another narration of the same report the wording adds that he vomited, meaning he coughed."
"14148","Al-Wāziʿ رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with al-Ashajj, al-Mundhir son of Asim, or ʿAmir son of al-Mundhir, accompanied by an afflicted man. When they reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saw the Prophet ﷺ, they leapt from their mounts and kissed his hand. Then al-Ashajj dismounted, hobbled their mounts, opened his bag, and came to the Prophet ﷺ with greeting. The Prophet ﷺ told him he possessed two traits loved by Allah and His Messenger: forbearance and deliberation. He asked whether he had cultivated them or Allah had fashioned him upon them. The Prophet ﷺ replied that Allah had fashioned him upon them. He praised Allah for fashioning him upon two traits loved by Allah and His Messenger. Al-Wāziʿ then said he had with him an afflicted maternal uncle and asked him to supplicate to Allah for him. The Prophet ﷺ asked where he was and told him to bring him. He treated him as al-Ashajj had done, dressing him in his two garments and bringing him. The Prophet ﷺ lifted part of his cloak until the whiteness of his armpit was seen, then struck his back, ordering the enemy of Allah to come out. The man turned away, looking with the gaze of a sound man. > **""O Ashajj, in you are two traits loved by Allah and His Messenger: forbearance and deliberation.""**"
"14149","Umm Abān bint al-Wāziʿ رضي الله عنها reports from her father that her grandfather al-Wāziʿ set out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a mad son of his, or a sister's son. Her grandfather said that when they arrived at Madina he told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he had with him a mad nephew or sister's son, brought for him to supplicate to Allah for him. The Prophet ﷺ told him to bring him. He went to him among the riders, freed him, cast off his travel clothes, dressed him in two fine garments, took his hand, and brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ told him to bring him near with his back toward him. He seized the folds of his garment from top and bottom, struck his back until the whiteness of his armpits was seen, and kept saying, come out, enemy of Allah. The boy turned to look with sound gaze, unlike his former gaze. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated him before him, supplicated for him, wiped his face, and after his supplication none in the delegation was preferred over him. > **""Bring him near me, with his back toward me. Come out, enemy of Allah.""**"
"14150","ʿUthmān ibn Abi al-As رضي الله عنه reports complaining to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of forgetting the Qurʾān. He struck his chest with his hand and ordered the devil to come out of the chest of ʿUthmān. After that he never forgot anything he wished to remember. > **""O devil, come out of the chest of ʿUthmān.""**"
"14151","Sulayman ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Ahwas al-Azdi reports from his mother that she saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ casting the pebbles from the belly of the valley, with a man behind him shielding him from the people lest stones strike him, while he said that people should not kill one another and when they cast should cast with pebbles like sling pellets. Then a woman brought her son and said he was witless, asking him to supplicate to Allah for him. He told her to bring water. She brought water in a stone vessel. He spat into it, washed his face therein, supplicated over it, and told her to go wash him with it and seek healing from Allah. The narrator asked the woman to spare a little for her own son, took a little with her fingers, wiped her son's lip with it, and he became among the most dutiful of people. Asked afterward what became of the woman's son, she said he recovered with excellent recovery. > **""O people, let none of you kill another; when you cast, cast with pebbles like sling pellets.""**"
"14152","ʿAʾisha رضي الله عنها reports that the household of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a wild animal which, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out, played, ran hard, advanced and withdrew, but when it sensed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ it crouched and did not stir while he remained in the house, out of dislike of harming him."
"14153","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that an Ansari household owned a camel for drawing water which became refractory, denied them its back, while crops and palms thirsted. The Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and explained that their watering camel had become unmanageable and withheld its back. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told his companions to rise. They rose, and he entered the garden with the camel in its corner, walking toward it. The Ansar warned that it had become like a rabid dog and they feared its attack upon him. He replied that there was no harm upon him from it. When the camel looked at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ it advanced toward him until it fell prostrate between his hands. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ seized its forelock, the most docile it had ever been, until he returned it to work. His companions said a dumb beast without reason prostrated to him while they possessed reason, so they were more entitled to prostrate. He replied that it is not fit for a human to prostrate to a human; had it been fit he would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband for the greatness of his right upon her, for had he a wound from his foot to the crown of his head bursting with pus and matter, and she faced him and licked it, she would not have fulfilled his right. > **""It is not fit for a human to prostrate to a human; had it been fit I would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband.""**"
"14154","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that some people came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said their camel had broken loose in a garden. The Prophet ﷺ came to it and told it to come. It came with lowered head until he bridled it and handed it to its owners. Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه said it was as though it knew he was a prophet. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that none between its two lava plains but knows he is a prophet, except the disbelievers among jinn and men. > **""Come.""**"
"14155","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that an Ansari man owned two stallions in rut. He put them in a garden and shut the door upon them, then came to the Prophet ﷺ, who was seated with a group of Ansar, wishing him to supplicate that Allah subdue them for him. The Prophet ﷺ told his companions to rise with them. He went to the door and told him to open. The man feared for the Prophet ﷺ. He repeated the order, and he opened. One stallion stood near the door; when it saw the Prophet ﷺ it prostrated to him. The Prophet ﷺ asked for something to bind its head and enable the man over it. He brought a bridle, he bound its head and enabled him. Then he walked to the far end of the garden to the other stallion; when it saw him it fell prostrate. He told the man to bring something to bind its head, bound it, and enabled him. Then he told him to go, for they would never disobey him. When the companions of the Prophet ﷺ saw that, they asked why two unreasoning stallions should prostrate while they did not. He replied that he commands no one to prostrate to anyone; had he commanded anyone to prostrate to anyone he would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband. > **""Open. Bring me something with which to bind its head. Go, for they will never disobey you.""**"
"14156","Yaʿlā ibn Murra رضي الله عنه reports seeing three things from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which none had seen before him nor would see after him. Going with him on a journey, they passed a seated woman with her boy. She said the boy was afflicted with trial affecting them too, seized daily countless times. He asked her to hand him over. She lifted him to him, and he carried him between himself and the middle of the saddle, opened his mouth, blew into it thrice, and said in Allah's name, I am the servant of Allah, withhold, enemy of Allah. Then he returned him to her and told her to meet them on the return at that place and inform them how he fared. They went and returned to find her there with three sheep. Asked how her boy fared, she swore by the One Who sent him with truth that they had sensed nothing from him until that hour, and asked him to take the sheep. He told his companion to dismount, take one, and return the rest. Going out another day to the open gardens, when they emerged the Prophet ﷺ asked him to look for anything concealing him. He saw nothing but a tree he did not think would conceal him. Asked what was near it, he replied a like tree, or near it. He told him to go to them and say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands them to join by Allah's leave. They joined, he relieved himself, then returned and told him to order each to return to its place. They returned. Sitting with him another day, a camel came trotting until it set its neck before him, then its eyes shed tears. He told him to see whose camel it was, for it had a matter. He found its owner, a man of the Ansar, and summoned him. Asked about his camel, the man professed ignorance beyond working and watering upon it until it failed at watering, whereupon last night they resolved to slaughter it and divide its meat. He told him not to do so but to grant or sell it to him. The man granted it to him, O Messenger of Allah. He branded it with the charity brand and sent it on. > **""In the name of Allah, I am the servant of Allah, withhold, enemy of Allah.""**"
"14157","Yaʿlā رضي الله عنه reports in another narration that he did not think anyone saw from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but less than what he saw. He mentioned the like, except that he told the camel's owner that his camel complained he had worked it until grown old and now intended to slaughter it. The man affirmed by the One Who sent him with truth that he had intended so, and swore he would not do it. > **""What is the matter with your camel that it complains of you? It claims you worked it until grown old and now intend to slaughter it.""**"
"14158","In another narration, they travelled and alighted, and the Prophet ﷺ slept. A tree came splitting the earth until it covered him, then returned to its place. When he awoke and was informed, he said it was a tree that had sought its Lord's permission to greet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and permission was granted it. > **""It is a tree that sought its Lord's permission to greet the Messenger of Allah, and He permitted it.""**"
"14159","In one of his narrations al-Tabarani reports a camel passing him, stretching its neck and bellowing. He said, bring me its owner. When he came he said it reported being born among them, worked until grown old, whereupon they intended to slaughter it. He added that nothing exists but knows he is the Messenger of Allah, except the disbelievers, or the corrupt, among jinn and men. > **""Bring me its owner.""**"
"14160","Yaʿlā ibn Murra reports from his father رضي الله عنه that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ with a boy of hers afflicted with madness. The Prophet ﷺ said, come out, enemy of Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah. He recovered. She gifted him two rams, some ghee, and some dried yogurt. The Prophet ﷺ told him to take the dried yogurt, the ghee, and one of the rams, and return the other to her. > **""Come out, enemy of Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"14161","Murra رضي الله عنه reports by the same chain being with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey. He alighted and told him to go to those two small trees and say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands them to join. He went, spoke to them, and one leapt to the other and they joined. The Prophet ﷺ came out, screened himself by them, relieved himself, then each leapt back to its place. > **""Go to those two small trees and tell them the Messenger of Allah commands you to join.""**"
"14162","Yaʿlā ibn Siyāba رضي الله عنه reports travelling with the Prophet ﷺ when he wished to relieve himself. He commanded two young palm shoots, and one joined the other, then commanded them and they returned to their roots. A camel came setting its neck to the ground, rumbling until the ground around it grew wet. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether they knew what the camel said. It claimed its owner intended to slaughter it. He sent for its owner and asked whether he would grant it to him. The man replied no wealth was dearer to him. The Prophet ﷺ told him to treat it kindly. He pledged he would honor no wealth as he honored it, O Messenger of Allah. Passing a grave whose occupant was punished, the Prophet ﷺ said he was punished for no great matter. He ordered a palm stalk placed upon his grave and said perhaps it would lighten him while moist. > **""Do you know what the camel says? It claims its owner intends to slaughter it. Treat him kindly.""**"
"14163","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered a garden, and a camel came and prostrated to him. They said they were more entitled to prostrate. He replied that had he commanded anyone to prostrate to anyone he would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband. > **""Had I commanded anyone to prostrate to anyone, I would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband.""**"
"14164","Jābir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports returning with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a journey until they reached a garden of Banu al-Najjar containing a camel that charged whoever entered. They mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ. He came to the garden, called the camel, and it came with its lip to the ground until it knelt before him. The Prophet ﷺ called for a bridle, bridled it, handed it to its owner, then turned to the people and said nothing between heaven and earth but knows he is the Messenger of Allah, except the disobedient among jinn and men. > **""Bring a bridle.""**"
"14165","Jābir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports going with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the expedition of Dhat al-Riqa. At the lava plain of Waqim a bedouin woman met them with her son and told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Satan had overpowered him. He told her to bring him near, open his mouth, and he spat into it, saying thrice, be gone, enemy of Allah, while I am the Messenger of Allah. Then he told her to attend to her son, for nothing of what afflicted him would return to him. They alighted in a barren desert plain without trees, and the Prophet ﷺ told Jābir to seek a place for ablution. He found only two scattered trees which if joined would screen him. The Prophet ﷺ told him to go and tell them the Messenger of Allah ﷺ says to join. He did, and they joined as though in one root. The Prophet ﷺ relieved himself, returned, and told him to order them to return as they were. They returned. They alighted in a valley of Banu Muharib, where Ghaurath ibn al-Hārith of Banu Muharib confronted them while the Prophet ﷺ wore his sword. He asked Muhammad for the sword. He unsheathed and handed it to him. He shook it, looked at it a while, advanced, and asked what would prevent him from him. The Prophet ﷺ replied that Allah would prevent him from him. His hand trembled until the sword fell. The Prophet ﷺ took it and asked who would prevent him from him. He answered none, by his parents. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated Allah to suffice them against Ghaurath and his people. Returning, a companion of the Prophet ﷺ brought a birds' nest carrying chicks, with their parents following, alighting upon the man's hand. The Prophet ﷺ asked those with him whether they marvelled at the two birds' deed toward their chicks, swearing by the One Who sent him with truth that Allah is more merciful to His servants than those two birds to their chicks. Returning to the lava plain of Waqim, the bedouin woman who had brought her son met them with a skin of milk and a sheep as gift. Asked how her son fared and whether anything of his affliction had returned, she swore by the One Who sent him with truth that nothing had returned. He accepted her gift. At a descent of the lava plain a camel came pacing. He asked whether they knew what it said. They said Allah and His Messenger know best. He said it had come complaining against its master, claiming he had ploughed upon it for years until it grew mangy, emaciated, and old, and now intended to slaughter it. He told Jābir to go to its owner and bring him. Jābir said he did not know its owner. He said it would guide him. It went ahead trotting until it stopped him at the gathering of Banu Khatma. He asked for its lord; they named such son of such. He summoned him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ told him his camel complained that he had ploughed upon it for a time until mangy and emaciated and old, and now intended to slaughter it. He affirmed by the One Who sent him with truth that it was so. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him to sell it to him. He assented, and he bought it, then released it among the trees until it raised its hump. Whenever any riding camel of the Emigrants or Helpers ailed, he gave it to him. It remained so for a time. > **""Be gone, enemy of Allah, while I am the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"14166","Abdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports travelling with the Prophet ﷺ toward Makka. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to relieve himself he withdrew until none saw him. He sighted two distant trees and told Ibn Masʿūd to go and tell them the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands them to join so he may screen himself by them. One walked to the other, he relieved himself, then they returned to their places. Proceeding to the lanes of Madina, a camel came rushing until it prostrated to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then stood before him with tears flowing. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for its owner. They named such. He told them to summon him. When brought, the Prophet ﷺ told him it complained of him. The man said they had drawn water upon it for twenty years, then intended to slaughter it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that it complained so, an evil requital after twenty years' service until its bones thinned and its skin thinned, now to slaughter it, and asked him to sell it to him. He granted it to him, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered it turned toward the pasturing camels. His companions said the camel had prostrated while they were more entitled to prostrate. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that Allah forbid anyone prostrating to anyone; had anyone prostrated to anyone he would have commanded a wife to prostrate to her husband. > **""O son of Masʿūd, go to these two trees and tell them the Messenger of Allah commands you to join for his screening.""**"
"14167","ʿAʾisha رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among a group of Emigrants and Helpers when a camel came and prostrated to him. His companions said beasts and trees prostrated to him, so they were more entitled. He replied that they should worship their Lord and honor their brother. > **""Worship your Lord and honor your brother.""**"
"14168","Yaʿlā ibn Umayya رضي الله عنه reports travelling one day with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a camel appeared. Upon seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ it raised its head. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Yaʿlā to go to its people, buy it from them, and if they would not sell, tell them the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoins them concerning it. They swore by Allah they had drawn water upon it for twenty years and had intended to slaughter it in the morning, but since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had enjoined it, they would not fall short in good toward it. > **""O Yaʿlā, go to the people of this camel and buy it from them; if they will not sell, tell them the Messenger of Allah enjoins you concerning it.""**"
"14169","Yaʿlā رضي الله عنه reports by the same chain travelling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when three scattered acacias appeared. He told Yaʿlā to go and say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands them to join by Allah's leave. They walked until they became as one root, and he screened himself by them for his need. Then he told Yaʿlā to command them to return by Allah's leave. They walked until each returned to its stand. > **""O Yaʿlā, go to those acacias and say the Messenger of Allah commands you to join by Allah's leave.""**"
"14170","Burayda رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to show a sign. He told him to go to that tree and call it. He went and said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ calls you. It inclined on every side until its roots were uprooted, then advanced until it came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded it to return. The man rose, kissed his head, hands, and feet, and accepted Islam. > **""Go to that tree and call it.""**"
"14171","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that a man of Banu Amir who treated and cured came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked whether he might treat him for things he said. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invited him, then asked whether he might treat him. He assented, with palms and trees nearby. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called a cluster among them. It advanced toward him, prostrating and rising, prostrating and rising, until it reached him and stood before him. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told it to return to its place, and it returned. The man swore by Allah he would never belie anything he said thereafter, addressing Banu Amir ibn Sasa that he would never belie him thereafter. > **""Return to your place.""**"
"14172","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at al-Hajun, and the idolaters rejected him. He supplicated Allah to show him a sign that day after which he would not mind who belied him. He was told to call a tree. It advanced ploughing the earth until it reached him and greeted him, then by his command it returned to its growth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he would not mind thereafter who of his people belied him. > **""O Allah, show me a sign today after which I shall not mind who belies me.""**"
"14173","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reports going one morning with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until at the meeting of Madina roads they saw a bedouin holding his camel's bridle, standing before the Prophet ﷺ while they surrounded him. He greeted him as prophet with Allah's mercy and blessings. The Prophet ﷺ returned it and asked how he awoke. The camel bellowed, and a guard-like man came claiming the bedouin had stolen the camel. The camel bellowed and moaned for a while, to which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ listened attentively until it quieted. The Prophet ﷺ turned to the guard and told him to leave him, for the camel had testified against him that he lied. The guard departed. The Prophet ﷺ turned to the bedouin and asked what he had said when he came to him. He replied with his parents as ransom that he had said, O Allah, bless Muhammad until no blessing remains, O Allah, grant grace to Muhammad until no grace remains, O Allah, grant peace to Muhammad until no peace remains, O Allah, have mercy on Muhammad until no mercy remains. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that Allah had manifested it to him while the camel spoke its excuse, and the angels had filled the horizon. > **""Turn away from him, for the camel has testified against you that you lie.""**"
"14174","Al-Hakam ibn al-Hārith al-Sulami رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him concerning spoils. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed him while his she-camel had knelt and he was beating her. He told him not to beat her, and the Prophet ﷺ said, loose her. She rose and travelled with the people. > **""Do not beat her. Loose her.""**"
"14175","Jābir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports losing his camel at night. He passed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was fastening for ʿAʾisha, and he asked what was wrong, O Jābir. He said he had lost his camel, or his camel had gone, on a dark night. He told him his camel was there and to go take it. He went as directed but did not find it, returned, and swore he had not found it, by his parents, O Prophet of Allah. He repeated the order, and again he went and found nothing. He told him to wait at ease. When finished he took his hand, led him to the camel, handed it to him, and said this was his camel. The people had already marched. Riding behind upon his camel, which trotted with short steps, he exclaimed at his mother for having only a trotting camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode behind, heard him, caught up, and asked what he had said. He forgot, denied saying anything, then remembered and confessed his lament. The Prophet ﷺ struck the camel's rump with a whip, or his own whip, and it set off the swiftest or fastest camel he had ever ridden, tugging its bridle. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he would sell him that camel. He assented. Asked for how much, he said for an uqiyya. He exclaimed approval at an uqiyya for a watering camel. Jābir said no watering camel at Madina would please them in its place. The Prophet ﷺ said he had taken it for an uqiyya. Jābir dismounted to the ground. Asked his affair, he said it was his camel. He told him to ride his camel. He replied it was not his but his. He noted they would review a matter with him twice, but when he commanded a third time they did not review. He rode until he reached his aunt at Madina and told her he had sold their watering camel to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for an uqiyya. She seemed displeased, for it was an excellent watering camel. He took some twine, goaded it, seized its bridle, and led it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, finding him standing conversing with a man. He offered him his camel. He seized its bridle, then ordered Bilal to weigh out an uqiyya for Jābir in full. He went with Bilal, who weighed him an uqiyya in full. He returned while the Prophet ﷺ still spoke to the man and reported full payment. While so, he went home unawares, and the Prophet ﷺ called where is Jābir. Told he had gone to his family, he ordered him overtaken and brought. His messenger came running, calling him. He came, and the Prophet ﷺ told him to take his camel. He protested it was not his but his, O Messenger of Allah, thrice. He took it, and the Prophet ﷺ swore they had not profited him merely to dismount him. He came to his aunt with the watering camel and the uqiyya, telling her the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given him an uqiyya and returned the camel to him. > **""What is wrong with you, O Jābir? This is your camel, go and take it.""**"
"14176","ʿAlī, meaning ʿAlī ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه, reports seeing them on the day of Badr sheltering behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was nearest them to the enemy and among the mightiest of people in valor that day."
"14177","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was favored over people with four: generosity and courage, with the rest of the report mentioned earlier in marriage. > **""I have been favored over people with four: generosity and courage.""**"
"14178","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he would inform them of the most generous of the most generous. Allah is the most generous of the most generous, he is the most generous of Adam's children, and the most generous after him is a man who learns knowledge and spreads it, to be raised on Resurrection Day as a single community, and a man who expends himself in Allah's path until slain. > **""Shall I not inform you of the most generous of the most generous? Allah is the most generous of the most generous, and I am the most generous of Adam's children.""**"
"14179","Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr reports that Abū Usayd رضي الله عنه used to say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never refused anything he was asked."
"14180","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ was asked something he intended to do he said yes, and when he intended not to do it he fell silent, and he never said no to anything. > **""Yes.""**"
"14181","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reports that an Arab man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him for land between two mountains. He wrote it for him, and the man accepted Islam. Then he came to his people and told them to accept Islam, for he had come from a man who gives the giving of one who does not fear poverty. > **""Accept Islam, for I have come to you from a man who gives the giving of one who fears no poverty.""**"
"14182","Al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ رضي الله عنها reports that Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ sent her with a basket of ripe dates upon which were young cucumbers to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for the Prophet ﷺ loved cucumbers. Ornaments had arrived from Bahrayn, and he filled his hand from them and gave them to her."
"14183","In another narration, he gave her a handful of ornament or gold, and added, adorn yourself with this. > **""Adorn yourself with this.""**"
"14184","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ come to a cloth merchant, buy a shirt for four dirhams, and go out wearing it. A man of the Ansar said, O Messenger of Allah, clothe me with a shirt, may Allah clothe you of Paradise's garments. He removed the shirt and clothed him with it, then returned to the shopkeeper, bought another shirt for four dirhams, with two dirhams remaining. On the road he found a slave girl weeping. He asked what made her weep. She said her people had given her two dirhams to buy flour, which had perished. The Prophet ﷺ gave her the remaining two dirhams. She turned away weeping. He called her and asked why she wept after taking the two dirhams. She said she feared they would beat her. He walked with her to her people, greeted, and they recognized his voice. He greeted again and a third time, and they answered. He asked whether they had heard the first greeting. They replied yes, but they had wished him to increase greetings. He asked what had brought him, by their parents. He said the girl feared beating. Her owner said she was free for Allah's sake because of his walking with her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them glad tidings of good and Paradise, and said Allah had blessed the ten: Allah clothed His prophet with a shirt and an Ansari man with a shirt, freed a neck therefrom, and he praised Allah Who had granted this by His power. > **""What makes you weep? Allah has blessed the ten.""**"
"14185","Umm Sunbula رضي الله عنها reports bringing a gift to the Prophet ﷺ, but his wives refused to accept it, saying they would not take. The Prophet ﷺ commanded them and they took it. Then he granted her a valley as allotment, which Abdullah ibn Jahsh later bought from Hasan ibn ʿAlī."
"14186","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports gathering with al-ʿAbbās, Fatima, and Zayd ibn Hāritha before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-ʿAbbās said his age had advanced, his bones thinned, his burden grown, and asked him to order such and such wasq of food if he saw fit. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied they would do so. Fatima asked him to order for her as he had ordered for his uncle. He replied they would do so. Zayd ibn Hāritha said he had given him land from which he lived, then taken it back, and asked him to return it if he saw fit. He replied they would do so. ʿAlī added asking him to entrust him the right Allah had assigned him in His Book from the fifth, to divide in his place lest any dispute him after him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied they would do so. He entrusted it to him, and he divided it in his lifetime, then Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه entrusted it to him and he divided it. > **""We shall do so.""**"
"14187","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that when his father was slain, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned him and asked whether he loved dirhams. He replied yes. He said had wealth come to them he would have given him such and such. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died before giving him. When Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه was made successor, wealth came to him from Bahrayn, and he told him to take as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. He took. > **""Do you love dirhams? Had wealth come to us I would have given you such and such.""**"
"14188","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was sent only to perfect righteous morals, in another wording the noble morals. > **""I was sent only to perfect righteous morals.""**"
"14189","Safiyya bint Huyayy رضي الله عنها reports seeing none better in character than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She saw him mounting her behind his she-camel at night from Khaybar; as she dozed and her head struck the saddle's back, he touched her with his hand, saying gently, O daughter of Huyayy, gently. When they reached al-Sahbāʾ he apologized to her for what he had done to her people, explaining what they had said to him. > **""Gently, O daughter of Huyayy, gently.""**"
"14190","ʿAmr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would turn his face and speech toward even the worst of people to reconcile him thereby. He turned his face and speech toward him until he thought himself the best of the people. He asked whether he was better or Abū Bakr. He replied Abū Bakr. He asked about Umar, and he replied Umar. He asked about ʿUthmān, and he replied ʿUthmān. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered and turned from him, he wished he had never asked. > **""Abū Bakr. Umar. ʿUthmān.""**"
"14191","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never given choice between two matters but chose the easier of them."
"14192","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that none ever took the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but he kept his hand in his until the man released him. None ever saw his knees, or knee, protruding beyond his companion's knee. None ever shook his hand but he turned his face to him, then did not turn from him until he finished speaking."
"14193","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that a bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking aid in something, as Ikrima thought concerning blood. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him something, then asked whether he had done well to him. The bedouin replied no, nor beautifully. Some Muslims grew angry and moved to rise against him. The Prophet ﷺ signaled them to restrain. When the Prophet ﷺ rose to his house he summoned the bedouin inside and told him they had given him when he asked, yet he had said what he said. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ increased him something, then asked whether he had done well to him. The bedouin replied yes, may Allah reward him from kin and clan with good. The Prophet ﷺ told him his companions harbored something against him for it, so when he came he should say before them what he had said before him, to remove it from their breasts. He assented. When the bedouin came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said their companion had come, asked, been given, said what he said, then they had summoned and given him and he claimed satisfaction. He asked whether it was so. The bedouin replied yes, may Allah reward him from kin and clan with good. Abū Hurayra said the Prophet ﷺ then said his likeness and the bedouin's was like a man whose she-camel strayed, with people pursuing her only increasing her flight, until her owner said leave me with my she-camel, for I am gentler and more knowing with her. He turned to her, took herbage from the ground, called her until she came and obeyed, saddled and mounted her. Had he obeyed them where he said what he said, he would have entered the Fire. > **""Did I do well to you? Leave me with my she-camel, for I am gentler and more knowing with her.""**"
"14194","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade farewell to a man, he would take his hand and would not release his hand until the other man himself released the hand of the Prophet ﷺ."
"14195","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that he served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for nine years, and for anything displeasing he never said to him how ugly was what you did, nor for anything pleasing did he ever say how excellent was what you did."
"14196","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for ten years, never perceiving him to approve one thing or oppose another, but content with whatever Allah decreed, and if any of his wives said if only you had done such and such he would intervene on his servant's behalf, and he never saw him take revenge for himself except when a sanctity of Allah was violated, whereupon his anger for Allah was severest, and whenever two matters were presented to him he chose the easier so long as it entailed no displeasure of Allah, otherwise he kept furthest from it. > **""Leave him, for only what Allah Almighty wills shall come to be.""**"
"14197","Muhajir, the freedman of Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, reports that he served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for years, and for anything he did he never said why did you do it, nor for anything he left did he ever say why did you leave it."
"14198","Muhammad ibn Maslamah رضي الله عنه reports that he arrived from a journey, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took his hand and did not release his hand until he himself released his hand."
"14199","Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit reports that a group came to Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه asking him to narrate some hadith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon he replied asking what he should narrate, for he had been his neighbour, and when revelation descended he would send for him and he would write it, and when they spoke of the Hereafter he joined them in it, when they spoke of the world he joined them in it, and when they spoke of food he joined them in it, and all such conduct is what he could narrate of him."
"14200","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among the most smiling of people and the sweetest of them in spirit."
"14201","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said he jests yet speaks only truth, whereupon they said that he bantered with them, and he affirmed that he speaks only truth. > **""Indeed I jest, yet I speak only truth. Indeed I speak only truth.""**"
"14202","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that when revelation came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ or when he admonished, one would have said he was a warner of a people visited by punishment, yet when that state left him one saw him with the most radiant face, the most laughter, and the finest cheerfulness."
"14203","Abdullah ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would line up Abdullah, Ubaydullah and Kathir, the sons of al-Abbas, and announce a reward for whoever reached him first, whereupon they would race to him and fall upon his back and chest, and he would kiss them and embrace them. > **""Whoever reaches me first shall have such and such.""**"
"14204","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not turn around while walking, and sometimes his cloak would catch upon a tree or some object yet he would not turn until others lifted it, for they would jest and laugh, secure that he ﷺ would not turn toward them."
"14205","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more modest than a virgin behind her veil, and when he disliked something it was recognized in his face."
"14206","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more modest than a virgin behind her veil, and when he disliked something it was recognized in his face, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that modesty is entirely good. > **""Modesty is entirely good.""**"
"14207","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would bathe from behind the chambers, and his private area was never seen at all."
"14208","Harb ibn Surayj reports from a man of Baladawiyyah, from his grandfather, who said that he set out to Madinah and halted at the valley where two men stood disputing over a single goat, the buyer urging the seller to deal fairly with him, and he wondered within himself whether this Hashimi who had led people astray was present, then he looked and saw a man of handsome build, broad forehead, fine nose and fine eyebrows, with black hair like a black thread from the hollow of his throat to his navel, wrapped in two worn garments. He drew near and greeted them with peace, which they returned, and the buyer soon called upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to bid the seller deal fairly, whereupon he extended his hand and admonished them concerning their property and his hope of meeting Allah on the Day of Resurrection without any claim of wrongdoing in wealth, blood or honour, and invoked mercy upon the gentle seller, buyer, taker, giver, payer and claimant, then went on. The narrator resolved to follow him for the beauty of his speech, addressed him by name, and he turned to him fully asking what he wanted, whereupon he charged him with misleading and ruining people and barring them from what their fathers worshipped, and he answered that such worship belonged to Allah, and that he called the servants of Allah to Allah, testifying that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that he, Muhammad, is the Messenger of Allah, commanding belief in what was revealed to him, disbelief in al-Lat and al-Uzza, establishment of prayer and payment of zakah, which he explained as the rich returning wealth to their poor, and the narrator declared it an excellent call. He added that none on earth had been more hateful to him than he, yet he did not leave him until he became dearer to him than his child, his father and all mankind. He professed recognition, affirmed the testimony and belief, and told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he frequented a water visited by many people and would invite them to what he had invited him to, hoping they would follow him, and he permitted him to invite them, so the men and women of that water embraced Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stroked his head. > **""Peace be upon you. You own your wealth. I hope to meet Allah Almighty on the Day of Resurrection with none of you seeking from me anything wherein I wronged him in wealth, blood or honour, except by its right. May Allah have mercy upon a man who is gentle in selling, gentle in buying, gentle in taking, gentle in giving, gentle in paying, and gentle in claiming. What do you wish? That is Allah. I call the servants of Allah to Allah. I testify that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that I, Muhammad, am the Messenger of Allah, and you shall believe in what was revealed to me, disbelieve in al-Lat and al-Uzza, establish prayer and give zakah. Our rich shall return wealth to our poor. You have recognized. Do you testify that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that I, Muhammad, am the Messenger of Allah, and do you believe in what was revealed to me? Yes, so invite them.""**"
"14209","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that Jibrīl sat with the Prophet ﷺ and looked to the sky where an angel was descending, and Jibrīl said this angel had never descended since the day he was created until this hour, and when he descended he said that his Lord had sent him to make him a king prophet or a slave messenger, whereupon Jibrīl told him to humble himself before his Lord, and he chose a slave messenger. > **""O Muhammad, has your Lord sent me to you. Shall I make you a king prophet or a slave messenger? Humble yourself before your Lord, O Muhammad. Rather a slave messenger.""**"
"14210","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had he wished mountains of gold would have gone with him, for an angel whose girth equalled the Kaʿbah came saying his Lord greeted him with peace and gave him choice to be a slave prophet or a king prophet, and he looked to Jibrīl who signalled him to humble himself, so he said a slave prophet. Thereafter the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never ate reclining, saying he eats as the slave eats and sits as the slave sits. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, had I wished mountains of gold would have gone with me. Your Lord greets you with peace and says to you: if you wish a slave prophet, and if you wish a king prophet? Lower yourself. A slave prophet. I eat as the slave eats, and I sit as the slave sits.""**"
"14211","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that an angel descended from heaven who had never descended upon any prophet before him nor would descend upon anyone after him, and he was Isrāfīl, with Jibrīl alongside him, greeting him and conveying his Lord's command to choose whether to be a slave prophet or a king prophet, and he looked to Jibrīl who gestured to humble himself, and the Prophet ﷺ then said that had he said a king prophet the mountains would have gone with him as gold. > **""Peace be upon you, O Muhammad, I am a messenger of your Lord to you. He has commanded me to give you choice: if you wish a slave prophet, and if you wish a king prophet? Humble yourself. Had I said a king prophet, the mountains would have gone with me as gold.""**"
"14212","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in private converse with Jibrīl, the horizon of the sky split and Jibrīl drew near the earth swaying, and an angel stood before the Prophet ﷺ bidding him choose between a slave prophet or a king prophet, and Jibrīl signalled with his hand to humble himself, and knowing him to be his sincere adviser he said a slave prophet, whereupon that angel ascended to heaven. He then asked Jibrīl, whose state had preoccupied him from asking, who this was, and Jibrīl said this was Isrāfīl whom Allah created arrayed before Him with feet in rank, never raising his glance, with seventy lights between him and the Lord such that any light approached would burn, and before him a tablet which when Allah decrees anything in heaven or earth rises and strikes his forehead and he looks, directing to Jibrīl what is his task, to Mīkāʾīl what is his, and to the Angel of Death what is his. Asked over what Jibrīl is appointed, he said over wind and hosts; Mīkāʾīl over vegetation and rain; the Angel of Death over seizing souls; and he thought Isrāfīl was appointed for the Hour, and what was seen of Jibrīl was only fear of the Hour. > **""O Muhammad, your Lord commands you to choose between a slave prophet or a king prophet? Humble yourself. A slave prophet. O Jibrīl, I had wished to ask you of this, but what I saw of your state preoccupied me from asking, so who is this, O Jibrīl? Upon what are you appointed? Upon what is Mīkāʾīl? Upon what is the Angel of Death? Upon wind and hosts. Upon vegetation and rain. Upon seizing souls.""**"
"14213","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما used to narrate that Allah sent an angel of the angels with Jibrīl to the Prophet ﷺ, and the angel said that Allah gave him choice to be a slave prophet or a king prophet, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to Jibrīl as if consulting him, and he gestured to humble himself, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said rather a slave prophet, and thereafter the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never seen eating reclining until he joined his Lord. > **""O Muhammad, Allah gives you choice between being a slave prophet or a king prophet? Humble yourself. Rather a slave prophet.""**"
"14214","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was given choice between this world and the Hereafter, and he chose the Hereafter."
"14215","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was brought the keys of the world upon a piebald horse bearing a velvet cloth of sundus. > **""I was brought the keys of the world upon a piebald horse, upon it a cloth of sundus.""**"
"14216","Abu Ghālib reports that he asked Abu Umāmah رضي الله عنه to narrate a hadith heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said the discourse of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the Qurʾān, with abundant remembrance, brief address, lengthy prayer, and he felt no disdain nor arrogance to walk with the indigent and the weak until he had fulfilled their need."
"14217","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ used to ride a donkey named ʿUfayr."
"14218","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a donkey named ʿUfayr."
"14219","Abu Mūsā رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would ride the donkey, wear wool, milk the sheep, and attend to the guest's care himself."
"14220","Jarīr رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ from before him and trembling seized him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him to ease himself, for he was no king but only the son of a Qurashi woman who used to eat dried meat. > **""Ease yourself, for I am no king. I am only the son of a woman of Quraysh who used to eat dried meat.""**"
"14221","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that if a man from the outlying hills invited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at midnight for barley bread, he would accept."
"14222","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that he sits upon the ground, eats upon the ground, ties the sheep, and accepts the invitation of the slave even for barley bread."
"14223","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would accept the invitation of the slave."
"14224","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reports that a man called the Prophet ﷺ thrice, and each time he answered him with ready obedience. > **""Labbayk, labbayk.""**"
"14225","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jubayr al-Khuzāʿī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was walking among some of his Companions when he was screened with a garment, and when he saw its shadow he raised his head and found a sheet held over him, whereupon he said hush to him, took the garment and laid it aside, saying he was only a mortal like them. > **""Hush. I am only a mortal like you.""**"
"14226","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said he is only a slave and eats as the slave eats. > **""I am only a slave. I eat as the slave eats.""**"
"14227","Abu Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports that there was a woman who wrangled with men and was foul tongued, and she passed the Prophet ﷺ while he was eating tharīd upon a mat, remarking that he sat as the slave sits and ate as the slave eats, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked what slave is more enslaved than he. She complained that he ate and did not feed her, so he said eat, and she demanded he hand her with his hand, which he did, then demanded he feed her from what was in his mouth, which he gave her and she ate, and thereafter modesty overcame her so she never wrangled with anyone until she died. > **""And what slave is more enslaved than I? Eat.""**"
"14228","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reports to love them with love of Islam, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to raise him above his right, for Allah took him as a slave before He took him as a messenger. > **""Do not raise me above my right, for Allah Almighty took me as a slave before He took me as a messenger.""**"
"14229","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had he been invited for a shank he would have accepted. > **""Had I been invited for a shank, I would have accepted.""**"
"14230","Ḥanẓalah رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saw him sitting cross legged."
"14231","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would walk in turn apart from his riding companion."
"14232","ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah رضي الله عنه reports that he went out with the Prophet ﷺ to the mosque when his sandal strap broke, so he took his sandal to mend it, but he took it from his hand saying it was preferential treatment and he did not love preferential treatment. > **""It is preferential treatment, and I do not love preferential treatment.""**"
"14233","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه said he did not know how long the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would remain among them, so he suggested he take a trellis to shade him, whereupon he said he would remain among them with them treading his heels and contesting his cloak until Allah relieved him of them. > **""I shall remain among them with them treading my heels and contesting my cloak, until Allah relieves me of them.""**"
"14234","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that twenty youths of the Anṣār kept to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his needs, and when he intended a matter he would dispatch them in it."
"14235","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reports that five or four of his Companions would not part from the Prophet ﷺ or from the door of the Prophet ﷺ."
"14236","Abu Saʿīd رضي الله عنه reports that they took turns attending the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his need or for him to send them on a matter, until those awaiting reward and turns grew many, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them while they were discussing the Dajjāl and asked what this whispering was and whether he had not forbidden them whispering. > **""What is this whispering? Have I not forbidden you whispering?""**"
"14237","ʿĀṣim ibn Sufyān reports that he heard Abu al-Dardāʾ or Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه say that he sought permission of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to spend the night at his door so he might wake him for his need, and he permitted him, and he spent a night there."
"14238","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen, he went out with him advising him while Muʿādh rode and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked beneath his mount, and when he finished he said that perhaps he would not meet him after this year of his and might pass by this mosque of his and his grave, whereupon Muʿādh wept in anguish at parting from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He then turned and faced Madinah saying the nearest of people to him are the Godfearing, whoever and wherever they are, and in one chain he added weep not, for weeping is from Satan. > **""O Muʿādh, perhaps you shall not meet me after this year of mine, and perhaps you shall pass by this mosque of mine and my grave. The nearest of people to me are the Godfearing, whoever they are and wherever they are. Weep not, O Muʿādh, for weeping is from Satan.""**"
"14239","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that he was with the Prophet ﷺ on the night of the deputation of the jinn, and when he turned back he breathed deeply, so he asked what was the matter and he said his own death had been announced to him. > **""My own death has been announced to me, O Ibn Masʿūd.""**"
"14240","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that when the verse When the help of Allah and the victory comes was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his own death had been announced to him, meaning he would be taken in that year. > **""My own death has been announced to me.""**"
"14241","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that when When the help of Allah and the victory comes was revealed to its end, it was said his own death was announced to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon its revelation, so he exerted himself most intensely ever in the affair of the Hereafter. After that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said victory had come, the help of Allah had come, and the people of Yemen had come, and asked about them he said they are a people of delicate hearts and tender affections, with faith and understanding being Yemeni, with an added word that wisdom too is Yemeni. > **""Victory has come, and the help of Allah has come, and the people of Yemen have come. A people of delicate hearts and tender affections. Faith and understanding are Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni.""**"
"14242","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that when When the help of Allah and the victory comes was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها and said his own death had been announced to him, whereupon she wept, and he told her not to weep for she would be the first of his household to join him, whereupon she laughed. One of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ saw her weeping then laughing and questioned her, and she recounted that he told her his death had been announced so she wept, then he said she would be the first of his household to join him so she laughed. > **""My own death has been announced to me. Weep not, for you shall be the first of my household to join me.""**"
"14243","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that before he died the Messenger of Allah ﷺ frequently said glory be to You O Allah and with Your praise, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. She asked why he repeated it so often, and he said he had been commanded with a command, then recited When the help of Allah and the victory comes. > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise. I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. I have been commanded with a command.""**"
"14244","Abu Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of the conquest that this was what his Lord had promised him, then recited When the help of Allah and the victory comes, saying that when people entered the religion of Allah in crowds and the religion of Allah prevailed over all religion, then people were good and they were good. > **""This is what my Lord promised me. When people enter the religion of Allah in crowds, and the religion of Allah prevails over all religion, then people are good and we are good.""**"
"14245","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that she used to say that in the illness in which he was taken, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها to lean toward him, so she leaned and he confided to her awhile, then she withdrew weeping while ʿĀʾishah was present. After awhile the Messenger of Allah ﷺ again said lean toward him, my daughter, so she leaned and he confided to her awhile, then she withdrew laughing. ʿĀʾishah asked what her father had confided, but Fāṭimah said he had confided a secret and she would not disclose his secret while he lived, which weighed upon ʿĀʾishah that it be a secret apart from her. When Allah took him, ʿĀʾishah asked Fāṭimah to inform her of that report, and she agreed now, saying he first told her that Jibrīl used to review the Qurʾān with him once each year and this year had reviewed it with him twice, and told her that he had informed him that no prophet lived but half the age of the one before him, and told her that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam lived one hundred and twenty years and he saw himself departing at the head of sixty, which made her weep, whereupon he said that no woman among Muslim women bore a greater affliction than hers, so she should be second to no woman in patience. > **""Lean toward me, my daughter. Lean toward me, my daughter. Jibrīl used to review the Qurʾān with me once each year, and this year he has reviewed it with me twice. He informed me that no prophet lived but half the age of the one before him. ʿĪsā ibn Maryam lived one hundred and twenty years, and I see myself departing at the head of sixty. My daughter, there is no woman among Muslim women of greater affliction than you, so be second to no woman in patience.""**"
"14246","Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه reports that he saw in a dream as though the earth were being drawn up to heaven with strong ropes, and he recounted it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said it was the death of his nephew. > **""That is the death of your nephew.""**"
"14247","Abu Muwayhibah, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for him in the depth of night saying he had been commanded to seek forgiveness for the people of al-Baqīʿ and bade him go with him. He went with him, and when he stood among them he greeted the dwellers of the graves, congratulating them upon their state compared with what people had entered, for had they known what Allah had saved them from, trials approaching like cuts of dark night with each last following the first and the last worse than the first. He then turned to him saying he had been given the keys of the treasuries of the world and abiding therein then Paradise, and was given choice between that and meeting his Lord Almighty and Paradise. He urged him to take the keys of the world and abiding therein then Paradise, but he swore he had chosen meeting his Lord then Paradise. He then sought forgiveness for the people of al-Baqīʿ and turned back, and upon morning began the pain in which Allah Almighty took him. > **""O Abu Muwayhibah, I have been commanded to seek forgiveness for the people of al-Baqīʿ, so go with me. Peace be upon you, O people of the graves. Congratulation to you for what you have entered compared with what people have entered. Had you known what Allah has saved you from: trials approach like cuts of dark night, the last following the first, the last worse than the first. O Abu Muwayhibah, I have been given the keys of the treasuries of the world and abiding therein, then Paradise, and I have been given choice between that and meeting my Lord Almighty and Paradise. No, by Allah, O Abu Muwayhibah, I have chosen meeting my Lord, then Paradise.""**"
"14248","In another narration from him he said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to pray over the people of al-Baqīʿ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over them thrice, and at the third he said to saddle his mount for him. He rode and the narrator walked until they reached them, then he dismounted from his mount while the narrator held the animal, thereafter narrating likewise. > **""O Abu Muwayhibah, saddle my mount for me.""**"
"14249","Abu Wāqid al-Laythī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a servant among the servants of Allah was given choice between the world with its dominion and bliss and the Hereafter, and he chose the Hereafter, whereupon Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه said they would ransom him with their wealth and selves. > **""A servant among the servants of Allah was given choice between the world, its dominion and its bliss, and the Hereafter, and he chose the Hereafter.""**"
"14250","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah has roaming angels who convey to him the greeting from his nation, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his life is good for them as they speak and speech is relayed for them, and his passing is good for them as their deeds are presented to him, so whatever good he sees he praises Allah for, and whatever evil he sees he seeks forgiveness of Allah for them. > **""Indeed Allah has roaming angels who convey to me the greeting from my nation. My life is good for you: you speak and speech is relayed for you. My passing is good for you: your deeds are presented to me, so whatever good I see I praise Allah for, and whatever evil I see I seek forgiveness of Allah for you.""**"
"14251","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that their beloved and Prophet, for whom his father and soul be ransom, was announced to them six days before his death, and when parting drew near he gathered them in the house of their mother ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, looked at them and his eyes filled with tears, then welcomed them with supplications for life, protection, shelter, help, guidance, provision, success, peace and acceptance, enjoining Godfearing, commending them to Allah and appointing Him guardian over them, declaring himself a clear warner not to exalt themselves over Allah among His servants and lands, citing the Abode of the Hereafter for those who seek no exaltation nor corruption with the outcome for the Godfearing, and asking is there not in Hell an abode for the arrogant. He then said the term had drawn near with return to Allah, to Sidrat al-Muntahā, to Jannat al-Maʾwā, to the fullest cup and to the Highest Companion. Asked who would wash him, he said the men of his household, nearest then nearest; asked in what they would shroud him, he said in these garments of his if they wished, or in a Yemeni suit, or in white Egyptian cloth. Asked who among them would pray over him, they wept and he wept and told them to wait, with forgiveness and reward invoked, instructing that when they had washed him and placed him upon his bier in this house of his at the edge of his grave, they should leave him awhile, for the first to pray over him would be his beloved and companion Jibrīl, then Mīkāʾīl, then Isrāfīl, then the Angel of Death with his hosts, then the angels altogether, then they should enter upon him in companies, praying and greeting with full greeting, without harming him with a wailing woman nor shrieking nor lamentation, with the men of his household beginning the prayer over him then the rest, conveying greeting from him to themselves and to his absent brothers and to all who entered their religion after him and all who followed him upon his religion from this day until the Day of Resurrection. Asked who would lower him into his grave, he said the men of his household with many angels seeing them whence they could not see them. > **""Welcome to you. May Allah greet you, preserve you, shelter you, help you, guide you, provide for you, grant you success, keep you safe, and accept from you. I enjoin upon you Godfearing, and I commend you to Allah and appoint Him guardian over you. I am to you a clear warner: do not exalt yourselves over Allah among His servants and His lands. That is the Abode of the Hereafter which We assign to those who seek no exaltation in the earth nor corruption, and the outcome is for the Godfearing. Is there not in Hell an abode for the arrogant? The term has drawn near, and the return to Allah, and to Sidrat al-Muntahā, and to Jannat al-Maʾwā, and to the fullest cup, and to the Highest Companion. The men of my household, nearest then nearest. In these garments of mine, if you wish, or in a Yemeni suit, or in white Egyptian cloth. Gently, may Allah forgive you and reward you well on behalf of your Prophet. When you have washed me and placed me upon my bier in this house of mine at the edge of my grave, then leave me awhile, for the first to pray over me shall be my beloved and companion Jibrīl, then Mīkāʾīl, then Isrāfīl, then the Angel of Death with his hosts, then the angels altogether in full, then enter upon me in companies and pray over me and greet with full greeting. Do not harm me with a wailing woman, nor a shrieker, nor a lamenter. Let the men of my household begin prayer over me, then you after, and convey greeting from me to yourselves, and to my absent brothers convey greeting from me, and to whoever enters with you into your religion after me, I call you to witness that I convey greeting to him and to every one who follows me upon my religion, from this day of mine until the Day of Resurrection. The men of my household, with many angels seeing you whence you do not see them.""**"
"14252","Al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him and he went out to him finding him feverish with his head bound, and he said take his hand, O Faḍl, so he took his hand until he reached the pulpit and sat upon it, then told him to call out among the people, so he called and people gathered, and after praising Allah he said rights due from among them had drawn near upon him, so whomever he had flogged upon his back then here was his back for requital, whomever he had reviled in honour then here was his honour for requital, and from whomever he had taken wealth then here was his wealth for requital, and none should say he feared ill feeling from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for ill feeling was neither his nature nor his way, and dearest to him was he who took a right if it was his or absolved him so he might meet Allah with a clear soul, and he did not see that sufficing him until he stood among them repeatedly. He then descended and prayed Ẓuhr, returned to the pulpit repeating his words on ill feeling and other matters, then said whoever held anything should return it and not speak of worldly disgrace, for worldly disgrace is easier than that of the Hereafter. A man claimed three dirhams owed by him, and he said they neither belie a claimant nor put him to oath but asked how it arose, and he recalled a pauper passing by whom he had ordered paid, so he told al-Faḍl to pay him. Another man confessed to three misappropriated dirhams in the path of Allah, and asked why he had done so he pleaded need, so he told al-Faḍl to take them. He then said whoever feared anything of himself should rise that he might supplicate for him, so a man rose confessing lying, hypocrisy and sleepiness, and he prayed for truthfulness, faith and removal of sleep when intended. Another rose confessing lying, hypocrisy and every manner of deed, and when ʿUmar رضي الله عنه rebuked him for disgracing himself, he told Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb to desist, for worldly disgrace is easier than that of the Hereafter, then prayed for truthfulness, faith and turning his affair to good. ʿUmar spoke a word to them, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ʿUmar is with him and he is with ʿUmar, and truth after him is with ʿUmar wherever he is. In the closing addition a timid sleepless man rose and he prayed for him until al-Faḍl later saw him bravest and least sleeping. He then went to the house of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, spoke to the women as to the men, invited whoever was overcome by anything to ask that he supplicate, a woman gestured to her tongue and he prayed for her, and she thereafter would counsel good prayer. > **""Take my hand, O Faḍl. Call out among the people. O you people, rights due from among you have drawn near upon me. Whomever I have flogged upon his back, then here is my back, so let him requite from it. Whomever I have reviled in honour, then here is my honour, so let him requite from it. And from whomever I have taken wealth, then here is my wealth, so let him requite from it. Let no man say: I fear ill feeling from the Messenger of Allah. Ill feeling is neither my nature nor my way. The dearest of you to me is he who takes a right if it is his, or absolves me, so I meet Allah with a clear soul. I do not see that sufficing me until I stand among you repeatedly. O you people, whoever holds anything, let him return it, and let him not speak of worldly disgrace, for worldly disgrace is easier than that of the Hereafter. We neither belie a claimant nor put him to oath, but how did it become due from me? Pay him, O Faḍl. Why did you misappropriate? Take them, O Faḍl. O you people, whoever fears anything of himself, let him rise that I supplicate for him. O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith, and remove sleep from him when he intends. Gently, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, worldly disgrace is easier than that of the Hereafter. O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith, and turn his affair to good. ʿUmar is with me, and I am with him, and truth after me is with ʿUmar wherever he is. Whoever is overcome by anything, let him ask us that we supplicate for him.""**"
"14253","Jabir and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهم report concerning His word When the help of Allah and the victory comes until its end that when it was revealed to Muhammad ﷺ he said his soul had been announced, and Jibril said the Hereafter is better for him than the first and his Lord would soon give him so he would be pleased. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then commanded Bilal رضي الله عنه to call for congregational prayer, so the Emigrants and Helpers gathered at the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he prayed with the people, ascended the pulpit, praised Allah Almighty and extolled Him, then delivered a sermon at which hearts trembled and eyes wept, then asked what manner of prophet he had been to them. They answered with profuse gratitude that he had been to them as a merciful father and as a sincere compassionate brother, fulfilling the messages of Allah Almighty, conveying His revelation, and calling to the path of his Lord with wisdom and fair admonition, invoking for him the best reward granted a prophet on behalf of his nation. He then adjured them by Allah and by his right over them that whoever held a wrong from him should rise and requite from him, and none rose, then a second and a third adjuration that whoever held a wrong should rise and requite before the requital of the Resurrection. Then an aged shaykh called Ukkashah rose from among the Muslims, stepped through them until he stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, excusing himself that only repeated adjuration had moved him forward, recalling an expedition after Allah had granted victory when his she-camel came alongside his, and he dismounted to kiss his thigh, whereupon he raised the stick and struck his flank, unsure whether intentional or aimed at the camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared he would never intentionally strike him and told Bilal to go to the house of Fatimah رضي الله عنها and bring the trimmed stick. Bilal left the mosque with his hand upon his head calling out that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was offering requital from himself, knocked at Fatimah's door asking for the trimmed stick, and when she asked what her father would do with a stick on neither a pilgrimage nor a battle day, he told her he was bidding people farewell, departing the world and offering requital from himself. Fatimah said who could bear to requite the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and bade Bilal tell al-Hasan and al-Husayn to stand to be requited in his stead rather than let him be requited from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Bilal returned to the mosque, handed the stick to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ handed it to Ukkashah. When Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما saw that they rose offering themselves before him for requital rather than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them to proceed, for Allah knew their place and station. Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه then rose offering himself alive before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his back and belly for requital by his hand with a hundred lashes rather than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but the Prophet ﷺ told him to sit, for Allah knew his station and intention. Al-Hasan and al-Husayn رضي الله عنهما then rose saying he knew they were the grandsons of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and requital from them was as requital from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but the Prophet ﷺ told them to sit, O coolness of his eyes, invoking Allah not to forget this station of theirs. The Prophet ﷺ then told Ukkashah to strike if he would strike, and he replied that he had struck him while his belly was bare, so he ﷺ uncovered his belly while the Muslims cried aloud asking whether Ukkashah would strike the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When Ukkashah saw the whiteness of the belly of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as though fine linen, he could not refrain from falling upon him, kissing his belly and declaring his ransom and asking who could bear to requite him. The Prophet ﷺ said either to strike or to pardon, and he pardoned him hoping Allah would pardon him on the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet ﷺ then said whoever was pleased to look at his companion in Paradise should look at this shaykh, whereupon the Muslims rose kissing between the eyes of Ukkashah and congratulating him upon attaining high degrees and companionship of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ fell ill that very day for eighteen days with people visiting him, he having been born on Monday, commissioned on Monday and passing on Monday. On Sunday his illness grew heavy, Bilal called the adhan then stood at the door asking peace upon him and whether to establish prayer, and Fatimah told him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was occupied with himself. Bilal entered the mosque, and when dawn brightened he swore he would not establish it until he sought permission of his master the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he returned to the door calling for prayer with mercy invoked, and upon hearing Bilal the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to enter, for he was occupied with himself, and to command Abu Bakr to pray with the people. Bilal went out with his hand upon his head lamenting loss of help, severed hope and broken back, wishing his mother had never borne him or he had never witnessed this day from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then told Abu Bakr that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to lead the people. Abu Bakr advanced, being tender hearted, and when he saw the place of the Prophet ﷺ empty he could not control himself and fell unconscious, the Muslims cried aloud, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard their clamour and was told it was weeping at his absence, he summoned Ali ibn Abi Talib and al-Abbas, leaned upon them to the mosque, prayed brief units, then turned with a kindly face entrusting them to Allah, assured of His grace and protection, with Allah their guardian after him, enjoining Godfearing and obedience after him, for he was parting the world on the first day of the Hereafter and the last day of this world. On Monday the matter intensified, and Allah Almighty revealed to the Angel of Death to descend to His beloved and chosen Muhammad ﷺ in the fairest form and to be gentle in seizing his soul. The Angel of Death descended, stood at the door in the likeness of a bedouin, greeting the household of prophethood, the source of the message and the resort of angels, asking entry. Aishah told Fatimah to answer the man, and Fatimah twice answered that Allah would reward his walking but the Messenger of Allah was occupied with himself. At the third call declaring entry inevitable, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard the voice of the Angel of Death and asked Fatimah who was at the door, and she described a man seeking entry repeatedly, at whose third call her skin had shuddered and her limbs trembled. The Prophet ﷺ told her this was the demolisher of pleasures, the splitter of gatherings, the widower of wives, the orphaner of children, the ruiner of houses and the builder of graves, the Angel of Death, and bade him enter with mercy. The Angel of Death entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked whether he had come visiting or seizing, and he answered both, commanded by Allah Almighty neither to enter upon him nor to seize his soul except by his permission, returning otherwise to his Lord. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked where he had left his beloved Jibril, and he said in the lowest heaven with the angels consoling him for him. Jibril soon came and sat at his head, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said this was departure from the world and asked for glad tidings of what was for him with Allah. Jibril gave tidings that the doors of heaven stood opened with angels arrayed in rows with greeting and sweet basil saluting his soul, then that the doors of the Gardens stood opened with rivers flowing, trees lowered and houris adorned for the coming of his soul, then that he would be the first intercessor and first granted intercession on the Day of Resurrection, at each of which he praised the Countenance of his Lord and asked for more. Jibril asked what his care was, and he asked about his grief over the reciters of the Quran after him, the fasters of Ramadan, the pilgrims of the Sacred House, and his chosen nation. Jibril bade him rejoice, for Allah Almighty had forbidden Paradise to all prophets and nations until he and his nation entered it, whereupon his soul was content and he told the Angel of Death to proceed as commanded. Ali asked, when he was taken, who would wash him, in what they would shroud him, who would pray over him and who would lower him into the grave, and the Prophet ﷺ said as for washing Ali should wash him with al-Fadl ibn Abbas pouring water over him and Jibril as their third, then shroud him in three new garments with Jibril bringing embalming from Paradise, then place him upon the bier in the mosque and go out, for the first to pray over him would be the Lord Almighty from above His Throne, then Jibril, then Mikail, then Israfil, then the angels in companies, then they should enter and stand in rows with none preceding over him. Fatimah lamented the parting and asked when she would meet him, and he said she would meet him on the Day of Resurrection at the Hawd while he gave drink to whoever came to it of his nation, and if not there then at the Balance while he interceded for his nation, and if not there then at the Sirat while he called upon his Lord to save his nation from the Fire. The Angel of Death then set to seizing the soul of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when it reached the knees he cried in pain, when it reached the navel he cried in anguish with Fatimah echoing his distress, and when it reached the throat he addressed Jibril upon the severity of the bitterness of death. Jibril turned his face from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when asked whether he disliked looking at him, Jibril answered that no soul could bear to look at him while he endured the agonies of death. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was then taken, Ali ibn Abi Talib washed him with Ibn Abbas pouring water and Jibril with them, he was shrouded in three new garments, carried upon a bier, brought into the mosque, placed there and the people went out. The first to pray over him was the Lord Blessed and Exalted from above His Throne, then Jibril, then Mikail, then Israfil, then the angels in companies. Ali said they heard a murmur in the mosque without seeing a person, then heard a caller bid them enter with mercy and pray over their Prophet ﷺ, so they entered and stood row upon row as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded, magnifying with the magnification of Jibril and praying over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the prayer of Jibril, with none preceding over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Ali ibn Abi Talib and Ibn Abbas entered the grave, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was buried. When the people left, Fatimah asked Ali whether they had buried the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and upon his assent she asked how their souls had borne scattering dust over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whether no mercy for him remained in their breasts when he had been the teacher of good. He affirmed yet said it was the command of Allah which none repels, and she wept and lamented for her father that now Jibril was cut off who used to bring them revelation from heaven. > **""O Jibril, my soul has been announced. The Hereafter is better for you than the first, and your Lord shall soon give you so you shall be pleased. O you people, what manner of prophet was I to you? O company of Muslims, I adjure you by Allah and by my right over you: whoever holds a wrong from me, let him rise and requite from me. O company of Muslims, I adjure you by Allah and by my right over you: whoever holds a wrong from me, let him rise and requite from me before the requital on the Day of Resurrection. I seek you refuge by the majesty of Allah lest the Messenger of Allah intentionally strike you. O Bilal, go to the house of Fatimah and bring me the trimmed stick. O daughter of the Messenger of Allah, hand me the trimmed stick. O Fatimah, how heedless you are of what your father is in: the Messenger of Allah bids people farewell, departs the world and offers requital from himself. O Bilal, then tell al-Hasan and al-Husayn to stand to this man to be requited from them, and not let him requite from the Messenger of Allah. O Ukkashah, here we are before you, so requite from us and do not requite from the Messenger of Allah. Proceed, O Abu Bakr, and you, O Umar, proceed, for Allah has known your place and your station. O Ukkashah, I am alive before the Messenger of Allah, and my soul cannot bear that you strike the Messenger of Allah, so here is my back and my belly, requite from me by your hand, flog me a hundred, and do not requite from the Messenger of Allah. O Ali, sit, for Allah has known your station and your intention. O Ukkashah, do you not know that we are the grandsons of the Messenger of Allah, and requital from us is as requital from the Messenger of Allah? Sit, O coolness of my eyes, may Allah not forget this station of yours. O Ukkashah, strike if you are striking. Either strike or pardon. Whoever is pleased to look at my companion in Paradise, let him look at this shaykh. Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and mercy of Allah, shall I establish prayer? Enter, O Bilal, the Messenger of Allah today is occupied with himself. Command Abu Bakr to pray with the people. O Abu Bakr, the Messenger of Allah commands you to pray with the people. O company of Muslims, I entrust you to Allah. You are in the grace of Allah and His protection. Allah is your guardian after me. O company of Muslims, upon you is Godfearing and guarding obedience after me, for I part the world. This is the first day of the Hereafter and the last day of this world. Peace be upon you, O household of prophethood, and source of the message, and resort of the angels, may I enter? May Allah reward your walking, O servant of Allah, the Messenger of Allah is occupied with himself. O Fatimah, who is at the door? O Fatimah, do you know who is at the door? This is the demolisher of pleasures, the splitter of gatherings, this is the widower of wives, the orphaner of children, this is the ruiner of houses and the builder of graves, this is the Angel of Death. Enter, with mercy of Allah upon you, O Angel of Death. Have you come to me visiting or seizing? Where have you left my beloved Jibril? This is departure from the world, so give me glad tidings of what is for me with Allah. To the Countenance of my Lord be praise, so give me glad tidings, O Jibril. Now my soul is content. Come, O Angel of Death, and proceed to what you were commanded. O Ali, as for washing, wash me yourself, with al-Fadl ibn Abbas pouring water over you, and Jibril as your third. When you finish washing me, shroud me in three new garments, and Jibril shall bring me embalming from Paradise. When you place me upon the bier, place me in the mosque and go out, for the first to pray over me shall be the Lord Almighty from above His Throne, then Jibril, then Mikail, then Israfil, then the angels in companies. Then enter and stand in rows, with none preceding over me. My daughter, you shall meet me on the Day of Resurrection at the Hawd, while I give drink to whoever comes to me at the Hawd of my nation. You shall meet me at the Balance, while I intercede for my nation. You shall meet me at the Sirat, while I call: Lord, save my nation from the Fire. O Jibril, how severe is the bitterness of death. O Jibril, did you dislike looking at me? Enter, with mercy of Allah upon you, and pray over your Prophet.""**"
"14254","Yazid ibn Babanus reports that he and a companion went to Aishah رضي الله عنها, sought permission, and she cast a cushion to them and drew the veil to herself, and he asked her about intimacy with a menstruating woman. She then said that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by her door he would sometimes cast a word by which Allah benefited, but one day he passed saying nothing, then again saying nothing twice or thrice. She bade her slave girl place a cushion at the door and bound her head, and when he passed her he asked what ailed her, and she said she complained of her head, and he said he too complained of his head. He went away and before long he was brought carried in a cloak, entered, and sent for the women saying he had fallen ill and could not go round among them, so they should permit him to stay with Aishah, which they did. She nursed him as she had nursed none before, and one day while his head lay upon her shoulder it inclined toward her head, and thinking he wanted something from her head, a cold drop came from his mouth falling upon the hollow of her throat, which made her skin shudder, and thinking he had swooned she covered him with a garment. Umar and al-Mughirah ibn Shubah sought permission and she admitted them and drew the veil, and Umar looked at him lamenting his swoon and how severe it was, then rose, and near the door al-Mughirah asked Umar whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had died, and he denied him saying he was a man agitated by tribulation and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not die until Allah annihilated the hypocrites. Then Abu Bakr came, lifted the veil, looked at him and said they belong to Allah and to Him they return, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had died. He came from the side of his head, lowered his mouth and kissed his forehead, then lamented his son, then raised his head and again lowered his mouth kissing his forehead lamenting his chosen one, then again kissing his forehead lamenting his intimate friend, affirming the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had died. He went out to the mosque while Umar addressed the people saying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not die until Allah annihilated the hypocrites. Abu Bakr spoke, praised Allah and extolled Him, then cited that he is dead and they are dead, and that Muhammad is only a messenger before whom messengers have passed, asking if he died or was killed would they turn upon their heels, declaring whoever worshipped Allah then Allah is Living and never dies, and whoever worshipped Muhammad then Muhammad had died. Umar exclaimed that it was in the Book of Allah though he had not realized it, then said this is Abu Bakr, the elder of the Muslims, so pledge him, and they pledged him. In the added word Abu Bakr entered asking how she saw him, she said swooned, he drew near uncovering his face lamenting the swoon, then uncovering discerned death, declared they belong to Allah and to Him they return, then wept, and she said for the sake of Allah at the cessation of revelation and Jibril entering her house, and he placed his hand upon his temples and his mouth upon his forehead weeping until his tears flowed upon the face of the Prophet ﷺ, then covered his face and went out weeping asking the Muslims whether any had a covenant of the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they said none, then asking Umar likewise who said none, whereupon he swore he had tasted death though it was said to them he is dead and you are dead. The people clamoured and wept intensely, then left him to his household, and Ali ibn Abi Talib washed him with Usamah ibn Zayd pouring water over him, Ali saying no part he forgot to wash but it turned for him until he saw naught, washing without seeing until finished. They shrouded him in a red Yemeni cloak and two wraps already worn, then laid him upon the bier and admitted the people who entered upon him company after company praying over him without an imam, until none remained in Madinah, free or slave, but prayed over him. They then disputed where to bury him, some saying by the staff he held and beneath his pulpit, others in al-Baqi where he buried his dead, but they refused lest a slave or maidservant angered by his master cling to his grave as precedent, and their view settled to bury him in his house beneath his bed where his soul was taken. When Abu Bakr died he was buried with him, and when death approached Umar ibn al-Khattab he enjoined that when he died they carry him to the door of the house of Aishah and ask whether he should enter or go out. She fell silent awhile then said admit him and bury him, with Abu Bakr to his right and Umar to his left. When Umar was buried she took the outer wrap and veiled herself, and asked about the wrap she said this was her husband and this her father, but when Umar was buried she veiled. > **""O Aishah, what ails you? I too complain of my head. I have fallen ill, and I cannot go round among you, so permit me to stay with Aishah. Woe at his swoon, how severe is the swoon of the Messenger of Allah. You have lied, rather you are a man agitated by tribulation. The Messenger of Allah shall not die until Allah annihilates the hypocrites. To Allah we belong and to Him we return. The Messenger of Allah has died. O my son. O my chosen one. O my intimate friend. Allah Almighty says: you are dead and they are dead. Muhammad is only a messenger before whom messengers have passed. If he dies or is killed, will you turn upon your heels? Whoever worshipped Allah, then Allah is Living and never dies, and whoever worshipped Muhammad, then Muhammad has died. O you people, this is Abu Bakr, and he is the elder of the Muslims, so pledge him. How do you see him? O his swoon, what can this swoon be? To Allah we belong and to Him we return. For the sake of Allah, at the cessation of revelation and Jibril entering my house. O company of Muslims, has any of you a covenant of the passing of the Messenger of Allah? O Umar, have you a covenant of the passing of the Messenger of Allah? By Him save Whom none is worthy of worship, he has tasted death. This is Umar ibn al-Khattab greeting you with peace, saying: shall I enter or go out? Admit him and bury him.""**"
"14255","Asma bint Umays رضي الله عنها reports that the first complaint of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began in the house of Maymunah رضي الله عنها, his illness intensified until he swooned, his wives consulted about administering medicine by the mouth and they did so, and when he recovered he asked what this was. They said it was the doing of women who had come from thence, gesturing to the land of Abyssinia, among whom was Asma bint Umays. They said they had feared pleurisy for him, whereupon he said that was a malady Allah Almighty would never afflict him with, and none should remain in the house but be given mouth medicine except the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, meaning al-Abbas رضي الله عنه. She said Maymunah took mouth medicine that day though fasting, by the resolve of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""What is this? That is a malady Allah Almighty would never afflict me with. Let none remain in the house but be given mouth medicine, except the uncle of the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"14256","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ called near his death for a sheet to write therein a writing after which they would never go astray, but Umar ibn al-Khattab differed over it until it was set aside."
"14257","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ fell ill he said to fetch a sheet and inkpot that he might write for them a writing after which they would never go astray, but they disliked it intensely. He again said to fetch a sheet that he might write for them a writing after which they would never stray, whereupon the women from behind the curtain said did they not hear what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. He said they were as the companions of Yusuf, squeezing their eyes when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell ill and mounting his neck when he recovered. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said to leave them, for they were better than them. > **""Fetch me a sheet and inkpot, that I may write for you a writing after which you shall never go astray. Fetch me a sheet, that I may write for you a writing after which you shall never stray. Leave them, for they are better than you.""**"
"14258","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reports that to swear nine times that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was slain is dearer to him than to swear once that he was not slain, for Allah Almighty made him a prophet and took him as a martyr. Al-Amash said he mentioned it to Ibrahim who said they held that the Jews had poisoned him and Abu Bakr."
"14259","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died only of pleurisy."
"14260","Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith رضي الله عنها, the mother of the child of al-Abbas and sister of Maymunah, reports that she came to the Prophet ﷺ in his illness weeping, and he raised his head asking what made her weep. She said they feared for him and knew not what they would meet from people after him, whereupon he said they would be deemed weak after him. > **""What makes you weep? You shall be deemed weak after me.""**"
"14261","Ali ibn al-Husayn reports that he heard his father say that before the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Jibril came to him saying Allah Almighty had sent him honouring him, preferring him and singling him out, asking what He knew better than him, namely how he found himself, and the Prophet ﷺ said he found himself grieved and distressed. On the third day Jibril descended with the Angel of Death and with them an angel in the air called Ismail over seventy thousand angels, each over seventy thousand angels, escorted by Jibril, repeating that Allah Almighty had sent him honouring him, preferring him and singling him out to ask how he found himself, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he found himself grieved and distressed. The Angel of Death sought permission at the door, and Jibril said this was the Angel of Death seeking permission of him, never having sought permission of any man before him nor ever after him. He said to admit him, so Jibril admitted him and he came until he stood before him, saying Allah Almighty had sent him and commanded him to obey him in whatever he ordered, whether to seize his soul if commanded or to leave it if disliked. He asked whether he would indeed do so, and he affirmed that thus he was commanded to obey him. Jibril then said Allah Almighty longed to meet him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to proceed as commanded. Jibril said this was his last treading upon earth, for he had been his need in this world. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed and consolation came, there came one whose sound they heard without seeing his person, greeting them and reciting every soul shall taste death, declaring in Allah consolation from every calamity, succession from every perishing, and attainment from every loss, bidding trust in Allah and hope in Him, for the stricken is he deprived of reward. > **""O Muhammad, Allah Almighty has sent me to you honouring you, preferring you and singling you out. I ask you of what He knows better than you. He says: how do you find yourself? I find myself, O Jibril, grieved, and I find myself, O Jibril, distressed. O Muhammad, this is the Angel of Death seeking permission of you, and he has never sought permission of any man before you, nor shall he seek permission of any man after you. Admit him. O Muhammad, Allah Almighty has sent me to you, and commanded me to obey you in whatever you command me: if you command me to seize your soul I shall seize it, and if you dislike I shall leave it. Yes. Thus I was commanded to obey you in whatever you command me. Allah Almighty has longed to meet you. Proceed to what you were commanded. This is my last treading upon earth. You were my need in this world. Peace be upon you and mercy of Allah and His blessings. Every soul shall taste death. In Allah is consolation from every calamity, succession from every perishing, and attainment from every loss. In Allah so trust, and in Him so hope, for the stricken is he deprived of reward. Peace be upon you and mercy of Allah.""**"
"14262","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died from the meat which the Jewess had poisoned, his aorta severed from the poison after a year, and he kept saying he still felt its sensation. > **""I still feel its sensation.""**"
"14263","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that no night passed over her like the night in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whether dawn had risen, and she answered no, until Bilal called for Fajr, then Bilal came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked who this was, she said this is Bilal, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to command Abu Bakr to pray with the people. > **""O Aishah, has dawn risen? What is this? Command Abu Bakr to pray with the people.""**"
"14264","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said at his passing, extending his hand and asking where Jibril was, then clasping and spreading it repeatedly, asking Jibril to intercede for him with his Lord to ease death upon him. Abu Hurayrah mentioned that he heard Aishah say she heard what no ear had heard from Jibril, saying at your service. > **""O Jibril, where are you? O Jibril, intercede for me with my Lord to ease death upon me. At your service, at your service.""**"
"14265","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Angel of Death came to the Prophet ﷺ in the illness in which he was taken, seeking permission while his head lay in the lap of Ali, and greeted with peace and mercy and blessings of Allah. Ali told him to return for they were occupied from him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he knew who this was, declaring him the Angel of Death and bidding him enter rightly. When he entered he said his Lord greeted him with peace. He asked where Jibril was, and he said he was not near him but would come now. The Angel of Death went out until Jibril descended, and Jibril standing at the door asked what had brought him out, and he said Muhammad ﷺ sought him. When they sat, Jibril greeted him as father of al-Qasim, declaring this a farewell between them. It reached him that the Angel of Death had never greeted any household before him nor would greet after him. > **""Peace be upon you and mercy of Allah and His blessings. Do you know who this is, O father of al-Hasan? This is the Angel of Death. Enter rightly. Where is Jibril? What brought you out, O Angel of Death? Peace be upon you, O father of al-Qasim. This is a farewell between me and you.""**"
"14266","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ grew heavy with Aishah and Hafsah رضي الله عنهما beside him when Ali رضي الله عنه entered, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw him he raised his head saying draw near him twice. He leaned him against himself and remained with him until he passed, and when it was decreed Ali rose and closed the door. Al-Abbas came with the sons of Abd al-Muttalib and stood at the door, and Ali kept saying his father be his ransom, pure in life and pure in death, with a fragrant scent rising the like of which they never found, then said away with wailing like that of a woman and attend to their companion. Ali said to admit al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas to him. The Ansar adjured them by Allah and by their share of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so they admitted one of them called Aws ibn Khawli carrying a jar in one hand. They heard a voice in the house not to strip the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but to wash him as he was in his shirt, so Ali washed him inserting his hand beneath the shirt with al-Fadl holding the garment from him and the Ansari conveying water, with a cloth upon the hand of Ali inserting his hand beneath the shirt. > **""Draw near me, draw near me. With my father you are ransomed, pure in life and pure in death. Away with wailing like that of a woman, and attend to your companion. Admit al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas to me. Do not strip the Messenger of Allah, and wash him as he is in his shirt.""**"
"14267","Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ enjoined him that none should wash him but he, for none sees his nakedness but his eyes are obliterated. Ali said al-Abbas and Usamah رضي الله عنهما handed him water from behind the curtain. > **""None sees my nakedness but his eyes are obliterated.""**"
"14268","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say that no prophet has his soul taken but he sees the reward, then it is returned to him and he is given choice between its return to him until he joins. She had memorized that from him, and while she supported him against her chest she looked at him until his neck inclined and she said he had been decreed. She said she then recognized what he had said. She looked at him until he rose and gazed, and she said then he would not choose them, and he said with the Highest Companion in Paradise, with the prophets and the truthful, to the end of the verse. > **""No prophet has his soul taken but he sees the reward, then it is returned to him and he is given choice between its return to him until he joins. With the Highest Companion in Paradise, with the prophets and the truthful.""**"
"14269","It is reported from Aishah رضي الله عنها that in another narration the words were the Most High and Most Blessed Companion. She said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken while he lay between my collarbone and my chest. She said she had thought Allah would return his soul to him, and she said that is what He does with the Prophets. She said he then stirred, and she said to herself that if he were given a choice on that day, he would not choose us. > **""The Most High, Most Blessed Companion.""**"
"14270","It is reported from Aishah رضي الله عنها that she said the Prophet ﷺ died, and when his soul came forth she never smelled any fragrance at all more pleasant than it."
"14271","It is reported from Aishah رضي الله عنها that she said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted a curtain and opened a door during his illness, and he looked at the people praying behind Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, and he was pleased with that. He then praised Allah and turned to the people and addressed them, informing them that no Prophet died until a man from his Ummah had led him in prayer, and commanding that whoever is struck by a calamity after him should take comfort in his calamity through him, for no one of his Ummah after him will ever be struck by a calamity like his calamity through the loss of him. > **""Praise be to Allah. No Prophet ever died until a man from his Ummah had led him in prayer. O people, whoever among you is struck by a calamity after me, let him take comfort in his calamity through me against the calamity that strikes him, for no one of my Ummah after me will ever be struck by one like my calamity through me.""**"
"14272","It is reported from Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه that he said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell unconscious while he was in the lap of Aishah رضي الله عنها. He then regained consciousness while she was wiping his chest and supplicating for his healing. He refused that supplication for recovery and said instead that he asked Allah for the Most High and Most Blessed Companion, naming Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl and Isrāfīl. > **""No. Rather I ask Allah for the Most High, Most Blessed Companion: Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl and Isrāfīl.""**"
"14273","It is reported from Abū ʿUsayb, or Abū ʿUsaym رضي الله عنه through Bahz, that he was present at the prayer over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The people asked how they should pray over him, and he said they should enter in successive groups. They would enter through one door and offer supplication over him, then leave through the other door. When he was placed in his lahd, al-Mughirah رضي الله عنه said that something at his feet remained unarranged. They told him to enter and set it right, so he entered and put his hand in and touched his feet ﷺ, then said to heap soil over him. They heaped soil over him until it reached halfway up his shins, then he came out and would say that he was the one among them with the most recent contact with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Enter in successive groups. Heap dust over him.""**"
"14274","It is reported from Aishah رضي الله عنها that she said she used to enter her house in which lay the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and her father, and she would lay aside her outer garment, saying that he was only her husband and he was only her father. But when ʿUmar رضي الله عنه was buried with them, she swore by Allah that she never entered it thereafter except with her garments drawn tightly about her, out of modesty before ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"14275","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that he said al-ʿAbbās and ʿAlī and al-Faḍl رضي الله عنهم entered the grave of the Prophet ﷺ, and a man from the Ansar cut his lahd, and he was the same one who had cut the graves of the martyrs on the day of Uḥud. The compiler adds that Ibn Majah narrated it at greater length than this, without mention of al-ʿAbbās and without mention of the one who cut his lahd ﷺ."
"14276","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that he said when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه was in a district of Madīnah. He came in to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and placed his mouth upon the forehead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and began to kiss him, saying may my father and mother be ransom for him, he was pure in life and in death. When he came out he passed by ʿUmar, may Allah have mercy on him, who was saying by Allah the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not died and would not die until he had slain the hypocrites, for they had rejoiced at the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and raised their heads. Abū Bakr passed by him and said restrain thyself, O man, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died, and reminded him of the words of Allah Most High that thou art mortal and they are mortal, and that no immortality was granted to any mortal before him, so if he died would they remain immortal. He then went to the minbar and ascended it and praised Allah and extolled Him, then addressed the people, telling them that if Muhammad had been their god whom they worshipped then their god had died, but if their God was the One in the heaven then their God was alive and never died, and he recited that Muhammad is but a Messenger before whom Messengers have passed away. Then he descended, and the believers took gladness from that and their joy intensified, while grief seized the hypocrites. ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it was as though coverings lay upon our faces and were then lifted away. > **""May my father and mother be ransom for thee; thou wast pure in life and in death. O man, restrain thyself, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died. O people, if Muhammad was your god whom you worship, then your god has died, and if your God is the One in the heaven, then your God is alive and never dies.""**"
"14277","It is reported from Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that after him people would console one another with the consolation of a Prophet. The people would ask what that meant, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away they met one another consoling one another through the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""After me people will console one another with the consolation of a Prophet.""**"
"14278","It is reported from Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه that he said no sooner had they buried the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the dust than they found their hearts estranged."
"14279","It is reported from Aishah رضي الله عنها that she said she saw as though three moons had fallen into her lap. Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه said that if her dream proved true, the three best of the people of the earth would be buried in her house. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, Abū Bakr said to her, O Aishah, this is the best of thy moons. Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما were later buried in her house. The compiler adds that this has already come in raised form, that she related it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he interpreted it thus in the chapter on the interpretation of dreams. > **""If thy dream has proved true, the three best of the people of the earth will be buried in thy house. O Aishah, this is the best of thy moons.""**"
"14280","It is reported from ʿUrwah that Safiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنها eulogized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying in verse: O the grief of my soul, I spent the night as one robbed, keeping vigil through the night like one despoiled by war, from cares and sorrow that kept me awake, would that I had been given to drink of it through death, when they said the Messenger ﷺ had entered the evening and the decreed death had come to him; when we came to the household of Muhammad ﷺ grey hair covered my temples, when we saw his houses desolate with no familiar life in them after him, so a long sorrow befell me and mingled with my heart until it was as though stricken with terror. She also said in verse: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, thou wast our ease and thou wast kind to us and wast never harsh; our Prophet ﷺ was kind to us and merciful, so let whoever weeps weep for him today; by my life, I do not weep for the Prophet ﷺ only because of his death, but because of the turmoil that would come after him; it is as though upon my heart, for the loss of Muhammad ﷺ and love of him, there were brandings thereafter; O Fatimah رضي الله عنها, may the Lord of Muhammad ﷺ send blessings upon the grave that has settled in Yathrib as a dweller; I see Hasan رضي الله عنه left orphaned by him, weeping and calling upon his grandfather from afar today; may my mother and my maternal aunt and my paternal uncle and myself and my household be ransom for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he was patient and conveyed the message truthfully and died with the religion firm, radiant, and pure; had the Lord of the Throne kept him among us we would have been felicitous, but His decree was carried out; upon him be peace from Allah as a greeting, and may he be admitted into the Gardens of Eden, well pleased."
"14281","It is reported from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Safiyyah رضي الله عنها came out trailing her cloak and saying in verse that after him there had come reports and turmoil such that had he witnessed them, discourse would not have multiplied. > **""After thee have come reports and turmoil; hadst thou witnessed them, discourse would not have multiplied.""**"
"14282","It is reported from Ghunaym ibn Qays رضي الله عنه that he said he remembered what his father had said concerning the Prophet ﷺ on the day the Prophet ﷺ died, in verse lamenting woe upon himself over Muhammad ﷺ, that during his life he had stood on guard, sleeping his night in safety until the morning. > **""Woe is me over Muhammad. During his life I stood on guard, sleeping my night in safety until the morning.""**"
"14283","It is reported from Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه that he said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to them that soon one of them would dearly love to behold him with a single glance in exchange for all he possessed of family and wealth. > **""Soon one of you will dearly love to behold me with a glance in exchange for all he has of family and wealth.""**"
"14284","It is reported from ʿUmar رضي الله عنه that he said when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, he and Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه went to ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, and asked what he said concerning what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left. He said they were the people with the most right to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. ʿUmar asked him about that which was in Khaybar, and he affirmed it, and asked him about that which was in Fadak, and he affirmed it. ʿUmar then swore by Allah that even if they cut their necks with saws, that would not be. > **""We are the people with the most right to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, even if you cut our necks with saws, it shall not be.""**"
"14285","It is reported from Juwayriyah رضي الله عنها that she said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left nothing on the day he died except a white mule and his weapons and a piece of land which he had made charity."
"14286","It is reported from Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said they are not inherited from, and whatever they leave is charity. > **""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.""**"
"14287","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said they are not inherited from, and whatever they leave is charity. > **""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.""**"
